## 1. INTRODUCTION
Myriad technological gains of the 20th century were enabled by the discovery and optimization of permanent magnets materials that can retain their magnetization in the absence of an applied magnetic field $(H)$ below a certain critical ordering temperature $(\breve{T}_{\mathfrak{c}})$ .1 For instance, nearly all portable electronic devices, household appliances, and information-recording media rely on permanent magnet technology. In addition, permanent magnets are critical to the function of the motors that power electric cars and the generators that convert renewable energy resources, such as wind and wave power, into electrical energy .2−9 Accordingly, there is an increasing demand for generating new permanent magnets with various properties to meet the requirements of our fast-growing society.
Conventional magnets comprise inorganic solids, typically rare earth- and transition metal-based intermetallic compounds or oxides,9 in which direct metal−metal bonding or short mono- or diatomic ligands allow for efficient long-range communication between spin centers.1,4,10,11 While these materials have met tremendous success and exhibit high magnetic ordering temperatures and maximum energy density values $\big[\big(B H\big)_{\mathrm{max}}\big],^{\circ}$ their all-inorganic compositions lead to dense materials with limited chemical programmability and tunability and a lack of processability. As such, the rational design of inorganic solid-state magnets with targeted structures and properties is exceedingly challenging.
In contrast, molecule-based magnets possess high synthetic programmability and tunability. In particular, this moleculebased approach has afforded single molecules, one-dimensional (1D) chains, two-dimensional (2D) layered materials, and three-dimensional (3D) networks possessing unique magnetic behavior.1,12−16 Moreover, some of these compounds have demonstrated fascinating multifunctional properties,17,18 including coexistent long-range magnetic order and electrical conductivity.19−22 In addition, the low density of moleculebased magnets may enable their application as lightweight permanent magnets for electrical devices and energy technologies. Despite the many potential advantages of molecule-based magnets over traditional solid-state inorganic magnets, a vast majority of molecule-based magnets suffer from low operating temperatures, and thus, an active focus of research in this area involves increasing the strength of magnetic exchange interactions between spin centers to realize the implementation of these and similar magnet materials in practical applications.
With respect to developing molecule-based magnets that function at high temperatures, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) represent an ideal, yet underexplored, chemical platform, owing to their unparalleled synthetic programmability and tunability, in conjunction with their extended and porous structures. Because long-range magnetic order necessitates strong interactions along two or more dimensions,1,4,10,12,13,23 the organized arrangement of magnetic entities within MOFs can enable cooperative exchange interactions between spin centers through the organic linkers, thus engendering long-range magnetic order. Indeed, by leveraging the gains that coordination chemistry has provided to the fields of MOF synthesis and molecular magnetism over the past decades, in particular with regard to constructing metal coordination environments with high magnetic anisotropy, researchers can generate permanent magnets with specifically tailored structures and properties for targeted applications.
In addition, MOFs offer an opportunity for the simultaneous implementation of strong magnetic coupling and porosity, two properties that are generally inimical to one another. Specifically, while magnetic exchange interactions require short distances between spin centers, porosity is generally favored by employing extended organic linkers. Nevertheless, owing to the extensive library of organic ligands capable of adopting versatile coordination modes, several design strategies have been demonstrated to successfully afford porous metal− organic magnets.18 These strategies include the use of short linkers, typically with one-, two-, or three-atom24 bridges, preformed metalloligands,25 and radical organic linkers.26,27 In addition to promoting potential applications such as magnetic gas separations28−30 and sensing, 29,31−33 structural porosity offers a possible route for accessing unusual materials through postsynthetic chemical modification. For instance, postsynthetic redox chemistry can be employed to tune the electrical and optical properties,34−37 and metal38−43 and linker exchange40−42,44,45 c an provide kinetically metastable materials that cannot be accessed through direct synthesis.
## 1.1. Classes of Magnetic Order
In contrast to paramagnets, permanent magnets feature unpaired electrons with strong long-range interactions such that spontaneous order of the magnetic dipoles occurs at a sufficiently low temperature $(T_{c})$ where the spin−spin interactions outcompete thermal fluctuations. The relative alignment and magnitude of neighboring spin centers determine whether ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic, or ferrimagnetic order is formed (see Figure 1).1−4,10,12 To illustrate, parallel alignment of nearest-neighbor spins will lead to a ferromagnetic state, regardless of whether the spin centers are of the same magnitude or different magnitudes. In this state, the spins are ferromagnetically coupled to one another (see Figure 1, top left). When neighboring spins adopt an antiparallel configuration, where the spins are antiferromagnetically coupled to one another, two possible ordered states may arise. If all spins are of the same magnitude, antiferromagnetic order is observed (see Figure 1, top right). In contrast, an uncompensated magnetic moment is obtained when the magnetic centers possess spins of unequal magnitudes, giving rise to ferrimagnetic order (see Figure 1, bottom left). Accordingly, ferrimagnetism can be considered as a manifestation of antiferromagnetism, even though the magnetic behavior of ferrimagnets is closer to that of ferromagnets due to the presence of a permanent magnetic moment.
In addition to these three classes of magnetic order, tilting of the spins by a small angle about their axes leads to canted spin structures that show canted antiferromagnetic order (see Figure 1, bottom right). The presence of canted magnetic order is commonly encountered when the magnetic centers are crystallographically inequivalent and/or display large magnetic anisotropy. Note that this ordered state displays weak ferromagnetic-like behavior, as it possesses a nonzero magnetic moment.
The magnetic ordered states summarized in Figure 1 are present in materials only below a specific critical ordering temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ , which is directly correlated to the strength of magnetic interactions between spin centers and the number of nearest-neighbor spins.1,4,13,46 The magnetic ordering temperature is known as the Curie temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{C}}\right)$ for ferromagnets and the Néel temperature $(\Bar{T}_{\mathrm{N}})$ for antiferromagnets and ferrimagnets. Above the ordering temperature, a paramagnetic state is found for which thermal energy is large enough to overcome the cooperative ordering of the magnetic moments.1−4,10,12,13,46 This paramagnetic behavior obeys the Curie−Weiss expression $\bar{\chi_{\mathrm{M}}}=\bar{C7}(T-\theta).$ , where $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ is the magnetic susceptibility, $C$ is the Curie constant, $T$ is the absolute temperature, and $\theta$ is the Weiss constant, which reflects short-range interactions between spins. The Weiss constant is positive when the spins are aligned parallel and negative when the spins are aligned antiparallel.1−4,10,12,13 Overall, the magnetic properties of materials are determined by the relative orientations and magnitudes of their spin carriers as well as the strength of the interactions between them.
## 1.2. Experimental Characterization of Magnetic Order
The occurrence of long-range magnetic order can be established by several experimental means, such as direct current (dc) and alternating current (ac) magnetic susceptibility $(\chi_{\mathrm{M}})$ , magnetic hysteresis, neutron diffraction, muon spin rotation, and heat capacity measurements.1−4,10,46 For instance, a magnetic phase transition is typically associated with a dramatic increase or decrease in dc magnetic susceptibility for ferromagnets/ferrimagnets or antiferromagnets, respectively (see Figure 2). The observation of a minimum followed by a sharp upturn in a plot of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ versus $T$ is indicative of ferrimagnetic or canted antiferromagnetic order, as antiferromagnetic interactions dominate at high temperatures, but spontaneous magnetization occurs below TN.
Common methods for determining the magnetic ordering temperature of a material from magnetic measurements13,47 include (1) the occurrence of a remanent magnetization $\left(M_{\mathrm{r}}\right)$ , (2) the bifurcation point of $M$ versus $T$ or $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ versus $T$ curves for data cooled in the absence and presence of a dc magnetic field, (3) the maximum of in-phase $({\chi_{\mathrm{M}}}^{\prime})$ ac magnetic susceptibility, and (4) the onset of a peak in out-of-phase $\left(\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{\prime\prime}\right)^{-}$ ac magnetic susceptibility (see Figure 3). Note that an antiferromagnetic phase transition is not associated with energy dissipation, and thus, no ${\chi_{\mathrm{M}}}^{\prime\prime}$ signal is observed. In addition, magnets may display metamagnetic behavior, for which a critical external magnetic field induces a transition from an antiferromagnetic ordered ground state to a ferromagnetic-like state. This behavior, known as metamagnetism, is characterized by a sigmoidal $M$ versus $H$ curve.13 Furthermore, some magnets show spin glass behavior,48 which is often evident from broad and frequency-dependent peaks in ${\chi_{\mathrm{M}}}^{\prime}$ and/or $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{\prime\prime}$ . Such glassiness can result from factors such as crystallographic disorder and topological spin frustration. Accordingly, it can be challenging to determine magnetic ordering temperatures from magnetic measurements alone, as the properties of ordered magnets can be highly complicated and dependent on the experimental conditions.
Several parameters in addition to the magnetic ordering temperature can be employed to characterize and compare the performance of permanent magnets. Specifically, permanent magnetsnamely, ferromagnets, ferrimagnets, and canted antiferromagnetsdisplay hysteretic behavior, where a field of opposite direction is required to demagnetize the material after it has become magnetized.1−4,10,13 The maximum width of the hysteresis loop is equal to twice the coercive field $\left(H_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ , also called coercivity, of the material (see Figure 4) and is defined as the field necessary to demagnetize the fully magnetized material. As such, the coercive field is a direct measure of the ability of magnets to withstand an external magnetic field without becoming demagnetized. Importantly, $H_{\mathrm{c}}$ is a kinetic parameter that depends on the time scale over which a magnetization curve is measured, i.e., the sweep rate of the magnetic field. The magnetization remaining upon removal of the applied magntic field during the hysteresis measurement is known as the remanent magnetization $\left(M_{\mathrm{r}}\right)$ and can be significantly different from the saturation magnetization $\left(M_{s}\right)$ . In addition, the maximum energy density $\left[\left(\overset{\smile}{B}H\right)_{\mathrm{max}}\right]^{9}$ may be extracted from a magnetic hysteresis loop, as illustrated in Figure 4. The parameters $H_{c},M_{\mathrm{r}},M_{s},$ and $\bar{(}B H)_{\mathrm{max}}$ are all key variables that determine the practicality of magnets for specific applications. For example, hard magnets, which have $H_{\mathrm{c}}>100$ Oe and significant $M_{\mathrm{r}},$ are required for data storage devices, whereas soft magnets, which have $H_{\mathrm{c}}<10$ Oe and $M_{\mathrm{r}}\approx0$ Oe, are better suited for magnetic shielding and electric motors.13
## 1.3. Scope and Structure of the Review
Within this review, we restrict the term “MOF magnet” to compounds that fall under the definition of a MOF and show magnetic order at a measurable temperature. Historically, multiple definitions of a MOF have been proposed on the basis of different structural and functional characteristics.49−51 Here, we adopt the IUPAC-approved definition, which states that a “MOF is a coordination network with organic ligands containing potential voids”.52 Although, according to this definition, cross-linked 1D chains can be considered MOFs, we limit our discussion to materials that are covalently linked to extend in at least two dimensions. While the presence of a metal is inherent to the term MOF, the nature of the organic components has led to some debate.50,51 Molecules are typically considered organic if they feature $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ and/or $C-$ H bonds. Furthermore, it is generally accepted that the organic ligands in MOFs should be multitopic, with two or more sdtirsotingctcfouvnaclteinotnablognrdosupwsitahvamilaetballe ifonrsmoer acl ubsitnedrisn.4g9,−a5n4dUfsoirnmg these criteria, compounds based on cyanide, cyanamide, and formate bridges do not fall within the definition of a MOF and thus will not be covered in depth in this review. However, those bridged by oxalate $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-})$ and tetracyanoethylene $\mathrm{[C_{2}(C N)_{4}]}$ will be discussed, as they contain a $\mathrm{C-C/C=C}$ bond and multiple metal-binding groups. Because the precise establishment of structure−property relationships is critical to understanding the magnetic behavior of materials, we limit our discussion to structurally characterized compounds. The structures should be derived from single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) or from refinement of powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data. Furthermore, as the porosity of many frameworks is not well established, we include numerous compounds whose properties we view as being instructive to the reader within the context of this review, even though the presence of voids is not explicitly reported.
In this review, we survey the current state of metal−organic framework magnets. In section 2, we briefly examine compounds that do not fall within the strict definition of a MOF but have nonetheless led to important breakthroughs in the field of molecule-based magnetism and have contributed significantly to the development of MOF magnets. In section 3, we provide an overview of 2D and 3D MOF magnets based on diamagnetic linkers, including phosphonates, oxalates, oxamates, carboxylates, and N-heterocycles, along with those featuring linkers bearing mixed N and O donors, and benzoquinoid-based ligands. In section 4, we focus on surveying current examples of framework magnets based on organic radical linkers, including nitroxides, organonitriles, and semiquinoid derivatives. Note that this last class of compounds has provided the MOF magnets with the highest reported ordering temperature of $171\ \mathrm{K}.^{72-74}$ Within all sections, we adopt the notation of the form $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{m}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}^{n}$ to describe the inorganic and organic dimensionality of the structures, where $m$ and $n$ represent the dimensionality of the inorganic and organic connectivity, respectively, as introduced by Cheetham, Rao, and co-workers.55,75 Finally, section 5 provides an outlook for the field and discusses some potential strategies for increasing the ordering temperatures of MOF magnets above room temperature while retaining structural integrity and other functionality.
## 2. TOWARD STRUCTURALLY CHARACTERIZED MOF MAGNETS
A number of structurally characterized 1D chain compounds have been reported to exhibit long-range magnetic order.11,92−122 This class includes metalloporphyrin-based magnets comprising linear chains of $[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{\dot{TPP})}]^{+}$ $(\mathrm{TPP}^{2-}$ = meso-tetraphenylporphyrinato) or [MnIII(TXPP)]+ [TXPP2− $\c=$ meso-tetrakis(4-halophenyl)porphyrinato; $\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{F}$ , Cl, Br, or I] cations and $\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet}$ or $\mathrm{{QCl}_{4}^{-\bullet}}$ $\left(\mathrm{QCl}_{4}\ =\ \right.$ tetrachloro-1,4- benzoquinone) radical anions.92−96 These materials behave as ferrimagnets or canted antiferromagnets with ordering temperatures in the range of $3.5\mathrm{-}28\ \mathrm{K},$ owing to antiferromagnetic coupling between $S\ =\ 2\ \mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers and $S\ =\ ^{1}{}^{\prime}{}_{2}$ radical anions. However, complicated spin glass or metamagnetic behaviors are frequently observed at low temperatures. Remarkably, several members of this family of compounds display coercive fields above $2.0\mathrm{T}$ at $2.0\mathrm{K},$ and the $H_{\mathrm{c}}$ value of $2.7\mathrm{~T~}$ for $\mathrm{\Delta[Mn^{III}(T B r P P)]^{+}(T C N E)^{-\bullet}}$ is one of the largest coercive fields reported for a molecule-based material.96
Chain compounds of transition meta $97-104\$ and lanthanide ions105−111 bridged by nitronyl nitroxide and imino nitroxide radicals have also been shown to display bulk magnetic order. These chains exhibit strong antiferromagnetic metal−radical coupling, and the long-range order is driven by dipolar interactions between chains. Notably, chains featuring the highly anisotropic ions $\mathrm{Co^{II},\ D y^{I I I},}$ , and $\mathrm{{Tb}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}$ can behave as single-chain magnets, thus providing examples of compounds with coexistent slow magnetic relaxation and long-range magnetic order.104,108−111 The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -based chain compound $\mathrm{Co}^{\smash{\check{\Pi}}}(\mathrm{hfac})_{2}(\mathrm{BPNN})$ $(\mathrm{hfac^{-}}=1,1,1,5,5,\xi$ -hexafluoroacetylacetonate; $\mathbf{BPNN}={\mathfrak{p}}$ -butoxyphenyl nitronyl nitroxide radical) is especially interesting, as it exhibits the highest magnetic ordering temperature of ${\sim}45\mathrm{~K~}$ for this class of compounds and displays a giant coercive field of Hc = 5.2 T at 6 K.104 Indeed, this value of $H_{\mathrm{c}}$ exceeds the room-temperature values of commercial permanent magnets, such as $\mathsf{S m C o}_{5}$ $\mathit{\tilde{(H_{c}=4.4}}$ T) and Nd2Fe14B (Hc = 1.9 T),104,123 and renders $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{hfac})_{2}(\mathrm{BPNN})$ the record holder of the largest coercive field known among permanent magnets.
In addition to these radical-bridged chains, diamagnetic oxamato,112−115 oxalato,116−120 and carboxylato121,122 ligands have provided 1D chain compounds showing long-range magnetic order. Nevertheless, a majority of these compounds exhibit ordering temperatures of $<10~\mathrm{K},$ likely stemming from weak interchain interactions. Indeed, only two structurally characterized ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ chain compounds order at a higher temperature. Specifically, the zigzag chains ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathbf{ox})\breve{(1,3\mathbf{-}}$ pdiol)120 ( $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{\sigma}_{0\mathbf{X}}^{\prime}^{2-}=\mathbf{\sigma}}\end{array}$ oxalate; $1,3{\cdot}\mathrm{pdiol}\ =\ 1,3$ -propanediol) and $\mathbf{\hat{K}}_{2}\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}\bigl(\mathbf{ox}\bigr)_{2}^{\mathrm{~119~}}$ exhibit canted antiferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=10.6$ and $37\mathrm{~K},$ respectively. Note, however, that dehydration of bimetallic $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{C\hat{u}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ and $\mathrm{\dot{C}o^{I I}C u^{I I}}$ oxamate-based chains affords compounds with ordering temperatures of $30{-}38\ \mathrm{K},$ but no structural data are available for those compounds.115,124
In addition to 1D chains, 2D and 3D frameworks can also behave as single-chain magnets without magnetic order.125 Compounds that show such behavior typically feature strong magnetic coupling through short bridging ligands along one direction and much weaker nearest-neighbor interactions along the other directions due to longer magnetic exchange pathways. One example of a 3D framework displaying this phenomenon is the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{0.67}\mathrm{b}\mathring{\mathrm{dt}}\big)_{3}{\cdot}2\mathsf{\bar{0}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\bar{\big[}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{dt}~=~5,5^{\prime}.$ -(1,4-phenylene)bis(1H-tetrazole)], which is comprised of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -tetrazolate chains linked through the phenyl tethers of the $\mathrm{bdt}^{2-}$ ligands in the other two directions.126 ac susceptibility and magnetic hysteresis measurements confirmed single-chain magnet behavior with a relaxation barrier $\Delta_{\tau}$ of $30.{\overset{-}{2}}\operatorname{cm}^{-1}$ and a pre-exponential factor $\tau_{0}$ of $5.1\times10^{-9}\:\mathrm{s},$ , along with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=450$ Oe at $1.8~\mathrm{K}$ Other examples of compounds within this class of framework materials include 3D ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{{\bar{II}}}}$ -based MOFs featuring 1D ferromagnetic chains of $\mathrm{[Co_{3}^{I I}(O H)_{2}]^{4+}}$ units with $\Delta_{\tau}=11.0~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\stackrel{\triangledown}{\left(\tau_{0}=2.03\times10^{-9}\right.}$ s)12 7 or mixed carboxylato- and hydroxo-bridged $\Delta$ -chains with $\Delta_{\tau}=74~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\tau_{0}=2.80\times10^{-10}s)$ .128 Furthermore, a 2D framework comprised of carboxylato-bridged chains of ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ paddlewheel units exhibits energy barriers of $\Delta_{\tau1}=56.2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\bar{\left(}\tau_{0}=5.19\times10^{-11}s\right)$ and $\Delta_{\tau2}=34.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\phantom{0}{\langle}\tau_{0}=5.59\times10^{-8}s)$ for two different temperatur