As noted above, frameworks bridged by small inorganic molecules or atoms, such as cyanide, cyanamide, tricyanomethanide, thiocyanide, azide, and halides, will not be covered in this review unless these ligands can be considered as coligands within otherwise organic ligand-bridged networks. Extensive research on Prussian blue analogues131,132 decades has afforded a number of magnets with high ordering temperatures, reaching 376 K for KVII[CrIII(CN)6]·2H2O.133 Other cyano-bridged solids, such as those based on octacyanometalates134 and inorganic−organic networks,135,136 along with those bridged by the polydentate cyanocarbons cyanamide137−141 and tricyanomethanide, $\begin{array}{r c l}{{142-14\dot{4}}}\\ {{\cdot}}\end{array}$ have also been found to display long-range magnetic order. Some of these frameworks exhibit permanent microporosity, as has been recently reviewed.145 Furthermore, 2D and 3D inorganic−organic compounds featuring thiocyanatobridged,146−149 azido-bridged,146,150−161 and chlorobridged162,163 chains or layers commonly show permanent magnet-like behavior, where the highest ordering temperatures have been observed for the azido-linked networks.
Magnetic formate-based frameworks include 2D square lattice-type structures, $^{164-167}$ homoleptic monometallic168−210 and heterobimetallic200 3D frameworks, and 3D networks with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine and related coligands.211,212 Among these compounds, the monometallic NiII and mixed-valence FeIIFeIII frameworks with $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)^{+}$ cations, $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ - $\left[{\bf N i}^{\mathrm{II}}\left(\mathrm{HCOO}\right)_{3}\right]$ 1 8 5 a n d $\mathbf{\widetilde{M}e}_{2}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ ) $\big[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ - $\left(\mathrm{HCOO}\right)_{6}\right],^{198,200,204}$ exhibit the highest magnetic ordering temperatures of 35.6 and $37~\mathrm{\K},$ respectively. These 3D frameworks are isostructural, featuring distorted octahedral metal centers linked by anti−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ bridges that favor antiferromagnetic coupling between spin centers, and $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations are located in the cages of the networks. The $\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ framework orders as a canted antiferromagnet, whereas the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}F e^{I I I}}$ network displays Néel N-type ferrimagnetism. Notably, these and other formate-based 3D frameworks show multiferroic behavior with coexistent and coupled magnetic and electric order,191,199,203−206,210 which is a rare phenomenon among metal-based coordination networks bearing carbon-containing ligands.
## 3. MOF MAGNETS WITH DIAMAGNETIC BRIDGING LIGANDS
## 3.1. Introduction
The vast majority of MOF magnets reported to date are comprised of paramagnetic metal centers bridged by diamagnetic ligands, where the magnetic interactions between metal centers occur via a superexchange coupling mechanism. The strength of this interaction, as quantified by the exchange coupling constant $J,$ is directly correlated to the magnetic ordering temperature of 2D and 3D magnets.23 As such, to realize magnets with high operating temperatures, the metal centers and linkers must be chosen with care to maximize the magnetic exchange interactions between spin centers. Specifically, the occupancy and degeneracy of the d orbitals of transition metal ions and their relative orientation with respect to bridging ligand orbitals are critical factors in determining the strength and sign of superexchange interactions. In simple systems comprised of paramagnetic metal ions bridged by a diamagnetic single-atom ligand such as $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ , a set of semiempirical rules, known as the Goodenough−Kanamori rules, have been developed on the basis of the symmetry relationships and occupancy of the overlapping metal d orbitals and ligand $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~p~}}$ orbitals.213,214 In general, these rules predict (i) strong antiferromagnetic coupling between two metal ions that have singly occupied symmetry-compatible d orbitals (typical for a $\bf{M-O-M}$ bond angle of ${\sim}180^{\circ}$ ), (ii) weak ferromagnetic coupling between two metal ions that have singly occupied symmetry-incompatible d orbitals (typical for a $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}$ bond angle of ${\sim}90^{\circ}.$ ), and (iii) weak ferromagnetic coupling between a metal ion with singly occupied d orbitals and a metal ion with empty or doubly occupied d orbitals of the same type.213−217 However, predicting the strength and sign of superexchange for more complicated systems is not as straightforward owing to multiple competing exchange interactions, deviations of metal−ligand−metal bond angles from $90^{\circ}$ or $180^{\circ}$ , and/or the presence of multiatom bridges. Accordingly, the strength and sign of superexchange interactions in metal−organic magnets are dictated by the nature and coordination geometry of the spin centers and the type of metal−ligand bonds ( $\scriptstyle{\dot{\sigma}}$ or $\pi$ ).
Because the strength of coupling between metal centers decreases drastically as the number of atoms in the bridge increases,145 the use of short linkers providing one-, two-, or three-atom coupling pathways is favored.24 However, most small ligands able to mediate strong exchange interactions between metal centers are inorganic in nature, such as oxo, hydroxo, azido, cyano, and thiocyanato ligands. Furthermore, short linkers often afford dense frameworks with no porosity. Therefore, a balance in linker length is critical for simultaneously imparting strong magnetic interactions and structural voids in extended networks.
Carboxylate-based ligands are particularly well-suited toward this end,218 as the carboxylate group can link metal centers through both single-atom $(\mathrm{M-}\mathrm{\bar{O}}-\mathrm{\bar{M}})$ and three-atom (M− $_{\mathrm{O-C-O-M}})$ bridges (see Figure 5). In addition, Nheterocycles, such as azolates and pyrimidines, are attractive linkers owing to their versatile bridging modes through short two-atom $(\bar{\bf M}{-}\bar{\bf N}{-}\mathrm{\bfN-}\mathrm{\bfM})$ or three-atom $\left(\mathbf{M-N-C-N-M}\right)$ $\bf{M}-\bf{N}-\bf{N}-\bf{N}-\bf{N}-\bf{M})$ linkages (see Figure 6). Notably, most MOF magnets are constructed from organic ligands featuring some degree of conjugation, as the presence of an extended $\pi\cdot$ - system enables additional spin−spin coupling mechanisms that may enhance the overall magnetic exchange interaction. One such coupling pathway is the spin-polarization mechanism between ${\bf d}_{\pi}$ and $\mathtt{p}_{\pi}$ orbitals of metal centers and organic ligands, respectively, where an unpaired electron on one atom polarizes the electron cloud on adjacent atoms such that the most stable spin configuration constitutes antiparallel arrangement of adjacent electronic spins. Accordingly, antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic interactions between metal centers are favored when the magnetic $\pi$ -pathway through the bridging ligand consists of even and odd numbers of atoms, respectively.219−224
The use of organic linkers that provide three-atom bridges between metal centers is of particular interest, as on the basis of symmetry considerations, this connectivity will in most cases allow the antisymmetric interaction known as the Dzyaloshinskii−Moriya interaction.24,225,226 Specifically, this interaction is allowed when there is no inversion center between the two bridged metal centers, and it can lead to canted spin structures. As a result, canted antiferromagnetic behavior is often observed in three-atom-bridged systems.
## 3.2. Phosphonate-Containing Compounds
## 3.3. Oxalate-Containing Compounds
3.3.1. Introduction. The oxalate ion $\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{O_{4}}^{2-}\right)$ acts as a short multiatom ligand (see Figure 8) that is commonly observed in 1D, 2D, and 3D magnetic materials owing to its ability to mediate both ${\boldsymbol{\sigma}}\cdot{}$ and $\pi$ -electronic pathways for magnetic superexchange through two connected $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ bridges. The presence of a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ bond between the two carboxylate groups promotes the formation of an extended $\pi$ - system and facilitates a bis(bidentate) ligand binding mode. The oxalato ligand has found enormous success in the rational design of molecule-based magnets, as a vast number of homoand heterometallic 2D and 3D oxalate frameworks have shown permanent magnetism with ordering temperatures of $\leq70$ $\stackrel{\star}{\mathrm{K}}^{235,236}$ In this section, we discuss the synthetic strategies adopted to afford oxalate-based magnets with different dimensionalities, structures, and magnetic properties. Specifically, we highlight the role of templating cations and the choice of metal centers in dictating the properties of these materials, and the different behaviors of homo- and heteroleptic frameworks. Finally, we conclude this section with a short discussion on the potential of this family of magnetic frameworks as multifunctional molecule-based materials. For a more comprehensive account on oxalate-based magnetic frameworks, the reader is referred to other review articles.237,238
3.3.2. Oxalate-Containing 2D Frameworks. A wide range of homoleptic mixed-valence metal oxalates of the general formula $\mathrm{(\hat{A)[{M^{II}}{M^{I I I}}({o x})_{3}]}}$ $\mathbf{\chi}^{\prime}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ , CoII, NiII, or ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{III}},$ , or $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ ; $\mathbf{ox}^{2-}={}$ oxalate), where ${\mathbf A}^{+}$ refers to a monovalent cation, have been reported $^{239-248}$ since the seminal contribution in 1990.239 The structure of these frameworks features an anionic 2D honeycomb lattice consisting of alternating octahedral ${{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ and $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{iII}}$ ions that are each chelated by three bis(bidentate) oxalato ligands. The formation of the honeycomb lattice requires adjacent metal centers to adopt opposite chirality ( $\dot{\Delta}$ or $\Lambda$ ) (see Figure 13), and the ${\mathbf A}^{+}$ cations are typically interleaved between the anionic layers. The nature of magnetic exchange within the layers is dictated by the identity of the trivalent metal center. For instance, $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ promote ferromagnetic interactions, whereas $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ promotes antiferromagnetic interactions. Interactions between adjacent layers are generally minimal, and thus, these compounds are best described as 2D magnets.
Investigations of the effects of different ${\mathbf A}^{+}$ cations on the magnetic properties of homoleptic $\mathrm{Fe^{II}F e^{I I I}}$ 2D oxalate frameworks revealed that the nature of ${\mathbf A}^{+}$ can play an important role in the magnetic behavior of these ferrimagnets. $240-242$ While several quaternary ammonium frameworks exhibit similar ordering temperatures of 44−46 K, the value of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ decreases to 34 and $36\mathrm{~K~}$ when $\mathrm{(Ph_{4}P)^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{(Ph_{4}A s)^{+}}$ cations are used, respectively (see Table S1). Furthermore, $\left(\mathrm{Ph}_{4}\mathrm{P}\right)\left[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathbf{ox})_{3}\right]$ and $(\mathrm{Ph}_{4}\mathrm{A}s)[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ display spin glass behavior below $T_{\mathrm{c}},$ whereas the analogous frameworks incorporating other quaternary phosphonium or ammonium cations show anomalous negative magnetization below a compensation temperature $(T_{\mathrm{comp}})$ , defined as the temperature at which the net magnetization value is zero as it changes from a positive to a negative value (see Table S1). A compensation temperature should occur in the magnetization for a system with two spin−lattices if the sublattice with the smaller saturation magnetization initially orders more rapidly as the temperature is decreased than the lattice with the larger saturation magnetization. Accordingly, the observed Néel Ntype ferrimagnetic order for these frameworks has been attributed to an initial steeper ordering of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sublattice relative to the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ lattice. This hypothesis was supported by analyzing the temperature dependence of the hyperfine fields experienced at the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ and $\mathrm{\hat{F}e^{\mathrm{III}}}$ nuclei through Mössbauer spectroscopy.240−242
Notably, the magnitude of the negative magnetization was later found to be influenced by the preparation conditions of these frameworks due to variable amounts of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ vacancies.243
The strong influence of the templating cation and solvent molecules in controlling the metal coordination environments in these systems, and the resulting magnetic properties, is exemplified in a series of heterobimetallic framework compounds of the general formula [K(18-crown6 $\mathrm{)]}_{3}[\mathbf{\bar{M}^{I I}}_{3}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{4}(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathbf{ox}\mathbf{\bar{)}}_{3})_{3}]$ $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}},\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{\mathrm{II}},{\mathbf{C0}^{\mathrm{II}}},\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{\mathrm{II}},\right.$ or ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ).264,265 These 2D frameworks are isostructural and display a honeycomb-like structure in which two-thirds of the divalent metal ions are coordinated by two $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules that are hydrogen-bonded to terminal oxalato ligands (see Figure 14). The $[\mathrm{K}(18–\mathrm{crown}–6)]^{+}$ cations are located in the hexagonal pores between layers, and their large diameter promotes the fragmentation of the network. The $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ frameworks exhibit long-range ferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{C}}=3.2\mathrm{-}8\ \mathrm{K},$ whereas the $\mathbf{\check{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ analogues behave as ferrimagnets $\begin{array}{r l r}{\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\right.}&{{}=}&{\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}}\end{array}$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ) or as a canted antiferromagnet $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\right)$ ) below $T_{c}=11.5\substack{-25.5\mathrm{~K~}}$ (see Table S3). Notably, the ordering temperatures for this series of frameworks are considerably lower than those obtained for the analogous 2D networks of regular honeycomb topolog y,240−245,266,267 highlighting that the superexchange through the longer ${\bf M}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{water}}{\bf\bar{\alpha}}{\cdot}{\bf O}{-}{\bf\bar{C}-}\mathrm{O-}{\bf M}^{\mathrm{III}}$ bridge in the fragmented compounds is significantly weaker than via the $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}-$ $\mathrm{O-C-O-\hat{M}^{I I I}}$ linkage in conventional oxalate honeycomb frameworks.
The first example of a neutral oxalate-based layered magnet, the heterobimetallic ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{Cr}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ compound $\begin{array}{r}{{\big[}{\dot{\mathrm{Co}}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\big(}\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}{\big)}_{2}{\big]}_{3}.}\end{array}$ - $\left[\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}\right]_{2}{\cdot}2\left(18{\cdot}\mathrm{crown}{\cdot}6\right)$ , was reported in 2007.268 The structure of this compound features layers of 12-membered rings constituting six distorted octahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions and six $[\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]^{3-}$ units, where two trans- or cis-oriented $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules complete the coordination sphere of ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . The crown ether moieties are located in the pores of the rings and provide further structural support through hydrogen bonding interactions (see Figure 15). Notably, the absence of interlamellar cations enables a short interlayer distance of 7.825(2) Å. This compound behaves as a ferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ $=~7.4~\mathrm{~K~},$ in accord with other 2D $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ oxalate-based frameworks.268 Magnetic hysteresis measurements at $2\ \mathrm{~K~}$ revealed a small coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=160$ Oe and a remanent
magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}\ =\ 2.3\ \mu_{\mathrm{B}}\ \mathrm{mol^{-1}}.$ , indicative of a soft ferromagnet.269
The synthetic strategy of using a neutral crown ether guest molecule as a templating agent has been extended to other paramagnetic metal ions, providing an isostructural family of neutral 2D layered magnets of the general formula $\mathrm{\widetilde{[M^{II}(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{3}[M^{I I I}(o x)_{3}]_{2}\cdot2\widetilde{(18\mathrm{-crown-}\ell}}}$ 6) $\begin{array}{r}{(\dot{\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}},}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ , ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ , or $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ; $\mathbf{\bar{M}}^{\mathrm{III}}=\mathbf{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ).269 These frameworks exhibit ferromagnetic $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}\ =\ \mathbf{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}\right)$ or ferrimagnetic $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}\ =\ \mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}\right),$ ) long-range order with ${T_{\mathrm{c}}=3.6{-}12.2\ \mathrm{K}}$ (see Table S4). The use of a mixture of divalent metal ions in a 1:1 ratio has also been explored. Among these compounds, the highest ordering temperature of Tc = 20.0 K was achieved for the FeIINiIIFeIII framework, whereas the $\mathbf{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ framework provided the highest coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=6300$ Oe at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ (see Table S4). All other bi- and trimetallic compounds were classified as soft magnets. Interestingly, these and other crown ether-containing oxalate frameworks are soluble in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ but insoluble in organic solvents, which may allow for the growth of high-quality single crystals suitable for a suite of magnetic studies to provide a more detailed understanding of the associated magnetism.269
Along these lines, intercalation of the organic $\pi$ donor bis(ethylenedithio)tetrafulvalene (BEDT-TTF) into the bimetallic anionic oxalate network $[\mathrm{Mn^{\mathrm{II}}C r^{\mathrm{III}}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]^{-}$ provided the first example of coexistent ferromagnetism and metallic conductivity in a molecule-based compound.21 Specifically, this compound behaves as a ferromagnet below $T_{\mathrm{C}}=5.5\ \mathrm{K}$ and is metallic down to at least $0.3\mathrm{~K~}$ However, the presence of electrically conducting organic layers between the metal− oxalate layers did not afford enhanced magnetic performance, as the two sublattices are quasi-independent of one another. Building on this initial report, researchers incorporated various tetrathiafulvalene derivatives, which form the basis of most known molecular electrical conductors and superconductors,271 into 2D oxalate frameworks.272−276 Structural analysis of these composite materials is challenging owing to the high degree of crystallographic disorder of the oxalate-based layers, preventing the definitive assignment of atomic positions. Accordingly, accurate determination of the chemical formula, formal charges, and interactions between the conducting and magnetic layers is not immediately forthcoming. Although current attempts have not yet afforded compounds demonstrating synergy between the two phenomena, this may be an attractive strategy for furnishing conductive magnetic materials.
Insertion of spin-crossover $\breve{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complexes into homo- and heterobimetallic layered oxalate canted antiferromagnets and ferromagnets has also been reported.273,277−282 The resulting compounds feature either a single or a double layer of the cationic spin-crossover complexes (see Figure 17). However, in either case, little interplay between the two magnetic entities has been detected, likely due to the large difference in temperature at which spin-crossover and magnetic order occur. Specifically, while spin-crossover takes place at high temperatures, in the range of $100{-}350\ \mathrm{K},$ the long-range magnetic order is only present below $8.1\mathrm{~K~}$ in these systems (see Table S8). More recently, the coexistence of long-range ferromagnetic order and photoinduced spin-crossover, also known as light-induced excited spin-state trapping (LIESST), has been demonstrated.279,282 However, the photoinduced spin-state change of the inserted $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ cations has a similar negligible influence on the magnetic behavior of the 2D oxalate framework. Nevertheless, because the LIESST effect is typically observed at lower temperatures than thermally induced spincrossover,283 it may be a more promising route in realizing cooperativity between the two magnetic processes.
Several layered oxalate frameworks of other topologies have also been reported to show long-range magnetic order.284−287 This set includes the homoleptic $\mathbf{\check{F}e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ compound $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{ox})$ - $\left(\mathrm{MeOH}\right)^{287}$ and the first coordination networks featuring both bridging oxalato and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy ligands, $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathbf{ox})(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{F}}$ y) $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\right.$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\Psi}}&{{}=\mathbf{\Psi}\tilde{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{II}}}\end{array}$ , ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , or $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ; $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{ox}^{2-}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ oxalate; $4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}\ =\ 4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}$ - bipyridine).284,285 T hese frameworks adopt a square-grid layered structure and exhibit long-range antiferromagnetic or canted antiferromagnetic order with TN = 12−26 K.284,285,287
3.3.3. Oxalate-Containing 3D Frameworks. The formation of 3D oxalate-based framework magnets is favored when chiral metal complexes, such as $[\mathrm{Z^{II}}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy})_{3}]^{2+}$ $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Sigma}}^{\mathrm{II}}=$ $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ , or $\hat{{\mathrm{Ru}}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}\ =\ 2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine), are employed as templating agents. Here, chiral networks of the general formulas $[\mathrm{Z^{II}\bar{(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}b p y)}_{3}][M_{\ 2}^{I I}(o x)_{3}]}$ and $[Z^{\mathrm{II}}(2,2^{\prime}.$ $\mathrm{\bar{b}p y)_{3}][M^{I I}M^{I I I}(o x)_{3}](C l O_{4})}$ $\mathrm{(Z^{II}=F e^{I I},C o^{I I},N i^{I I}}$ , or $\mathrm{{Ru}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ; ${\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ $\mathrm{\Lambda=Mn^{II},~F e^{I I},~C o^{I I},~N i^{I I},}$ or ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe^{III}}^{\mathrm{{III}}^{\mathrm{{}}}}$ ) are generated for monovalent and mixed-valence oxalate species, respectively.288−295 The 3D structure is comprised of trigonally distorted octahedral $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}/\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}$ metal ions that are tris-chelated by bis(bidentate) oxalato ligands and linked into threeconnected 10-membered rings to give a network with (10,3) topology (see Figure 18). The cavities in the lattice are occupied by $[\mathrm{Z^{II}}(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}$ cations and $(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})^{-}$ anions. When two types of metal centers are present in the oxalatebased lattice, they alternate in the framework. Notably, all of the metal centers in these compounds possess the same configuration ( $\dot{\Delta}$ or $\Lambda$ ), as the chirality of the templating cationic entities determines the chirality of the anionic framework, with $\Delta-\Delta$ and $\Lambda{-}\Lambda$ interactions being favored. Indeed, the chiral or achiral character of the templating cation is commonly a dominant factor in determining whether a 2D or 3D oxalate system is formed.
The 3D oxalate frameworks are attractive candidates for magneto-optical materials as they can be prepared in optically active forms.290,292 Indeed, oxalate frameworks represent the first examples of 3D molecule-based magnets displaying optical activity.290 Moreover, these ferromagnets were found to show measurable magnetic circular dichroism (MCD) below their ordering temperatures.
The employment of the templating cation to impart additional physical properties to the oxalate framework has also been investigated. Introduction of a paramagnet cation, in this case nitronyl nitroxide radical cation, did not significantly affect the magnetic behavior of the oxalate network,296 which is in line with previous studies for 2D oxalate frameworks. Similarly, no cooperative magnetic properties have been observed when $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{\hat{I}I}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ spin-crossover complexes are used as templates in an attempt to furnish the formation of hybrid magnets. The modest change in the magnetic ordering temperature (TC = 3.0−5.2 K) for the ferromagnetic MnIICrIII oxalate network in the presence of this set of cations likely stems from slight structural changes associated with the different sizes of the cationic complexes and the nature of the chelating ligands (see Table S14).278,281,297−300
In contrast, insertion of the single-molecule magnet $\left[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\bigl(\mathsf{s a l e n}\bigr)\bigl(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\bigr)\right]_{2}{}^{2+}$ $\left[\mathsf{s a l e n}^{2-}\right.\ =\ N,N^{\prime}.$ -ethylenebis(salicylideneiminate)] into the ferromagnetic $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}}$ lattice afforded the hybrid compound [MnIII(salen)(H2O)]2- $\left[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{III}}\left(\mathbf{ox}\right)_{3}\right]_{2}^{\prime}$ ·MeOH·2MeCN (see Figure 20, top), whose magnetic properties are not a simple sum of those for the two components, as illustrated by a comparison to control compounds.301 Specifically, the two magnetic networks in this compound interact antiferromagnetically to give a ferrimagnetic phase that exhibits large magnetic hysteresis below ${1}\ \bar{\mathrm{K}},$ which contrasts with the behavior observed for the singlemolecule magnet in a paramagnetic matrix and the ferromagnetic oxalate (see Figure 20, bottom). As such, this first example of the interplay between single-molecule magnetism and long-range magnetic order in an oxalate framework demonstrated that cooperative magnetic properties can be realized in these materials.
