## 3.4. Oxamate-Containing Compounds
3.4.1. Introduction. A closely related family of building units that has been employed to construct porous frameworks with predictable structures and tunable magnetic properties are aromatic polyoxamato ligands (see Figure 8). One of the most prominent members of this series is the metallamacrocycle $\mathrm{\hat{[}C u^{\mathrm{{II}}}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{mpba}\big)_{2}\mathrm{]^{4-}}$ $\begin{array}{r c l}{\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{mpba}^{4-}}&{=}&{N,N^{\prime}{-}1,3}\end{array}$ -phenylenebis(oxamate)]. This moiety features two $S=\sqrt[1]{\int_{2}^{}{\mathbf{C}}\mathbf{\dot{u}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ centers that are ferromagnetically coupled through a double 1,3- phenylenediamidate cyclophane-type skeleton, where the two aromatic rings are stacked in an eclipsed fashion to facilitate $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions (see Figure 23).314,315 Notably, this compound represents the first dinuclear complex for which ferromagnetic coupling between two ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centers arises from spin-polarization effects, which was ascribed to its peculiar topolog y.224,314,315 When this dinuclear CuII complex is treated with divalent transition metal ions, such as $\mathrm{Mn}^{2\bar{+}}$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , 2D or 3D frameworks are formed owing to coordination of the remaining oxamate $\mathrm{~O~}$ atoms to the divalent metal centers. As such, the $\mathrm{\bar{C}}\mathbf{u}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complex acts as a metalloligand toward divalent first-row transition metal centers. Successful examples of this metalloligand approach to constructing oxamate-based magnetic MOFs have recently been reviewed.316
3.4.2. Oxamate-Containing 2D Frameworks. The first framework featuring this building unit was the bimetallic compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}_{2}\mathrm{(mpba)}_{2}\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{\breve{O})}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .317 Its structure consists of chains of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{\bar{II}}}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions connected through bis(bidentate) oxamate groups, which are further linked by cyclophane moieties to give a corrugated 2D network of a brick wall-type topology. Each brick is formed by four ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions in a distorted octahedral geometry and six square-pyramidal ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions, where $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules complete the coordination spheres of the metal centers. The corrugated sheets stack in a zigzag fashion with $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solvent molecules occupying the space between the layers (see Figure 24, left). Strong antiferromagnetic interactions between $\mathrm{\bar{C}\mathbf{u}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{iI}}}$ ions within each layer, together with ferromagnetic interactions between adjacent $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions, afford overall ferrimagnetic layers. This compound orders as an antiferromagnet below $9.5~\mathrm{K}.$ However, a magnetic field above 1200 Oe is sufficient to overcome the weak antiferromagnetic interlayer interactions to give a ferromagnetic-like state. This metamagnetic behavior is evident from the field dependence of the magnetization and a butterflyshaped hysteresis loop, which was ascribed to reversal of the ferrimagnetic layers from an antiparallel to a parallel configuration (see Figure 24, right). Furthermore, ac magnetic susceptibility measurements revealed glassy magnetic behavior for this compound below the ordering temperature.
Interestingly, an analogous compound with a formula of ${\bf M}{\bf n}^{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\bf C}{\bf u}^{\mathrm{II}}_{2}(\tilde{\bf M}{\bf\bar{e}}_{3}{\bf m p b a})_{2}(\tilde{\bf H_{2}O})_{6}{\cdot}8\tilde{\bf H}_{2}\tilde{\bf O}$ $\left[\mathrm{Me_{3}m p b a^{4-}}~=~2,4,6-\right.$ trimethyl- $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -1,3-phenylenebis(oxamate)] was shown to behave as a soft ferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{C}}=20.0~\mathrm{K}.^{318}$ No crystal structure is reported for this compound, but PXRD analysis indicates that it is isostructural to the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ brick wall layered framework described above. Here, the oxamatobridged chains of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are linked into layers through a trimethyl-substituted 1,3-phenylenediamidate cyclophane spacer. The long-range ferromagnetic order was ascribed to ferromagnetic interactions between ferrimagnetic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Cu}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ layers, in contrast to the dominant antiferromagnetic interlayer interactions observed for the analogous ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}{\bf C}\mathbf{\breve{u}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework at low magnetic fields.
One example of a 2D oxamate magnet with an alternative topology is the compound $\mathrm{(MV)[Mn_{\Omega}^{I I}{}_{2}C u_{\Omega}^{I I}(m p b a)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}]}$ · $20\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}$ ( $\mathbf{\widetilde{MV}}^{2+}=$ methylviologen dication).319 It is built from tetra2kis(bidentate) square-pyramidal ${\mathbf{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}_{2}$ anions, $\mathrm{\small{[Cu^{H}}_{2}(m p b a)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]^{4-}},$ , and tris-chelated octahedral $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions with alternating opposite chirality ( $\Delta$ or $\Lambda$ ). These building blocks are connected into oxamato-bridged flat irregular hexagonal honeycomb layers. The layers are linked through two 1,3-phenylenediamidate spacers between the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions to give a double-layer architecture with hexagonal nanopores occupied by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solvent molecules, whereas the $\mathbf{MV}^{2\dagger}$ cations are located in the intralayer cavities. This framework exhibits long-range ferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{{C}}}={}$ 19.0 K owing to weak dipolar and/or through-bond ferromagnetic interactions between adjacent ferrimagnetic double layers, likely through hydrogen-bonded lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. Furthermore, this compound displays a reversible dehydration to an amorphous phase that shows shape-selective adsorption behavior and guest-induced luminescence.
3.4.3. Oxamate-Containing 3D Frameworks. This metalloligand design strategy has also afforded unique multifunctional oxamate-based bimetallic 3D MOFs.316 For instance, the compound $\big[\mathrm{Na}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{4}\big]_{4}\big[\mathrm{Mn}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(\mathrm{Cu}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(\mathrm{mpba}\big)_{2}-$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}\right)_{3}]\cdot56.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\bar{O}}$ $\mathrm{\small~\left[mpba^{4-}~=~\right.}N,N^{\prime}{-1}$ ,3-phenylenebis(oxamate)] features oxamato-bridged $\mathbf{Mn}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Cu}_{6}^{\mathrm{II}}$ layers interconnected through 1,3-phenylenediamidate spacers at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centers to give a 3D structure with square and octagonal pores.320 The large octagonal pores $(21\times\bar{3}0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2})$ host solvated ${{\mathrm{Na}}^{+}}$ ions and arrays of hydrogen-bonded $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. This framework undergoes a reversible dehydration to an amorphous phase, in analogy to the 2D framework described above, with associated changes in magnetic behavior. The hydrated crystalline framework orders as a ferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{C}}~=~22.5~\mathrm{K},$ while the amorphous anhydrous framework shows an onset of long-range ferromagnetic order below $2.3~\mathrm{K}.$ The dramatic reduction in magnetic ordering temperature upon dehydration has been attributed to a change in the coordination environment at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers, from six- to fourcoordinate ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ , leading to weaker intra- and interlayer interactions for the dehydrated framework. The isoreticular framework $\big[\mathrm{Na}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\dot{\mathrm{O}_{\big)}}_{3.25}\big]_{4}\big[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\big(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}\big(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{mpba}\big)_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{\left(H_{2}O\right)}_{3.33}\mathrm{)}_{3}]{\cdot}37\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $[\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{mpba}^{4-}=2,4,6\cdot$ -trimethyl- $N,N^{\prime}$ -1,3- phenylenebis(oxamate)], featuring a trimethyl-substituted 1,3- phenylenediamidate cyclophane linker, exhibits a similar structure and guest-dependent magnetic properties (see Figure 25).321 The hydrated framework orders as a ferromagnet with
$T_{\mathrm{C}}~=~21.0~\mathrm{{\calK}},$ whereas the anhydrous derivative exhibits a dramatic solvent-dependent modulation of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ . While the anhydrous framework itself is amorphous with $T_{\mathrm{c}}<2.0~\mathrm{K},$ the MeOH adsorbate is crystalline and isostructural to the hydrated framework based on PXRD analysis and orders as a ferromagnet below $T_{\mathrm{C}}=6.5\ \mathrm{K}$ (see Figure 25, bottom). The coexistence of selective vapor (MeOH over MeCN and EtOH) and gas ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ) adsorption behavior, in conjunction with solvent-dependent changes in the magnetic ordering temperature for this framework, makes it an attractive candidate for magnetic sensing of small guest molecules.
Furthermore, incorporation of the mononuclear $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complex $[\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{III}}}(\mathrm{sal}_{2}\mathrm{\cdottrien}{\hat{\ n}})]^{+}\ [(\mathrm{sal}_{2}\mathrm{\cdottrien})^{2-}=N,N^{\prime}.$ -disalicylidenetriethylenetetramine dianion] into the pores of the hydrated 3D framework in a single-crystal-to-single-crystal process afforded the hybrid material $\left[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{\tiny{III}}}\big(\dot{\mathsf{s a l}}_{2}{\mathsf{-t r i e n}}\big)\right]$ - $\mathrm{{Na}_{3}[\mathrm{{Mn}_{4}^{I I}(\mathrm{{Cu}_{\ 2}^{I I}(\mathrm{{Me}_{3}\mathrm{{mpba})_{2})_{3}]\cdot43H_{2}O,}}}}}$ which exhibits both long-range magnetic order and spin-crossover behavior.322 Specifically, the ${{\bf{N}}_{}}{{\bf{a}}_{}}^{+}$ counterions are partially replaced by $[\bar{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{sal}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{trien})]^{+}$ ions, which undergo a transition from an S $=\ {^5}/_{2}$ high-spin state to an $S~=~\sqrt[1]{\int}_{2}$ low-spin state with a decrease in the temperature from 400 to $100~\mathrm{K}.$ Interestingly, no spin-state change was observed for the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ precursor salt within this temperature range. Moreover, the magnetic ordering temperature for the framework was shown to increase from 14 to $19\mathrm{K}$ upon insertion of the spin-crossover complex, suggesting significant interactions between the two magnetic units. Note that the authors did not comment on the lower value of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ obtained for the unmodified 3D framework relative to that initially reported (Tc = 14 K versus Tc = 21 K).321,322 However, they pointed out that an aqueous suspension of the compound affords a $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ value of $19{\mathrm{~K~}}$ As such, it is possible that this discrepancy originates from the different sample preparation for magnetic measurements.
Along similar lines, the single-molecule magnet $\mathrm{\Gamma[Mn^{III}(\bar{T}P P)(H_{2}O)_{2}]^{+}}$ $\mathrm{TPP^{2-}}=$ meso-tetraphenylporphyrinato) was successfully incorporated into the pores of this 3D MOF magnet to afford a hybrid material with coexisting longrange magnetic order and slow magnetic relaxation.32 Specifically, the magnetic lattice slowed the quantum tunneling processes for the single-ion magnet such that slow magnetic relaxation was observed below $5\mathrm{K}$ in the absence of an applied dc field, in contrast to the behavior observed for $\left[{\bf M n}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{\bar{TPP}})\right.$ - $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}]\left(\mathrm{ClO}_{4}\right)$ . These two materials have enabled detailed studies of the interplay between magnetic host frameworks and magnetic molecular guests, adding to the extensive work in this area for oxalate-based frameworks.
The triple-stranded hexakis(bidentate) complex $[\mathbf{M}_{2}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{{mpba})}_{3}]^{8-}$ $\mathrm{[M^{\mathrm{II}}=N i^{\mathrm{II}}}$ or ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $\mathrm{\mpba}^{4-}~=~N,N^{\prime}{-}1,3{-}$ - phenylenebis(oxamate)] represents another dinuclear metalloligand that can provide 3D frameworks. This building unit facilitates the formation of structures with honeycomb topology; however, it has thus far not afforded crystalline frameworks exhibiting long-range magnetic order.324,325 Either amorphous compounds that display long-range ferromagnetic order, such as $\bar{\mathrm{Li}_{2}}\mathrm{[Mn^{II}_{3}C o^{I I}_{2}(\bar{m p b a})_{3}(\bar{H_{2}}O)_{6}]{\cdot22H_{2}O}}$ $\overset{\cdot}{T}_{\mathrm{C}}=$ $6.5~\mathrm{~K~}),$ or crystalline materials that show only weak magnetic interactions have been reported. In contrast, dianionic mononuclear ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ complexes have been employed as bis(bidentate) metalloligands toward solvated $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ cations to afford crystalline oxamate frameworks that exhibit longrange magnetic order.326
Here, the dimensionality of oxamato-bridged heterobimetallic MnII2CuII3 frameworks can be controlled by varying the steric effects of alkyl substituents on $N$ -phenyloxamato bridging ligands. To illustrate, the employment of $\mathrm{\dot{M}e_{2}p m a}^{2-}$ (N-2,6-dimethylphenyloxamate) as a bridging ligand afforded a 2D honeycomb framework with a formula of $\mathrm{(Bu_{4}N)_{4}}.$ - $\mathrm{\small\left[Mn_{\ell_{4}C u}^{I I}\bar{\Phi}_{6}(M e_{2}p m a)_{12}(D M S O)_{2}\right]\cdot8D M S O\cdot2H_{2}O}.$ However, replacing the Me groups on the oxamato ligand with slightly bulkier Et groups gave the compound $\left(\mathrm{B}\mathbf{\check{u}}_{4}\mathbf{N}\right)_{4}[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{\check{C}}\mathbf{u}_{6}^{\mathrm{II}}-$ $\left(\mathrm{Et_{2}p m a}\right)_{12}\mathrm{\cdotDMSO\cdot10H_{2}O}$ $\left(\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{pma}^{2-}=N{-}2,6\right.$ -diethylphenyloxamate), which is a 3D decagonal network. Moderate antiferromagnetic intramolecular interactions between CuII and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions were observed for both frameworks. However, while the 2D framework undergoes a paramagnetic-toferromagnetic phase transition at $T_{\mathrm{C}}=10\mathrm{K},$ the 3D framework orders as a ferrimagnet at $20\mathrm{~K~}$ The significantly different magnetic properties for the two frameworks are attributed to their different dimensionalities, as the coordination environments of the metal centers are very similar in these compounds.
## 3.5. Carboxylate-Containing Compounds
Owing to the myriad structures and versatile metal-binding modes of these ligands (see Figure 9), carboxylate-based frameworks exhibit a vast number of topologies and multiple types of magnetic exchange pathways. Specifically, the compounds discussed in this section display structures with connectivities affording subnetworks of inorganic chains $(\mathrm{I}^{1}\mathrm{O}^{1}$ and $\operatorname{I}^{1}\mathrm{O}^{2})$ and layers $\left(\breve{\mathrm{I}}^{2}\mathrm{O}^{1}\right)$ , or exclusively inorganic ( $\mathrm{^{I^{2}O^{0}}}$ and ${\mathrm{I}}^{3}{\mathrm{O}}^{0};$ ) or organic networks ( $\mathrm{^{I^{0}O^{2}}}$ and ${\mathrm{I}}^{0}{\mathrm{O}}^{{\dot{3}}},$ ), where carboxylato ligands are the primary organic moieties mediating magnetic exchange interactions. The magnitude and sign of magnetic exchange in these systems are highly affected by the type of bridge between metal centers, bridging angle, metal−metal distance, and the number of metal-based d electrons.1 The strongest interactions are typically observed through monoatomic $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ bridges, followed by three-atom syn−syn and anti−anti M−O−C−O−M pathways.213,214,327−331
3.5.2. Malonate-Containing Compounds. Malonate, with a methylene group separating two carboxylate functionalities, is the shortest dicarboxylato ligand besides oxalate and has been employed to construct magnets with ordering temperatures falling in the range of $2.6{-}24~\mathrm{K}.^{329,332,333}$ For instance, a series of isostructural 2D framework compounds with a formula of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{[M^{II}(m a l)}_{2}\mathrm{]}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathbf{\hat{M}^{I I}=M\hat{n}^{\hat{I I}}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{II},}$ ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ , or $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ; $\mathrm{mal}^{2-}=$ malonate) order antiferromagnetically in different magnetic structures depending on the nature of the metal center.332 These compounds are comprised of layers of slightly distorted $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra that are linked by anti−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges, with ${{\bf{N}}{\bf{a}}^{+}}$ ions and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules located in the interlayer space. The ordering temperatures for this family of compounds range from $T_{\mathrm{N}}=$ 8(1) K for $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ to $T_{\mathrm{N}}=24(1)$ K for the $\bf\Tilde{N i}^{\mathrm{II}}$ congener, as confirmed by neutron diffraction studies. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{\mathrm{II}}}}$ frameworks exhibit open magnetic hysteresis loops at $5\mathrm{~K~}$ with remanent magnetization values of 0.26 and $0.0{\bar{1}}4\ \mu_{\mathrm{B}}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively, owing to slight canting of the magnetic moments.
In addition to this family of 2D malonate frameworks, two 3D malonato-bridged ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ compounds, $\mathbf{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{mal})(\mathrm{DMF})$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}^{I I}{}_{2}(m a l)_{2}(p y z){\cdot}{2}H_{2}O}$ $\mathbf{\bar{\mal}^{2-}}=$ malonate; $\mathrm{\pyz=}$ pyrazine), exhibit long-range magnetic order, albeit with lower ordering temperatures of 2.6 and $3.2~\mathrm{K},$ respectively.329,333 Ferromagnetic interactions through syn−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ bridges are dominant for $\mathrm{Cu^{II}(m a l)(\bar{D}M\bar{F}),^{329}}$ whereas antiferromagnetic interactions through syn−anti carboxylate bridges and pyrazine ligands lead to an antiferromagnetic ground state for $\mathrm{{Cu}^{I I}{}_{2}(m a l)_{2}(p y z){\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ in low magnetic fields.329,333
3.5.3. Succinate-Containing Compounds. Magnets featuring derivatives of the succinate molecule, which has an ethylene group separating the two carboxylate groups, are more common than those based on malonate. Many of these compounds feature both succinato and hydroxo ligands, such as the 2D frameworks $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\dot{2},2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{dm}s\mathrm{ucc})_{2}$ (2,2- dmsuc $\mathsf{c}^{2-\mathrm{~}}=\mathsf{\ c}^{2,2}$ -dimethylsuccinate), $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{4}\big(\mathrm{OH}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{succ}\big)_{3}.$ - $\mathrm{\left(H_{2}O\right)}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (MIL-16), and $\mathrm{Ni^{II}}_{7}(\mathrm{OH})_{6}(\mathrm{succ})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}$ · $\mathrm{7H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\sec^{2-}=$ succinate) (MIL-73), and the 3D $C o^{\mathrm{II}}.$ - based framework $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{succ})_{4}$ (MIL-9).334−337 Note, however, that these compounds do not strictly fall within our definition of a MOF, as the magnetic exchange interactions are governed by $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathbf{O}{-}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ superexchange paths in hydroxobridged chains or layers of $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{O}_{x}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{\tilde{C}}\mathbf{\hat{o}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{\Pi}}}$ ; $x=4$ or 6) polyhedra, where the succinato ligands contribute significantly to bonding only in at most one dimension. Nevertheless, these networks order as ferrimagnets or antiferromagnets with ordering temperatures of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=10{-}20$ K.334−337
The homoleptic compound $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{succ})$ ( $\operatorname{succ}^{2-}=$ succinate) features a unique 3D structure and magnetic properties.338,339 The structure consists of alternating layers containing chains of edge-sharing $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}O_{6}}}$ octahedra and layers of corner-sharing $\mathrm{\bfM}\mathrm{\bf\bar{n}}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{\bfO}_{6}$ octahedra. The two types of layers are connected through succinate-based carboxylate groups with an interlayer separation of ${\sim}7.5$ $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ (see Figure 26). Interestingly, this framework features two magnetic structures. Specifically, the chains of edge-sharing $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{\breve{O}}_{6}$ octahedra order antiferromagnetically at $10~\mathrm{K},$ where $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{O}_{6}$ octahedra in individual chains are ferromagnetically coupled. At $6~\mathrm{K},$ the layers of cornersharing $\mathrm{{Mn}^{I\bar{I}}{O_{6}}}$ octahedra order antiferromagnetically, and the lower ordering temperature for these layers is consistent with their longer nearest-neighbor superexchange pathway (see
Figure 26, bottom). Notably, the magnetic orders of the two distinct layers are essentially independent of each other. The two antiferromagnetic phases undergo further transitions under high applied dc fields, demonstrating the complex magnetic behavior of this succinate-based framework.
While frameworks featuring the 2,2-dimethylsuccinato ligand favor 2D layered structures ,334,340 frameworks incorporating 2,3-dimethylsuccinate can form either 2D or 3D structures depending on the choice of isomer for the succinato ligand.341 For instance, the chiral isomers (D and L) prefer an arrangement with the methyl and carboxylate groups gauche to the neighboring functional groups of the same type, favoring the formation of layered structures, whereas the meso-ligand prefers to adopt trans geometry, which reduces the steric hindrance and favors 3D structures (see Figure 27, top). Only frameworks containing the meso-ligand exhibit long-range magnetic order.341 The 3D framework $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ (meso-2,3-dmsucc)
$\left(m e s o-2,3-\mathrm{dmsucc}^{2-}=m e s o-2,3\right.$ -dimethylsuccinate) orders as a low-dimensional antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{{N}}}~=~50~\mathrm{{K}},$ as determined by the broad feature observed in a plot of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ versus $T_{\mathbf{\delta}}$ . Similar behavior was observed for 2D frameworks bdiaspelda omn c2h, ldoiwmer oyrlsduecricning te,maplebreait tehs.o3s34e, c0 mTphoe snidgsnificantly higher value of $\dot{T}_{\mathrm{N}}$ for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}(m e s o-2,3$ -dmsucc) suggests that stronger magnetic exchange is achieved between edge-sharing $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\dot{\mathbf{O}}_{6}$ octahedra (see Figure 27, top and center) than corner-sharing octahedra. Furthermore, the degree of corrugation plays a critical role in determining the strength of magnetic interactions in these frameworks. For instance, the analogous hydrated compound $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}(m e s o-2,3\mathrm{-dmsucc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ · $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ exhibits a 3D structure with chains of edge- and cornersharing $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}O_{6}}}$ octahedra that are more corrugated than the edge-sharing chains in the structure for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}(m e s o-2,3$ -dmsucc) (see Figure 27, bottom), and this compound orders as a 3D antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{N}}~=~7~\mathrm{K}.^{341}$ The higher magnetic dimensionality and lower ordering temperature for the hydrated framework suggest weaker intrachain but stronger interchain interactions compared to those of the anhydrous compound. The isostructural ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ congener of the anhydrous $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework further illustrates the relatively weak interchain interactions in these succinate-based frameworks. This compound exhibits 3D antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=$ $6\mathrm{~K~}$ in low magnetic fields but undergoes a transition to a ferromagnetic-like state under applied fields above $1400\mathrm{Oe}$ .341 Note that the ferromagnetic state arises from ferromagnetic nearest-neighbor interactions, consistent with the Goodenough−Kanamori rules for ${\bf d}^{7}$ metal centers and a ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{-O-}}$ ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ superexchange angle of $86.00(7)^{\circ}.^{213,214}$ In contrast, the intrachain interactions are antiferromagnetic for the $\dot{\mathbf{M}}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ frameworks.341
One of the first reported transition metal frameworks with mixed linear dicarboxylato ligands is the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{succ})_{4}(\mathrm{adip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}{\cdot}\tilde{5}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\sec^{2-}=$ succinate; $\mathsf{a d i p}^{2-}\ =$ adipate), which features succinato, adipato, and hydroxo bridging ligands.342 As the other hydroxo-containing succinate frameworks discussed in this section, this network represents a borderline case of a MOF. Hydroxo and succinato ligands provide intralayer connectivity, while the adipato ligands link adjacent layers together. This compound exhibits significant spin frustration, as assessed by a comparison between the Weiss constant $\stackrel{\prime}{\theta}=-34.9\mathrm{~K~}$ for data above 40 K) and the magnetic ordering temperature $\overset{\prime}{T}_{\mathrm{N}}=2.2\ \mathrm{K})$ , affording a value of $f=|\theta|/T_{\mathrm{N}}\approx16.$ In contrast, the $2\mathrm{D}\ \mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework $\mathrm{Mn^{II}}_{2}(\mathrm{succ})(\mathrm{adip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ features no $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}-}}$ ${\bf{O}}{-}{\bf{M}}{\bf{n}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ connectivity.342 Rather, the structure is comprised of distorted $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}O_{6}}}$ octahedra that are arranged into dimers, which are connected via $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges from succinate ions in one direction and adipato ligands in a second direction. Despite the lack of an inorganic subnetwork, this compound exhibits long-range antiferromagnetic order with a similar ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{N}}~=~2.1~\mathrm{~K~}$ . In summary, frameworks featuring a mixture of succinato and adipato bridging ligands possess architectures significantly different from the architectures of those that contain only one of these ligands. Accordingly, the magnetic properties are drastically different for these families of compounds.
3.5.4. Glutarate-Containing Compounds. Two glutarate-bridged frameworks have been reported to exhibit permanent magnetic behavior.343,344 The compound $\mathrm{\hat{Ni}_{20}^{I I}(g l u)}_{20}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\tilde{O}})_{8}{\cdot}40\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathtt{\langle g l u^{2-}=}$ glutarate) (MIL-77) is a
3D inorganic framework built up from edge-sharing $\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ octahedra bridged by glutarato and aquo ligands.343 The $\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ octahedra are connected into corrugated 20-membered rings (see Figure 28, top) that intersect with each other to generate crossing channels, which are occupied by the organic backbone of glutarato ligands along with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ lattice molecules. Upon heating, the lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules can be reversibly removed without significant structural changes, affording a porous structure with a Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $346\big(10\big)\ \mathrm{~m~}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ . This framework behaves as a ferromagnet below $4\mathrm{~K~}$ . Such ferromagnetic exchange interactions are in accord with edge-sharing $\mathrm{\tilde{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{O_{6}}}$ octahedra, and the low value of $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ for this compound was explained by the significant deviations of the $\mathrm{\DeltaNi^{II}{-}O{-}N i^{I I}}$ superexchange angles $\left[97.1(4)-99.9(4)^{\circ}\right]$ from the ideal $90^{\circ}$ angle.213,214
The other glutarate-based framework magnet is $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{(glu)}$ 1 $\mathbf{\nabla}_{\mathbf{g}\mathbf{l}\mathbf{u}^{2-}}=$ glutarate). This 3D framework features exclusively three-atom syn−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges between tetrahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ units.344 The structure displays pseudo-2D square layers that are linked through the alkyl chains of glutarate molecules (see Figure 28, bottom). One noticeable feature of the structure is that the glutarato ligands possess two different conformations, gauche and anti forms. This framework displays long-range antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=14\ \mathrm{K},$ where the dominant pathway for magnetic interactions is through the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{-O-}}\hat{\mathrm{C-}}\mathrm{O-}\dot{\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ linkages within the squaregrid layers.
3.5.5. Adipate-, Pimelate-, and Sebacate-Containing Compounds. Several frameworks featuring dicarboxylates with longer linear alkyl chains have been found to order magnetically below 20 K.342,345−347 The compounds ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\smash{\widetilde{\Pi}}}\bigl(\mathsf{a d i p}\bigr)$ $)\ \dot{(\mathrm{adip}^{2-}=\mathrm{adipate})^{342,345}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{pim})$ $\begin{array}{r l}{(\dot{\mathrm{pim}}^{2-}=}&{{}}\end{array}$ pimelate)346 (MIL-36) display analogous 3D structures as $\mathrm{\hat{C}o^{I I}(g l u)}\mathrm{\:\:\:(glu^{2-}\:\:=\:\:}$ glutarate), with layers of carboxylatobridged $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{\tiny{\widetilde{II}}}}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ tetrahedra that are linked through the alkyl chains of the carboxylato ligands along the third direction. The carboxylate groups on either end of the adipato ligand adopt a syn configuration, while those of the pimelato ligand possess an anti configuration. This is in contrast to the mixture of anti and gauche conformations found in the glutarate-based framework. The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathsf{a d i p})$ framework orders antiferromagnetically below $10~\mathrm{K},^{345}$ whereas $\mathrm{{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{{pim})}}$ exhibits canted antiferromagnet order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=20~\mathrm{K}$ Indeed, the latter compound displays an open magnetic hysteresis loop at $4.2~\mathrm{K},$ indicative of a weak ferromagnetism that is consistent with a canted spin structure.346
The 3D framework $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{seba})_{3}$ $\mathbf{\seba}^{2-}=$ sebacate) is comprised of inorganic chains that are linked by sebacato ligands through linear alkyl chains of 10 carbon atoms.347 The inorganic chains consist of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{x}$ ( $x=5$ or 6) polyhedra that are connected by carboxylato $\mathrm{~O~}$ atoms and $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ ligands (see Figure 29, top left). The magnetic properties for this framework are dominated by intrachain $\mathsf{\bar{C}o^{I I}-O-C o^{I I}}$ superexchange interactions, as the intrachain $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\cdots\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ distances of $3.040(\bar{7})-3.857(2)$ Å are much shorter than the interchain distances that range from 11.44 to 20.77 Å (see Figure 29, bottom left). Specifically, this compound exhibits both 3D long-range canted antiferromagnetic order and slow magnetic relaxation below $5.4\ \mathrm{K}.$ The canted antiferromagnetic order was postulated to be the result of uncompensated antiferromagnetic interactions within the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ chains, whereas the slow dynamic behavior under a zero applied dc field was hypothesized to stem from domain wall motion (see Figure 29, right). A fit of the Arrhenius plot of relaxation time for the frequency range of $10{-}5000\mathrm{Hz}$ afforded a relaxation barrier of $\Delta_{\tau}\stackrel{-}{=}47\big(1\big)\mathrm{\cim}^{-1}$ and a pre-exponential factor of $\tau_{0}=1.4\times$ $10^{-11}$ s, consistent with values reported for single-chain magnets.125 As such, the unique magnetic properties for $\mathrm{Co}_{4}^{\widetilde\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{seba})_{3}$ arise from the combination of strong magnetic interactions within the 1D inorganic subnetwork and poor magnetic communication through the long sebacato ligands, highlighting that the length of the dicarboxylato bridging ligands plays an important role in determining magnetic behavior for this class of compounds.
3.5.6. Compounds Containing Carboxylato Ligands with Additional Functional Groups. Several magnets that feature carboxylato ligands with additional functional groups, including NH2, OH, and SH, have been reported.331,348−353 One such compound is $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{asp}\big)_{2}\big(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-}\hat{\mathrm{bpy}}\big){\cdot}1.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (L$\mathsf{a s p}^{2-}=\mathtt{L}$ -aspartate; $4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine),348 a 3D chiral framework comprised of corrugated square-grid layers of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions connected via $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ bridges of aspartato ligands. Each ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ center resides in a distorted octahedral coordination environment, with the axial positions occupied by one aspartate $\mathbf{N}$ atom and one $\mathbf{N}$ atom from $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, which link adjacent layers. The lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules can be removed without altering the structure to create a porous network that exhibits a relatively high affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules but is unable to adsorb ${\bf N}_{2}$ . This discrepancy was ascribed to the small pores in the framework. The hydrated framework exhibits spontaneous weak magnetization below $14~\mathrm{K},$ which was ascribed to 3D long-range canted antiferromagnetic order. This interpretation was supported by a negative Weiss constant of $\theta=-7.68\mathrm{~K~}$ (from data between 25 and $300~\mathrm{K})$ , indicating overall antiferromagnetic interactions between ${\dot{\mathbf{C}}}{\boldsymbol{0}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers. Furthermore, spin-canted weak ferromagnetism is frequently observed for noncentrosymmetric structures featuring aniosotropic CoII ions.211,354
Another framework that is comprised of carboxylato-bridged layers that are connected by $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine ligands is the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}(\mathrm{hypa})_{2}(4,4^{'}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})$ $(\mathrm{\hat{h}y p a}^{2-}=\mathsf{\bar{2}}$ -hydroxo-2- phenylacetate; $4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine).349 This 3D framework contains dimeric ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ subunits, in which pentacoordinated ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are bridged by two alkoxo $\mathrm{~o~}$ atoms from distinct $\mathrm{{hypa}}^{2-}$ ligands. The dimers are connected via syn−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges into neutral layers (see Figure 30). This framework orders antiferromagnetically with $\dot{T_{\mathrm{N}}}=$ $15.2~\mathrm{K}$ . The dominant magnetic exchange pathway is between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions in the dimers, and strong antiferromagnetic superexchange interactions are in accord with a ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{O}}-$ ${\bf C}\hat{\bf o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ angle of $99.89(6)^{\circ}$ .213,214 Exchange interactions via the syn−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges are also potentially antiferromagnetic; however, the long interlayer $\mathbf{\hat{C}}\mathbf{o}^{\mathrm{II}}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{\mathbf{\hat{I}}\mathrm{I}}$ distance of 11.191(1) Å suggests that magnetic interactions through $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine are extremely weak, thereby leading to overall 2D antiferromagnetic characteristics for this framework.
The tartrate-based frameworks $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\left(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{tart}\right)$ $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\right.$ or $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{\mathrm{II}}}}$ ; $\mathrm{{L}}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{{tart}}^{2-}=\mathrm{{\Omega}}_{\mathrm{{L}}}$ -tartrate) constitute another series of chiral magnets.351 These frameworks are built up from pseudotetragonal layers of distorted $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra bridged by syn− anti carboxylates. The layers are held together by the backbone of the tartrato ligands to give overall 3D structures. Notably, the chirality of the tartrato ligand imposes all metal centers to adopt a $\Delta$ conformation. For the $\hat{{\bf M}}\mathrm{{n}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ framework, nearestneighbor interactions within the layers are antiferromagnetic, but canting of the spins leads to a net magnetic moment. Accordingly, this compound behaves as a canted antiferromagnet below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=3.3\mathrm{~K~}$ and exhibits a magnetic hysteresis loop with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=450$ Oe at $2\ {\mathrm{K}}.$ The $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{\mathrm{II}}}}$ congener shows a more complicated magnetic behavior, exhibiting three distinct types of ordered states. In particular, it orders antiferromagnetically below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=6~\mathrm{K}$ in low magnetic fields, owing to antiferromagnetic coupling between ferromagnetic layers, and then undergoes a transition to a ferromagnetic-like state below $4.5\mathrm{~K~}$ under an applied field of $>3000$ Oe. This field-dependent magnetic behavior is typical for a metamagnet. In addition, an unusual second magnetic transition to a spin-canted antiferromagnetic phase occurs at lower applied fields.
In contrast to the malate- and tartrate-based frameworks discussed above, which contain dianionic carboxylato ligands with protonated hydroxyl groups, the framework $\mathrm{Co}_{2}^{\mathbb{M}}(\mathrm{O}-\$ $\mathrm{\na})(\bar{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{pyc}){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(\mathrm{O-ma}^{3-^{-}}\right)=2$ -oxidosuccinate; $4{\cdot}\mathrm{pyc}^{-}=4.$ - pyridinecarboxylate) features trianionic malato ligands with deprotonated hydroxyl groups.331 This 3D framework consists of layers of distorted $\bar{\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}\bar{\mathrm{NO}}_{5}$ octahedra and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{5}$ trigonal bipyramids connected by malato ligands through $\mu_{2}$ -alkoxo and $\mu_{2}$ -carboxylato O atoms $(\mathrm{Co^{II}-O-C o^{I I}})$ together with $\mathrm{O-C-}$ O carboxylate bridges. These layers are linked by 4- pyridinecarboxylato ligands to give saddle-like 1D channels (see Figure 31). Upon being heated in vacuum at $200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules can be removed without significant structural changes. Moreover, other guest molecules, including MeOH and ${\mathrm{HCONH}}_{2},$ can be introduced into the framework via a single-crystal-to-single-crystal process to give isostructural compounds.
These frameworks display geometrical frustration due to multiple exchange pathways between adjacent metal centers (see Figure 31, top). Antiferromagnetic interactions, primarily arising from intralayer interactions, are dominant, as evidenced by negative Weiss constants $\mathbf{\eta}(\theta)$ . Nevertheless, interlayer interactions cannot be neglected, and the frameworks ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{O}-\$ ma)(4-pyc) $\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $\mathrm{Co_{\phantom{A}_{2}}^{I I}(\mathrm{\bar{O}-m a)(4-p y c)\cdot M e O H},}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{ma})(4{\cdot}\mathrm{pyc}){\cdot}\mathrm{HCONH}_{2}$ order as canted antiferromagnets with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=8$ , 3.6, and $3.6~\mathrm{K},$ respectively. Note, however, that only $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}\mathrm{(O-ma)}\mathrm{(4-pyc)}{\cdot2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shows clear evidence for a longrange magnetic order. Furthermore, while $\mathbf{Co}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{(O-ma)}(\breve{4}$ - pyc) $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shows a weak spin glass behavior below the ordering temperature, $\mathrm{Co^{I}{}_{2}^{-}\big(O{-}m a\big)\big(4{-}p y c\big){\cdot}M e O H}$ and $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{O}\mathrm{-ma}\big)\big(\dot{4\mathrm{-pyc}}\big){\cdot}\mathrm{HCONH}_{2}$ exhibit metamagnetic behaviors. This discrepancy may be due to more pronounced spin frustration in the latter two compounds. Indeed, the degree of frustration in these systems, as quantified through the parameter $f=\vert\theta\vert/T_{\mathrm{N}},$ increases as the guest is varied from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to MeOH to ${\mathrm{HCONH}}_{2},$ suggesting that different host− guest hydrogen bonding interactions affect geometrical frustration behavior, magnetic exchange, and spin canting in this family of magnetic MOFs.
An example of a framework that contains a mixture of carboxylato ligands with protonated and deprotonated hydroxyl groups is the citrate-based MOF $\mathrm{KCo}_{3}^{\mathrm{a}}(\mathrm{cit})(\mathrm{O}\mathrm{-}$ cit) $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{{(\dot{c}1t^{3-}=}}$ citrate; $\mathrm{O-cit}^{4-}=2$ -oxidopropane1,2,3-tricarboxylate).352 This framework is built up from tetrahedral ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ ions as trigonal nodes and tetranuclear ${\mathrm{Co}}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}$ clusters as octahedral nodes to give a 3D network of (3,6)- connected anatase topology. The magnetic behavior of this framework is sensitive to applied magnetic field and is characteristic of a canted antiferromagnet with a canting angle of $28.9^{\circ}$ . ac susceptibility measurements revealed an onset of a frequency-dependent signal in the plot of ${\chi_{\mathrm{M}}}^{\prime\prime}$ versus $T$ below $5\mathrm{K},$ , but no peak was observed above $2\mathrm{K},$ indicating a short-range order. Nevertheless, a magnet-type behavior was demonstrated by an open magnetic hysteresis loop at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=20$ Oe and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=92~\mathrm{Oe}~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . Remarkably, the magnetic properties for this framework were largely retained after removal of the lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules, and the dehydrated framework was the first MOF to exhibit both microporosity and spin-canted antiferromagnetism.
3.5.7. Compounds Containing Cyclic Multi-Carboxylato Ligands. Frameworks bearing cyclic multicarboxylato ligands can also exhibit interesting magnetic properties. For instance, using camphoric acid as a bridging ligand in conjunction with 1,4-di(1-imidazolylmethyl)benzene (1,4- dimb) afforded a series of chiral isostructural frameworks with the formula $\mathbf{M^{II}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{D-ca}\big)_{2}\big(1,4\mathrm{-dimb}\big)$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}^{I I}=N i^{I I}}$ and/or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $\tt{D-}\tt c a^{2-\tau}=\tt$ D-camphoric acid dianion) that behave as antiferromagnets or canted antiferromagnets with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=8.0-$ 19.5 K.355 These frameworks feature dimeric paddlewheel building units that are connected into undulating (4,4) layers through carboxylato ligands, which are further linked by 1,4- di(1-imidazolylmethyl)benzene molecules to give noninterpenetrating 3D networks. The magnetic properties for the ${\hat{\mathbf{Ni}}_{2}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework are most consistent with canted antiferromagnetic order below $19.5\mathrm{~K~}$ along with spin glass behavior, which was hypothesized to arise from metal ion disorder or defects in the crystal structure. The observed spin canting was attributed to high single-ion anisotropy and Dzyaloshinskii−Moriya antisymmetric exchange interaction that is symmetry-allowed due to the chirality of the structures.225,226 Similar strong intradimer antiferromagnetic interactions are present in the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ congener; however, this compound exhibits long-range antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=8~\mathrm{K}$ and field-induced spinflop behavior under an applied field above ${\sim}3$ T. Furthermore, analogous mixed-metal frameworks demonstrated that the ordering temperature, coercive field, and remanent magnetization for this series of compounds can be tuned through metal ion modulation.355
The squarate dianion is another example of a cyclic dicarboxylate that can be employed to construct magnetic frameworks. The compound $\mathrm{M}\mathrm{\ddot{n}}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{squ})$ $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathsf{s q u}^{2-}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ squarate) consists of chains of hydroxo-bridged edge-sharing triangles of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions in distorted octahedral geometry (see Figure 32, top). The chains are linked by squarate dianions properties were observed.360,361 This discrepancy was speculated to be due to stronger antiferromagnetic interactions between the ferromagnetic ribbons in the hydrated compound, facilitated by hydrogen-bonded $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules.
One example of a squarate-based framework with magnetic properties that are not dominated by $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ superexchange interactions is the compound $\dot{\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\mathrm{sq}\dot{\mathrm{u}})_{1.5},$ which features $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions that are bridged by squarate dianions adopting $\mu_{1,2}$ and $\mu_{1,3}$ binding modes in all three dimensions.362 This framework orders as a canted antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{{N}}}=5.2\ \mathrm{{K}},$ demonstrating that the squarate dianion can mediate strong magnetic interactions between metal centers even in the absence of $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}$ coupling pathways. Note, however, that the magnetic ordering temperatures for the frameworks containing hydroxo-bridged chains are somewhat higher, in agreement with stronger interactions over shorter distances.
In addition to these dicarboxylato-bridged frameworks, one example of an analogous compound with cyclobutanetetracarboxylato linkers has been reported.363 The framework $\mathrm{Co}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\dot{\mathrm{cbut}})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}$ $\hat{\mathbf{\alpha}}_{\mathrm{cbut}}4-\mathbf{\alpha}_{=}1,2,\bar{3},4\cdot$ -cyclobutanetetracarboxylate) contains chains of alternating doubly and triply bridged distorted octahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions through $\mu_{2}$ -carboxylato $\mathrm{~O~}$ atoms, $s y n{\mathrm{-}}s y n\ \mathrm{O-C-O}$ carboxylate groups, and $\mu_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. These chains are linked into layers by a third type of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedron via syn−anti and anti−anti $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges, and the layers are connected through the backbone of the organic ligand into a 3D structure (see Figure 33). The framework exhibits long-range antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{\epsilon}=5.0~\mathrm{K}.}\end{array}$ . Furthermore, it displays metamagnetic behavior, as an application of a magnetic field of $>1500$ Oe is sufficient to overcome weak interlayer antiferromagnetic interactions and give a ferrimagnetic state. Analysis of the magnetic structure by neutron diffraction revealed that significant ferromagnetic interactions are present between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions within the chains, as expected for $\mathsf{C o}^{\mathrm{\tiny{\mathrm{II}}}}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ exchange pathways with angles of $9\bar{6}.11(2)-96.83(2)^{\circ}$ and syn−syn $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges.1,213,214 The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers in the chains are antiferromagnetically coupled to the isolated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions, resulting in uncompensated ferrimagnetic layers. Weak antiferromagnetic interactions between the layers through the long cyclobutanetetracarboxylato ligands $(\sim7.0\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ facilitate the longrange magnetic order.
into a 3D network. $^{356-358}$ This framework displays long-range antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=12.5\ \mathrm{K},$ and its magnetic properties and structure have been deduced from neutron diffraction and muon spin relaxation studies.357,358 The magnetic structure consists of two offset coparallel chains, with the spins aligned antiparallel along each chain. Systems comprised of approximately equilateral triangles are frequently subjected to spin frustration. However, no such frustration was observed for $\mathrm{\dot{M}n}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{squ})$ . This surprising observation was attributed to the slight deviation from ideal equilateral symmetry, as evidenced by the variation in the MnII−O−MnII angles from $96.8\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left(3\aftergroup\egroup\right)^{\circ}$ to $\dot{105}.4(3)^{\circ}$ .
In contrast, the 3D framework $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}\mathrm{(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{(squ)}_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ that consists of brucite-like ribbons formed from similar equilateral triangles (see Figure 32, bottom) displays complicated magnetic behavior attributed to geometrical frustration.359−361 Notably, despite only small structural changes between the hydrated and anhydrous networks, drastic changes in magnetic
3.5.8. Compounds Containing Cyclohexanecarboxylato Ligands. Compounds with linkers based on a cyclohexane core comprise a related class of frameworks. These ligands exhibit greater chemical tunability and less steric strain than those bearing four- and five-membered rings discussed above. Specifically, the cyclohexane core can adopt boat and chair conformations, with up to six carboxylate groups on its backbone able to coordinate metal centers in cis or trans configurations. For instance, trans-1,2-cyclohexanedicarboxylate $(t r a n s\ –1,2–\mathrm{chdc}^{2-})$ has been employed as a ligand to support triangular arrays of $[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}(\dot{\mu}_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{2}]^{4+}$ units featuring ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ tetrahedra and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra and thus afford a Kagomé-like lattice.364 The resulting compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\overline{{t r}}a n s\ –1,2\mathrm{-chdc})_{2}$ displays an inorganic−organic sandwich structure, with the cyclohexane rings decorating the 2D layers that are held together by weak van der Waals interactions. For the reasons outlined above for the hydroxosuccinato networks, this species represents a borderline example of a MOF. Nevertheless, it exhibits the coexistence of spin frustration $\left(f=|\theta/T_{\mathrm{N}}|=7.3\right)$ and long-range spincanted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=11\ \mathrm{K}.$ Distortion of the $[\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}(\mu_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{2}^{-}]^{4+}$ triangles, owing to different types of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ polyhedra and exchange pathways, was posited to weaken the spin frustration expected for an ideal Kagomé lattice system and thus lead to a canted spin structure. In addition, the occurrence of long-range order was attributed to dipolar interactions between the canted inorganic layers.
A framework of dramatically different topology was formed upon treating $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ions with cis-1,4-cyclohexanedicarboxylate $(\mathsf{\bar{c}}i s.1,4\mathrm{-chdc}^{\tilde{2}-},$ ) in the presence of hydroxide ions. The resulting compound $\mathrm{Ni_{3}^{I I}(O H)}_{2}(c i s\mathrm{-}1,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{chdc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\mathrm{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ consists of linear chains of pairs of edge-sharing distorted $\mathrm{Ni^{\mathrm{II}}O}_{6}$ octahedra that are connected through their apexes to distinct distorted $\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ octahedra via $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ and carboxylate groups (see Figure 34, bottom).365 The chains are further connected through syn−syn $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ bridges in a propeller blade fashion to give a 3D network with narrow channels (see Figure 34, top). This framework exhibits guest-dependent magnetic properties, as it can switch from a ferrimagnet with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=2.1\mathrm{~K~}$ to a ferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{C}}=4.5\mathrm{~K~}$ upon partial desolvation. Powder X-ray diffraction analysis suggested subtle structural changes upon dehydration that are not fully reversible upon rehydration, although the magnetic properties are largely restored. The ferrimagnetic behavior of the hydrated framework was explained by ferromagnetic interactions between the edge-sharing $\dot{\mathbf{Ni}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions and weaker antiferromagnetic coupling between corner-sharing $\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centers, affording overall ferrimagnetic chains. At low temperatures, weak through-bond and/or dipolar interchain interactions were postulated to become important and afford a ferrimagnetic ordered state. Note that a framework of similar structure featuring trans-1,4-cyclohexanedicarboxylate (trans1,4-chdc2−) bridging ligands does not exhibit long-range magnetic order above $2\ \mathrm{~K~}$ The difference in magnetic properties between the two frameworks was thought to be due to larger cavities in the framework featuring the trans isomer of the ligand, resulting in weaker interchain interactions.365
Frameworks with related inorganic chain structures were formed when cyclohexane-based bridging ligands bearing three carboxylate groups were employed. In particular, the compound $\bar{\mathrm{Co}}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}\bar{\left(}c i s,c i s{-}1,3,5{-}\bar{\mathrm{chtc}}\right)_{2}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}{\cdot}\bar{5}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (cis,cis$1,3,\bar{5}\mathrm{-chtc^{3-}}=$ cis,cis-1,3,5-cyclohexanetricarboxylate) features chains of similar trimeric structural units as the $\mathrm{\dot{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ framework described above, albeit the chains are arranged in a zigzag rather than linear fashion and bridged by $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules instead of hydroxo groups.366 The value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ for this 3D framework shows an abrupt increase below $4\ \mathrm{K},$ in this case assigned to the onset of ferrimagnetic order. This behavior was attributed to antiferromagnetic interactions within the trimeric subunits, leading to an uncompensated magnetic moment within each chain.
The neutral 3D framework $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}(\mathrm{chhc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}$ ( $\mathrm{\boldmath~\cdot~}\mathrm{chhc}^{6-}= $ trans,trans,trans,trans,trans-1,2,3,4,5,6-cyclohexanehexacarboxylate) features a cyclohexanehexacarboxylato ligand with the carboxylate groups adopting a trans arrangement with one another.367 The framework consists of $\mathbf{\bar{C}o}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions in an octahedral geometry that are connected by $\mu_{1,3}$ -carboxylato ligands each adopting a syn−anti bridging mode. From the viewpoint of magnetic superexchange pathways, the framework can be described as a quasi-regular 3D tetrahedral network. Magnetic studies revealed an antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{\mu}=3.0~\mathrm{K},}\end{array}$ owing to antiferromagnetic interactions between the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers through the syn−anti carboxylate bridges. Moreover, field-dependent measurements indicated that the antiferromagnetic interactions are overcome by an external field above $1.5\ \mathrm{T}\$ , characteristic of a metamagnetic behavior.
Finally, note that all of the foregoing frameworks bearing cyclohexanecarboxylato ligands magnetically order below $12\mathrm{K}.$ These ordering temperatures are much lower than that of a porous layered $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{\hat{II}}}}$ hydroxide framework with trans-1,4- chdc2− ligands linking the layers, which orders as a ferrimagnet below $60.5~\mathrm{K}.^{63}$ This comparison highlights that length, angle, type, and number of superexchange interactions all play a critical role in determining the magnetic coupling strength and thus the ordering temperature of framework materials.
3.5.9. Compounds Containing Benzenecarboxylato Ligands. Benzenepolycarboxylate derivatives have been used extensively in the construction of frameworks for a vast array of applications.368−370 These rigid organic linkers feature delocalized $\pi$ -electrons that can mediate relatively strong magnetic interactions despite the long distance and have therefore been shown to promote magnetic order in a number of cases. Specifically, frameworks bearing linkers comprised of an aromatic core, such as benzene, biphenyl, and terphenyl, functionalized with two to five carboxylate groups have been shown to behave as ordered magnets (see Figure 9).68,353,371−388 These frameworks are homoleptic or feature additional coligands such as oxo, hydroxo, or pyridine derivatives.
The most common member of this family of ligands is 1,4- benzenedicarboxylate $(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc}^{2-})$ . Three $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ frameworks with formulas of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\left(1,4\mathbf{-}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{c}\right)\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)_{2},^{68}~\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}\left(1,4\mathbf{-}\mathbf{\right.}$ $\mathrm{bdc})_{2}\big(\mathrm{DMF}\big)_{2},^{371}$ and ${\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}}_{3}\big(1,4{\mathrm{-b}}\mathrm{d}{\mathrm{c}}\big)_{3}\big(\mathrm{DEF}\big)_{2}^{371}$ exhibit antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=6.5,$ 4.2, and $4.3\mathrm{K},$ respectively. The latter two compounds display considerable spin glass behavior, which can be attributed to strong intrachain coupling and weak interchain interactions. This magnetic behavior can be well understood from considerations of their crystal structures. Namely, the structure of $\mathrm{Mn}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{D}\dot{\mathrm{MF}}\big)_{2}$ is comprised of $\dot{\mathbf{M}}\mathbf{n}_{~4}^{\mathrm{H}}\mathbf{O}_{20}$ tetrameric building units that are linked into chains through $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges. These chains are then connected into a 3D network with 1D channels through the backbone of 1,4-benzenedicarboxylato ligands. The compound $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc}\big)_{3}\big(\mathrm{DEF}\big)_{2}$ features a similar chain-like structure, except the chains consist of $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}O_{6}}}$ octahedra that share both corners and edges.371 Despite the $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}{-}O{-}M n^{I I}}}$ connectivity along the chains, which should strengthen the magnetic interactions, the magnetic ordering temperature for this framework is nearly identical to that of $\mathbf{M}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}_{2}^{\hat{\mathrm{II}}}\big(1,4{\mathrm{-bdc}}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{DMF}\big)_{2}$ . This observation strongly suggests that interchain interactions are responsible for the occurrence of long-range order in these compounds. To compare, the structure of $\mathrm{Mn^{II}}(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ is built up from layers of isolated $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}O_{6}}}$ octahedra that are connected via $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ carboxylate bridges. The layers are further linked through the aromatic backbone of the dicarboxylato ligands.68 Taken together, the low magnetic ordering temperatures for these frameworks illustrate the modest magnetic interactions between $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions mediated via 1,4-benzenedicarboxylato bridging ligands.
In contrast, 3D frameworks constructed from corner-sharing chains of $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ or ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{{\mathrm{III}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra that are linked via $\mathrm{O-C-}$ O bridges through the backbone of 1,4-benzenedicarboxylato ligands display much higher ordering temperatures.372−374 Specifically, the $\mathrm{\DeltaV}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -based framework $\hat{\mathrm{V}}^{\mathrm{II\hat{\mathrm{I}}}}(\mathrm{OH})(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc})$ · $0.75{\left(1.4{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}\right)}$ (MIL-47as) features hydroxo-bridged $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ chains and orders as an antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=95(5)$ K (see Figure 35, left). Indeed, this compound exhibits the highest ordering temperature yet reported for a MOF with exclusively diamagnetic linkers. The large negative Weiss constant of $\theta=-186(4)$ K (for data between 180 and $300~\mathrm{K}$ ) suggests dominant antiferromagnetic interactions at high temperatures.372 This is consistent with a $\mathrm{V^{III}{-}O{-}V^{I I I}}$ superexchange angle of $124.0(2)^{\circ}$ that is far from $180^{\circ}.$ , which would favor strong ferromagnetic interactions according to the Goodenough−Kanamori rules.213,214,389 As such, antiferromagnetic intra- and interchain interactions are anticipated. Notably, when this framework is heated above $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the terephthalic acid guest molecules are removed from the pores and oxidation of $\mathsf{\bar{V}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ to ${\mathrm{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ ions occurs, giving the compound $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}\mathrm{O}(1,4{-}\mathrm{bdc})$ (MIL-47). This framework exhibits a structure very similar to that of MIL-47as, where the vertices in the 1D chains are oxo groups instead of hydroxo ligands (see Figure 35, right). Furthermore, the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{V-O-V}$ superexchange angle of $129.4^{\circ}$ is slighty larger and the 1D channels have a more rectangular shape compared to those in MIL-47as ( $10.5\times11.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ for MIL-47 versus $\mathbf{\hat{\zeta}}_{7.9}\times12.0\mathbf{\hat{A}}^{2}$ for MIL-47as) (see Figure 35). The activated framework displays permanent porosity with Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) and Langmuir surface areas of 930(30) and 1320(2) $\dot{\mathrm{~m~}^{2}}\mathrm{~g^{-1}~};$ , respectively. Moreover, MIL-47 exhibits antiferromagnetism similar to that of MIL-47as, albeit with a lower ordering temperature of 75(5) K that was ascribed to weaker interactions between $\dot{\mathrm{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}\left(\mathrm{d}^{1}\right)$ ions than between $\mathbf{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ $\left(\mathrm{d}^{2}\right)$ ions.372
The compound $\mathrm{Cr^{III}(O H)}(1,4\mathrm{-bdc)}{\cdot}0.75(1.4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc})$ (MIL53as) is isostructural to MIL-47as, with $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions replacing the $\mathrm{\DeltaV}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers. This framework exhibits analogous antiferromagnetic behavior with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=65$ K. Removal of the terephthalic acid guest molecules is accomplished by heating at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{OH})(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc})$ (MIL-53ht). This framework is highly hygroscopic, and a compound with a formula of $\mathrm{Cr^{III}(O H)}\mathrm{\bar{(}}1,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{b\bar{d}c)\cdot H_{2}O}$ (MIL-53lt) is formed when MIL53ht is left under ambient conditions.373,374 All three compounds exhibit the same general 3D structure, but the pore sizes are considerably different, demonstrating the reversible breathing behavior of this framework. Notably, these $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -based networks were the first 3D $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ dicarboxylate frameworks and provided the first microporous $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -based solid (the Langmuir surface area for MIL-53ht is $>1500\mathrm{~m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ). The hydrated framework MIL-53lt shows canted antiferromagnetic behavior with $T_{\mathrm{N}}~=~55~\mathrm{~K~}$ . The different magnetic properties for MIL-53 and MIL-53lt were proposed to arise from the different $\mathrm{Cr^{III}{-}O{-}C r^{I I I}}$ superexchange angles in the two structures $(121.5^{\circ}$ and $124.8^{\circ}$ , respectively).374
Compounds with a formula of $\mathrm{\bfM^{II}}_{2}\big(2,\bar{5}{\cdot}\mathrm{dobdc}\big)\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}.$ xsolvent $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{\mathrm{II}},\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}},\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{\mathrm{II}},{\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}},\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{\mathrm{II}}\right)$ or $\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $^{2,5}$ -dobdc4 $^{=2,5}$ -dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) (MOF-74) represents a family of microporous frameworks that have been extensively studied owing to their excellent performance in gas separation application s.390−394 These frameworks are composed of inorganic chains of edge-sharing $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra that are connected through the backbone of 2,5-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylato ligands to give 3D networks of honeycomb topology with hexagonal 1D channels.375,376,390−394 Both coordinating $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and noncoordinating solvent molecules can be reversibly removed, affording square-pyramidal metal centers with empty coordination sites, suitable for host−guest interactions. For MOF-74 frameworks featuring paramagnetic metal centers, such as $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ , ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , and $\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ , ferromagnetic interactions are present within the chains, but weak antiferromagnetic interchain interactions result in nonmagnetic ground states. Notably, the desolvated $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ framework $\mathsf{\bar{F}e}_{\mathrm{~\tiny~2}}^{\mathrm{II}}(2,5{\cdot}\mathrm{dobdc})$ displays guest-dependent magnetic properties. For instance, adsorption of olefins favors antiparallel alignment of spins within the inorganic chains. However, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{\Pi}}$ -based frameworks do not show any indication of longrange magnetic order.391−394
In contrast, the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2}\big(2,5{\cdot}\mathrm{dobdc}\big)\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{-MOF-}74)$ ) exhibits antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=8$ K. The relatively weak antiferromagnetic interactions between adjacent chains can be overcome upon application of a magnetic field above $2.0\mathrm{~T~}_{\i}$ , resulting in a transition to a ferromagnetic-like ordered state.375 Furthermore, while the ${\bf N i}^{\mathrm{II}}$ congener does not show long-range magnetic order above $2\ \mathrm{K},$ the analoguos mixed-metal $\mathrm{{Ni^{II}\mathrm{{Fe^{III}}}}}$ frameworks display ferrimagnetic long-range order, together with permanent porosity. Specifically, controlled doping with high-spin $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions $\left(\dot{S}=\sqrt[5]{}_{2}\right)$ ) introduces antiferromagnetic interactions into the metal−oxo chains, leading to coexisting antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic intrachain coupling interactions. The ordering temperature for these frameworks can be modulated from 9.5 to $16.7\mathrm{~K~}$ upon varying the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ doping level from ${\sim}20\%$ to ${\sim}2\%$ . These compounds behave as relatively soft ferrimagnets with coercive fields smaller than 1000 Oe at 2 K.376
Frameworks comprised of dimeric tetracarboxylate paddlewheel motifs of the general formula $\mathrm{{M}^{I I}{}_{2}(C O O)_{4}}$ have also been studied extensively.395 In particular, frameworks featuring ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ paddlewheel building blocks have been found to exhibit interesting magnetic properties.377,378,396,397 For instance, the compound $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{\check{u}}_{\mathbf{\Phi}_{2}}^{\mathrm{\check{II}}}\big(1,3\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\hat{b}}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{\hat{c}}\big)_{2}(\mathbf{p}\mathbf{y})_{2}$ $\mathit{\Omega}^{'}1,3\ –\mathrm{bdc}^{2-}=1,3$ -benzenedicarboxylate) is comprised of bowl-shaped triangular secondary building units, where ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ dimers are positioned at the lattice points and are connected by 1,3-benzenedicarboxylato ligands into a Kagomé-type 2D network with hexagonal channels (see Figure 36).377 It is well-known that strong antiferromagnetic coupling between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centers within the paddlewheel units results in overall diamagnetic $S=0$ dimers.327,398 As such, the observation of an open hysteresis loop at $5\mathrm{~K~}$ and apparent remanent magnetization at room temperature for the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\big(1,3{\cdot}\mathrm{{bdc}}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{py}\big)_{2}$ framework were surprising. Initially, the ferromagnetic-like behavior of this compound was attributed to a canted spin structure arising from the geometrically frustrated antiferromagnetic state.377 However, this proposition was later questioned because a net spin is needed on each node to cause spin frustration, but the ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ dimers do not possess a net spin.
More recently, theoretical calculations have indicated that the spontaneous magnetization in this and related $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{\bar{C}O O})_{4}$ -based frameworks is caused by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ vacancy point defects.378 Specifically, the presence of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ vacancies breaks the antiferromagnetic state in the ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ dimers and creates quasi-localized spin states that interact ferromagnetically through itinerant $\pi$ electrons in the conjugated aromatic ligands. Further analysis of the magnetic structure, for example, through neutron diffraction, is needed to verify the origin of the ferromagnetic-like behavior of this ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework at low temperatures.
Similarly, more detailed magnetic measurements are required to establish the presence and nature of long-range magnetic order for the framework ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(1,3,5{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{btc}})_{2}{\big(}\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}{\big)}_{3}$ $(1,3,5{\cdot}\mathrm{btc}^{3-}=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylate) (HKUST1).396,397 This compound is comprised of analogous $\mathrm{Cu}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ paddlewheel building blocks as ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ (1,3- $\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{py})_{2},$ and preliminary magnetic studies suggested weak ferromagnetic interactions between the antiferromagnetically coupled ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ dimers.397 In contrast, a well-defined open magnetic hysteresis loop was observed at $5\mathrm{~K~}$ for the related compound $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bptc})\mathsf{\bar{(H_{2}O)}}_{3}(\mathrm{DMF})_{3}$ $(\mathrm{bptc}^{4-}~=~3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}.$ biphenyltetracarboxylate) (MOF-505), indicative of a ferromagnetic-like ordered state. Fits of the high-temperature dc magnetic susceptibility data to the Curie−Weiss law provided an estimate of $\mathrm{\hat{T}_{C}}=\mathrm{i}\mathrm{\hat{1}~K.}^{378}$ Whether the long-range order in MOF-505 originates from spin canting or ferromagnetic interactions between point defects is still not clear. Notably, the two phenyl rings and four carboxylate groups of each tetracarboxylato bridging ligand are coplanar to one another, providing a highly conjugated $\pi$ -system able to mediate significant magnetic communication between metal centers.399
Other examples of framework magnets featuring the 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylato bridging ligand are the isostructural c4o5am) aund $\mathrm{K}\big[\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{2.25}\mathrm{Fe^{II}}_{0.75}\big(1,3,5\mathrm{-btc}\big)\big(1,3,5\mathrm{-Hbtc}\big)_{2}\big]\cdot\mathrm{SH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ${\mathrm{K}}[\dot{\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}},3,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{btc})(\mathrm{1},3,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Hbtc})_{2}]{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (MIL-45b). These frameworks are comprised of undulating chains of edge- and corner-sharing $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\dot{\mathbf{O}_{6}}$ octahedra that are linked into 3D networks through the backbone of carboxylato ligands in different protonation states. Magnetic measurements revealed that both compounds behave as ferromagnets below $T_{\mathrm{C}}=10$ and $20\mathrm{~K~}$ for MIL-45a and MIL-45b, respectively, owing to ferromagnetic intra- and interchain interactions.379
Several frameworks containing the tetracarboxylato linker 1,2,4,5-benzenetetracarboxylate (1,2,4,5-btec4−) have been found to exhibit long-range magnetic order. $380-386$ For example. the homoleptic frameworks $\mathbf{M}_{\ 2}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(1,2,4,5{\cdot}\mathrm{btec}\big)$ $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\right.$ $\bf\Pi=\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{\tiny{~II}}},\epsilon\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},$ , or $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}^{\mathrm{{i}}}}$ ) are isostructural and consist of zigzag chains of edge-sharing $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra that are bridged into layers via $_{\mathrm{O-C-O}}$ linkages, and the layers are further connected through the backbone of the aromatic ligand to give the 3D networks.380−382 Both $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ frameworks behave as antiferromagnets with $T_{\mathrm{{N}}}~=~18$ and $26~\mathrm{~K~},$ respectively, where antiferromagnetic interactions are present between antiferromagnetic layers.381 On the other hand, the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework shows a complicated magnetic behavior that has been the subject of controversy.382 Specifically, an early report states that $\mathbf{Co}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(1,2,4,5{\cdot}\mathrm{biec}\big)$ exhibits long-range collinear antiferromagnetic order at $16~\mathrm{K},$ becomes a canted antiferromagnet below $12\mathrm{~K~}$ under small applied magnetic fields, and undergoes a field-induced transition to a ferromagnetic state at higher fields $\left(H>1500\mathrm{~Oe}\right)$ .381 More recent neutron diffraction studies confirm the presence of longrange antiferromagnetic order and field-induced ferromagnetic behavior; however, the origin of the weak ferromagntism, observed at low temperatures and small magnetic fields, remains unknown.382 The initial explanation in terms of spin canting was questioned because it can be rationalized by neither antisymmetric exchange225,226 nor the difference in anisotropy of the magnetic centers due to the high crystal symmetry and only one crystallographically independent $\mathrm{\dot{C}o^{I I}}$ site in the structure. An alternative explanation is based on the presence of stacking faults in the antiferromagnetic alignment of ferromagnetic layers, which produce a net macroscopic ferromagnetic component.382 Note that in this family of compounds, the $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathbf{O}{-}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ superexchange angles are ${\sim}104^{\circ}$ , which can lead to either ferromagnetic $\mathsf{\check{(}C o^{\mathrm{II}})}$ or antiferromagnetic (MnII and FeII) intralayer interactions.213,214
Related frameworks containing 1D inorganic subnetworks are the ferric carboxylate framework $\breve{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{OH})(1,2,4,5-$ $_{\mathrm{H_{2}b t e c}}){\cdot}0.88\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}82)^{383}$ and the $C o^{\mathrm{{II}}}.$ -based framework $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{5}\mathrm{(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{(1},2,4,5\mathrm{-btec)}_{2}\mathrm{(bpp)}$ ) [ $\cdot1,2,4,5\cdot\mathrm{btec}^{4-}=1,2,4,5\cdot$ benzenetetracarboxylate; $\mathrm{\bpp=1,3\mathrm{-}b i s(4\mathrm{-}p y r i d y l)p r o p a n e].}$ 384 The 3D structure of MIL-82 consists of chains of cornersharing $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ octahedra that are connected via trans-2,5- carboxylate groups of the organic ligand, whereas the carboxyl functional groups at positions 1 and 4 on the aromatic ring remain protonated and do not participate in covalent bonding within the framework.383 However, despite the structure of MIL-82 being topologically related to those of MIL-47 $\left(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}\right)$ and MIL-53 $({\dot{\mathbf{Cr}}}^{\mathrm{III}})$ discussed above, $372-374$ it does not undergo a reversible breathing effect during the dehydration/ hydration process. This discrepancy was attributed to strong hydrogen bonds between the dangling carboxylic acid groups in MIL-82, which keep the structure intact up to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, MIL-82 undergoes long-range antiferromagnetic order at a temperature of $T_{\mathrm{N}}=5.5(1\big)\ \mathrm{K}^{383}$ that is much lower than those of the related $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ frameworks. Because the $\mathrm{Fe^{III}-O-F e^{I I I}}$ superexchange angle of $126.59(7)^{\circ}$ is similar to those observed in MIL-47 and MIL-53, it is not obvious why the ordering temperature for MIL-82 is an order of magnitude lower than for the other two frameworks. One plausible explanation is that the electronic communication through the partially protonated $1,2,4,5{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{btec}^{2-}$ bridging ligands is weaker than via $\scriptstyle1,4-b d c^{2-}$ owing to the significant electron density present on the dangling carboxylic acid moieties.
The chain structure of $\mathrm{Co^{Ii}}_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(1,2,4,5{\cdot}\mathrm{btec})_{2}(\mathrm{bpp})$ is significantly more complex, as it features both $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ and $\mu_{1,1^{-}}$ carboxylate bridges.384 The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{-O}}$ backbone is comprised of edge-sharing octahedral trinuclear clusters that share corners with two distorted ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{5}$ square pyramids. The chains are connected into a 3D network via $^{1,2,4,5}$ -benzenetetracarboxylato ligands adopting two different coordination modes, and bowed 1,3-bis(4-pyridyl)propane ligands provide further structural support (see Figure 37, left). Owing to multiple types of nearest-neighbor magnetic exchange pathways among three crystallographically distinct ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers in the chains, i.e., via hydroxo, $\mu_{1,1}^{}$ -carboxylate, and syn−syn $\mu_{1,3}$ -carboxylate bridges, coexisting ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic interactions are present, leading to overall ferrimagnetic-like behavior within the magnetic chains (see Figure 37, right). Upon application of magnetic fields below 3500 Oe, a transition to an antiferromagnetically ordered state was observed with $T_{\mathrm{N}}~=~12.5$ K. At higher fields, the weak antiferromagnetic interactions between the ferrimagnetic chains can be overcome to afford a ferrimagnetic-like ordered state.
In addition to the frameworks comprised of 1D inorganic subnetworks and 1,2,4,5-benzenetetracarboxylato bridging ligands, a few framework magnets without $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}$ linkages have been reported.385,386 In particular, the 3D frameworks $\mathrm{Ni^{II}}_{2}\big(1,2,4,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{btec}\big)\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{4}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\hat{O}}^{385}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(1,2,4,5\cdot$ btec)- $\mathrm{(hypH)}_{0.5}\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}_{0.5}{\cdot}1.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(hypH=}$ hypoxanthine)386 exhibit long-range antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\ 3\mathrm{~K~}$ and $T_{\mathrm{C}}~=~4.5~\mathrm{K},$ respectively. Notably, the latter compound represents the first MOF with nucleobase ligands that exhibits long-range magnetic order.386
One framework with benzenepentacarboxylato linkers has been shown to display long-range magnetic order.387 Indeed, this ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -based compound with a formula of ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{bpc}).$ - $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{3}$ $(\mathsf{b p c}^{5-}\:=\:$ benzenepentacarboxylate) was one of the first coordination solids featuring this ligand. The 3D structure is comprised of distorted ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{{O}}}_{6}$ octahedra that are arranged into ${\dot{\mathbf{Co}_{\mathbf{\theta6}}^{\mathrm{II}}}}$ secondary building units (see Figure 38, top), which are bridged by pairs of carboxylate groups into 1D tapes. These 1D motifs are further connected along the other two directions through the pentacarboxylato ligands. Notably, multiple exchange pathways are present within and between the ${\dot{\mathbf{Co}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{II}}}}$ building units, $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}},$ $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{;}}$ , $\mu_{1,1}$ -carboxylate, and three types of $\mu_{1,3}$ -carboxylate bridges (see Figure 38, top), resulting in a complicated magnetic structure. Magnetic measurements revealed long-range antiferromagnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=3.8\ \mathrm{K}$ with some weak ferromagnetic-like contribution, attributed to either spin canting or competing antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic interactions within the 1D tapes. Furthermore, a metamagnetic transition was observed below $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ .
Two $\check{C}\circ^{\mathrm{II}}$ -based framework compounds, $\left(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{NH}\right)$ - $\left[{\bf C o}_{1.5}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{hfpbb})_{2}\right]$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}\mathrm{(hfpbb)}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\hat{P}h N}$ e $\begin{array}{r l}{[\mathbf{h}\mathbf{f}\mathbf{p}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{b}^{2-}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ hexafluoroisopropylidenebis(benzoate)], contain the $\mathrm{v}$ -shaped dicarboxylato ligand hexafluoroisopropylidenebis(benzoate).388 The structure of $\big(\mathrm{Et_{3}N H}\big)\big[\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{1.5}(\mathrm{hfpbb})_{2}\big]$ is built up from linear trimeric clusters of $\mathrm{Co^{II}O_{5}{-}C o^{I I}O_{6}{-}}$ ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{5}$ polyhedra that are bridged by $\mathrm{\hf{pb}}\mathsf{b}^{2-}$ into a 2D framework of square lattice topology with $(\bar{\mathrm{Et}}_{3}\mathrm{NH})^{+}$ counterions in the voids of the network. In contrast, the neutral compound $\mathrm{Co}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{hfpbb})_{2}$ ·PhMe displays a 3D structure comprised of zigzag chains of edge-sharing ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedra that are connected in the other two dimensions through the backbone of the dicarboxylato ligands. Despite the drastically different structures of the two compounds, both exhibit longrange canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=7{-}8\ \mathrm{K}.$ The origin of the ordered magnetic states was attributed to weak antiferromagnetic interactions between ferromagnetic subunitslinear trimeric clusters for the 2D framework and zigzag chains for the 3D frameworkwhere the ordered magnetic moments are slightly canted. For both compounds, the long-range magnetic order is destroyed upon application of a sufficiently high magnetic field. In particular, the intercluster interactions in $\mathbf{\check{(Et_{3}N H)}}[{\mathbf{Co}_{1.5}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathbf{hfpb}\mathbf{\bar{b}})_{2}}]$ are very weak owing to the long distance between them $\left(12.077{-}13.052\mathrm{~\AA}\right)$ , and a magnetic field of $1000\ \mathrm{Oe}$ is sufficient to preclude magnetic order.
Finally, a framework that is bridged by a sulfur-containing carboxylato ligand and exhibits long-range magnetic order is the layered compound ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}$ (thiosal) (thiosa $\begin{array}{r l r}{\lefteqn{|2^{\asymp}-}}&{{}}&{}\\ {\quad}&{{}}&{=}\end{array}$ thiosalicylate).353 This 2D framework features $\dot{\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions in a trigonal bipyramidal $S_{2}O_{3}$ coordination environment that are bridged by thiosal2− ligands (see Figure 38, bottom), with the phenyl groups protruding into the interlayer space. Four types of magnetic exchange pathways are present within the layers: ${\bf C}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathbf{0}}}^{\sharp}\mathrm{-}{\cal S}\mathrm{-}{\bf C}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathbf{0}}}^{\mathrm{II}},$ ， ${\bf C}{\bf{0}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{-}}{\mathrm{{\dot{O}}}}\mathrm{{-}}{\bf C}{\bf{0}}^{\mathrm{{Ii}}}.$ , and syn−syn and syn−anti $\dot{\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}-$ $\mathrm{O-C-O-Co^{II}}$ (see Figure 38, bottom). This framework exhibits strong antiferromagnetic coupling between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions at high temperatures, as indicated by a remarkably large negative value for the Weiss constant of $\theta=-585$ (3) K (from data above $100~\mathrm{~K~}$ . Moreover, it displays long-range canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=9~\mathrm{K}.$ Note that the observed weak ferromagnetic behavior was assigned to canted antiferromagnetism rather than ferrimagnetism because of only one crystallographically unique $\check{\mathbf{C}}\circ^{\mathrm{II}}$ center in the structure.
In summary, most framework magnets containing carboxylato bridging ligands display magnetic ordering temperatures below $25\mathrm{~K~}$ The current record holder for this class of compounds is the $\mathrm{\DeltaV}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -based framework MIL-47 with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=$ 95(5) K.372 This compound features an inorganic chain structure, as detailed above. Accordingly, increasing the inorganic dimensionality does not necessarily afford frameworks with higher $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ values. This may seem counterintuitive, as the strength of magnetic superexchange is typically greatest across short oxo or hydroxo bridges. However, frameworks of higher inorganic dimensionality $\breve{(}\mathrm{I}^{2}\mathrm{O}^{0},\mathrm{I}^{2}\mathrm{O}^{1},$ and ${\mathrm{I}}^{3}{\mathrm{O}}^{\circ}.$ ) often feature multiple types of superexchange pathways that may facilitate interactions of different signs and thus decrease the strength of the overall metal−metal interactions. Furthermore, those frameworks usually suffer from the lack of conjugated $\pi$ - systems that aid in establishing a long-range magnetic order.
## 3.6. Compounds Containing N-Heterocyclic Ligands
3.6.1. Introduction. Ligands based on N-heterocycles have been frequently employed in the construction of framework materials.400 These aromatic N-donor ligands can be classified according to their charge into neutral or anionic linkers. Neutral donors include pyrazine, pyridine, pyrimidine, and imidazole derivatives, whereas imidazolate, triazolate, and tetrazolate are examples of anionic ligands (see Figure 10). In addition to linking magnetic oxo-, hydroxo-, phosphonato-, or carboxylato-bridged layers in magnetic materials,70,71,230,348,349,355 N-heterocycles can also serve as primary ligands in the construction of framework magnets owing to the multiple relatively short and efficient magnetic exchange pathways between metal centers that are possible through these $\pi$ -conjugated ligands. For instance, azolate moieties may afford exchange pathways via two-atom ${\mathrm{N-N}}_{i}$ , three-atom $\mathrm{N-}$ $\mathrm{C-N},$ , and three-atom ${\mathrm{N-N-N}}$ bridges (see Figure 6). Due to the small size of these N-heterocycles, they are frequently connected by spacers of variable lengths to facilitate the formation of frameworks with higher porosity. Such assembly of two or more donor groups enables an additional control of ligand field strength and metal coordination geometry and, thus, the magnetic properties of the resulting framework materials. In this section, we survey framework magnets constructed from one or two types of N-heterocyclic ligands (see Figure 10), where these ligands provide connectivity in at least two dimensions.
3.6.2. 2D Frameworks Containing N-Heterocyclic Ligands. Following an extensive search for multiatom bridges to efficiently transmit magnetic interactions between paramagnetic metal centers, several 2D layered frameworks of $\mathbf{\hat{M}n}^{\mathrm{II}},$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{I\tilde{I}}}.$ , and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ with N-heterocyclic linkers were found to display long-range magnetic order.219,401−403 These frameworks consist of parallel square-grid layers of divalent transition metal ions that are bridged by bis(monodentate) N-heterocycle-based ligands such as pyrazine (pyz), pyrazine-1,4- dioxide (pzdo), and pyrimidine (pym). Each metal center resides in a compressed octahedral geometry with two $(\mathsf{N C S})^{-}$ ligands coordinating at the axial sites. Magnetic susceptibility measurements for the compound $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{pyz})_{2}^{\smile}(\mathrm{NCS})_{2}$ suggested the onset of antiferromagnetic order below ${\sim}9\mathrm{K},$ and neutron powder diffraction enabled the observation of a 3D long-range order below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=6.8\mathrm{~K~}$ Nevertheless, even in the ordered phase, the magnetic behavior of this compound is primarily 2D in character owing to the very weak dipolar interactions between the magnetic pyrazine-bridged layers.401,402 The analogous 2D frameworks $\mathrm{\bf\ddot{M}^{I I}(p z d o)}_{2}(\mathrm{\bf\bar{N}C S)}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}^{I I}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}},$ ) show similar magnetic properties characteristic of antiferromagnets, with $T_{\mathrm{N}}~{=}~8.4$ and $11.2\mathrm{~K~}$ for ${\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}={\mathbf{M}}{\mathbf{n}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}};$ , respectively.403 The slightly higher ordering temperatures for these frameworks compared to the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework with pyrazine bridging ligands may stem from the presence of weak hydrogen bonds between pyrazine-1,4-dioxide molecules in adjacent layers, which facilitates interlayer exchange interactions.
The antiferromagnetic behavior observed for the frameworks bridged by pyrazine derivatives can be explained by the topological symmetry of the conjugated $\pi$ -electron network for tnhisesme. 1s9y−st2e24ms,On stoh  nbaoswis af thihs sxpcihna-pnogle ipzatthiowna maelcohnae-, magnetic coupling between metal centers across the parasubstituted $_{\mathrm{N-C-C-N}}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-N-C-C-N-O}$ bridges in pyrazine and pyrazine-1,4-dioxide, respectively, should be antiferromagnetic (see Figure 39, top and center). In contrast, interactions through the meta-substituted $_{\mathrm{N-C-N}}$ bridge in pyrimidine should be ferromagnetic (see Figure 39, bottom). Along these lines, the 2D ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}^{\vee}}$ framework $\mathrm{C}\mathsf{\breve{o}}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{pym})_{2}(\mathrm{NCS})_{2},$ which features the same layered structure as described above, exhibits ferromagnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{C}}~=~8.2~\mathrm{K}.^{219}$ This compound behaves as a soft magnet, as evidenced from a magnetic hysteresis at $2.3\mathrm{~K~}$ with a small coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=$ $120\mathrm{~Oe~}$ and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=0.25~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}~\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ .
Recently, another class of 2D frameworks featuring Nheterocyclic bridging ligands was developed. Specifically, the family of isostructural layered coordination polymers Fe(R$\left.\mathrm{bim}\right)_{2}^{\cdot}$ $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{bim}^{-}=5$ -substituted benzimidazolate; $\mathrm{R}=\mathrm{Cl}_{\cdot}$ Br, $\mathrm{H},$ Me, or $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ) featuring distorted tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers connected by bis(monodentate) $\mathrm{{R}}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{{bim}}^{-}$ ligands was shown to exhibit long-range canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=$ $20.0{-}21.2\ \mathrm{~K}.^{404}$ While changing the substituent at position 5 on the benzimidazolate ring had an only modest effect on $T_{\mathrm{N}},$ the surface properties (hydrophobicity versus hydrophilicity) were dramatically altered. Furthermore, the weak interlayer interactions through van der Waals forces permitted micromechanical exfoliation of these compounds down to the monolayer, while preserving crystallinity, rendering them good candidates to investigate magnetism in the 2D limit.
3.6.3. Pyrimidinolate-Containing 3D Frameworks. A number of frameworks based on deprotonated hydroxopyrimidine ligands, also known as pyrimidinolates, have been reported.405−412 Specifically, 3D frameworks featuring 2- pyrimidinolate derivatives have been shown to display longrange magnetic order $409-412$ and guest-dependent magnetic behavior.409,410 Moreover, remote ligand substitution can be employed to tune the magnetic ordering temperature for these systems.412 The compound ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (F-pymo− $=5$ -fluoro-2-pyrimidinolate) is comprised of distorted $\mathrm{\bar{C}o^{I I}N_{4}}$ tetrahedra linked by $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -exo-bidentate 5-fluoro-2-pyrimidinolato ligands into a 3D framework with sodalite topology. The fluoro and oxo substituents alternatively protrude into the cavities of the framework, and the remaining space is occupied by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules (see Figure 40, left).409 Upon heating at 92 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules can be reversibly removed without significant structural changes; however, further heating leads to an irreversible formation of a layered structure.
Antiferromagnetic nearest-neighbor interactions dominate in these compounds, and the hydrated 3D framework exhibits long-range antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{{N}}}~=~29~\mathrm{{K}}.$ In contrast, the dehydrated layered phase shows hidden canted antiferromagnetic behavior below ${\sim}17\mathrm{~K~}$ The variation in magnetic properties upon desolvation was attributed to changes in the coordination environment at ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ . In addition to the modulation of the magnetic properties for this compound by thermal treatment, the anhydrous 3D framework exhibits selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{N}_{2},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH_{4}}\mathrm{^{40}}$ 9 indicating that this and related systems may be attractive targets for multifunctional magnetic materials.
A series of isostructural 3D frameworks with an acentric diamond-like topology are formed when the fluoro-substituted pyrimidinolato ligand is replaced by other 5-substituted halopyrimidinolates or the unsubstituted ligand. Specifically, the compounds with a formula of $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{R}{\cdot}\mathrm{pymo})_{2}$ ( $\mathbf{\tilde{R}}=\mathbf{H},$ , Cl, Br, or $:{\mathrm{R}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{pymo}}^{-}=2$ -pyrimidinolate or 5-halo-2-pyrimidinolate)411,412 feature $\mathrm{Co}^{\hat{\mathrm{II}}}$ ions in a tetrahedral coordination environment similar to that of the 3D frameworks with Fpymo− linkers discussed above. Nevertheless, the diamond-like frameworks exhibit weak ferromagnetism below the ordering temperatures of $T_{\mathrm{N}}=19{-}22.5\mathrm{~K~}$ attributed to spin canting. The presence of a canted spin structure in these compounds was posited to be a synergistic effect of the acentric crystal structure, structural distortions, and high magnetic anisotropy of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . Notably, the magnetic ordering temperature is highest for $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{pi}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{\bf{o}})_{2}$ and decreases with a decrease in electronegativity of the halo substituent. However, the derivative bearing the unsubstituted ligands (H-pymo−) does not fit this trend, as it orders below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=22~\mathrm{K}.$ Furthermore, the coercive fields obtained from magnetic hysteresis studies at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ decrease in the following order: $\mathrm{Br}>\mathrm{Cl}>\mathrm{I}>\mathrm{H},$ , and the remanent magnetization decreases in the following order: $\operatorname{Br}\approx$ $\mathrm{Cl}>\mathrm{I}>\mathrm{H}$ . While the lack of a coherent trend for the magnetic behavior in this series of compounds precludes a definitive magnetostructural analysis,412 this study nevertheless demonstrated that subtle changes in the steric and electronic properties of linkers can be employed to modulate the magnetic properties for framework materials.
By comparison, the hydrated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework $\mathrm{{Cu}^{I I}(F_{\overline{{\mathbf{\Pi}}}}}$ pymo) $_{2}{\cdot}1.25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\begin{array}{l l l}{{({\mathrm{F}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{pymo}}^{-}}}&{{=}}&{{5}}\end{array}$ -fluoro-2-pyrimidinolate) exhibits a 3D structure of gismondine topology with ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{8}^{\mathrm{II}}$ structural motifs (see Figure 40, center) instead of the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}_{6}^{\mathrm{II}}$ rings found in $\bar{\mathrm{{Co}}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{{F}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{pymo}})_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=0$ or 2.5).410 This framework also displays a reversible dehydration/hydration process while retaining the integrity of the structure. Magnetic measurements revealed antiferromagnetic coupling between square planar ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centers through the $_{\mathrm{N-C-N}}$ bridges of F-pymo−. However, the noncentrosymmetry of the crystal structure allowed the presence of an antisymmetric effect, resulting in canting of the spins. As such, $\mathrm{Cu^{II}}\mathrm{(F\mathrm{-}p y m o)}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}1.25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ displays long-range canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=24\ \mathrm{K},$ in analogy to the majority of $\mathbf{\bar{C}o}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ frameworks featuring 2-pyrimidinolato ligands. The magnetic properties for the dehydrated framework are very similar to those observed for the hydrated compound, but interestingly, incorporation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules in the pores increases the ordering temperature from $T_{\mathrm{N}}=22$ to $29\mathrm{K}$ (see Figure 40, right). The uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ results in structural perturbation of the framework, as evidenced by PXRD analysis, which was presumed to lead to a less distorted square planar coordination environment around the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites and thus provide greater overlap between the magnetic ${\mathrm{d}}_{x^{'}-y^{'}}$ orbital of $\mathbf{\bar{C}}\mathbf{u}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ and the orbitals of F-pymo−.410 Notably, this type of guest-modulated magnetic property induced by gas molecules is exceedingly rare for framework materials.
In summary, the 3D frameworks discussed in this subsection illustrate that magnetic interactions through the $_{\mathrm{N-C-N}}$ linkage of 2-pyrimidinolato ligands are antiferromagnetic in nature, which is in contrast to the ferromagnetic interactions expected for pyrimidine-based ligands considering the spinpolarization mechanism depicted in Figure 39. Note, however, that the spin-polarization mechanism is based on $\pi$ -exchange interactions only, but both $\sigma^{-}$ and $\pi$ -orbitals of N-heterocyclebased ligands may be capable of mediating magnetic coupling between metal centers. As such, the presence of a negatively charged O atom midway in the exchange pathway may favor $\sigma\mathrm{.}$ - type exchange interactions and thus lead to antiferromagnetic coupling between metal centers.
3.6.4. Imidazolate-Containing 3D Frameworks. Over the past several decades, a number of imidazolate-bridged dinuclear ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes and chain compounds have been shown to exhibit strong antiferromagnetic exchange coupling through the $_{\mathrm{N-C-N}}$ linkage.413−416 However, because the imidazolato ligand displays an $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -exo-bidentate binding mode toward metal centers similar to that of pyrimidine derivatives, one might expect favorable ferromagnetic interactions based on a $\pi$ -type exchange pathwa y.219−224 Indeed, heterobimetallic complexes and networks have demonstrated ferromagnetic exchange between metal centers through the imidazolate bridge.417,418 As such, the electronic configuration and geometry of the metal ions, and the symmetry of the magnetic orbitals, all play an important role in determining the type of magnetic exchange through imidazolato ligands.413−419
Among the first 3D imidazolate framework compounds reported to exhibit long-range magnetic order was $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}^{\mathrm{\tiny{\mathrm{~II}}}}(\mathrm{im})_{6}(\mathrm{im}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{im}^{-}\mathrm{~=~}\right.$ imidazolate).420 The structure of this framework is comprised of imidazolato-bridged chains of tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions that are cross-linked through octahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions (see Figure 41, left). The compound behaves as a soft canted antiferromagnet below $T_{\mathrm{{N}}}~=~17~\mathrm{{K}},$ and magnetic hysteresis measurements at $4.8~\mathrm{K}$ show a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=$ 200 Oe and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=2500$ Oe $\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ . The strongest antiferromagnetic nearest-neighbor interactions are present between the tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers within the chains, and the canted spin structure is believed to originate from different relative orientations of the metal centers along the chains.
Following this pioneering study, the effects of incorporating different substituents onto the imidazolate ring on the magnetic properties of this class of compounds were investigated.421,422 Specifically, introduction of a methyl group at position 2 of the imidazolate ring afforded the compound $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{Meim})_{2}{\cdot}0.13\mathrm{Cp}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ $\scriptstyle2-\mathbf{Meim}^{-}=2$ -methylimidazolate; $\mathrm{Cp^{-}~=~}$ cyclopentadienyl anion).421 This compound features exclusively tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers (see Figure 41, right) and exhibits long-range canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=27\ \mathrm{K},$ which is currently the highest ordering temperature reported for a framework that is exclusively bridged by imidazolato ligands. Furthermore, this framework is a significantly harder magnet than $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{im})_{6}(\mathrm{imH})_{2},$ as evidenced by a 25-fold enhancement in the coercive field at $4.8~\mathrm{K},$ from $H_{\mathrm{c}}=200$ Oe for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{im})_{6}(\mathrm{imH})_{2}$ to $H_{\mathrm{c}}=5000$ Oe for $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(2{\cdot}\mathrm{Meim})_{2}{\cdot}0.13\mathrm{Cp}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . In contrast, the use of a 4- azabenzimidazolato bridging ligand afforded a $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework with magnetic properties similar to those of the framework bearing the unsubstituted imidazolato linker.422
A series of homoleptic 3D imidazolate frameworks featuring tetrahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers have also been shown to exhibit longrange magnetic order at low temperatures.423,424 In particular, various polymorphous frameworks of the general formula $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathrm{im})_{2}$ ·xsolvent $\mathbf{\bar{i}m}^{-}=$ imidazolate) have been synthesized under different solvothermal conditions to give desolvated and solvated 3D frameworks of different network topologies. These compounds order as antiferromagnets or canted antiferromagnets with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=10.6\mathrm{-}16.8\ \mathrm{K},$ highlighting that the magnetic properties for this family of compounds are significantly affected by the nature of the guest molecules in the pores as well as the topological arrangement of metal ions in the structures. To illustrate, replacing pyridine guest molecules with cyclohexanol molecules resulted in a change from an antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=13.1~\mathrm{K}$ to a canted antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=\overline{{1}}5\mathrm{~K}^{423}$ Furthermore, two frameworks with the same formula, $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{im})_{2}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{DMA}$ , display different zeolitic structures with magnetic ordering temperatures differing by 4.8 K.424
3.6.5. Tri- and Tetrazolate-Containing 3D Frameworks. In contrast to the pyrazine, pyrimidine, and imidazolate derivatives outlined above, triazolate- and tetrazolate-based ligands frequently link three or more metal centers through various bridging modes. As such, these ligands can furnish the formation of MOFs with unique structures and magnetic properties. Along these lines, several frameworks featuring triazolato and tetrazolato ligands have displayed longrange magnetic order.425−429 Most of these compounds comprise $\check{\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ frameworks featuring inorganic coligands, such as $(\bar{\mathrm{OH}})^{-}$ , $|\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\rrangle$ , $(\mathbf{N}_{3})^{-}.$ , $(\mathrm{SO}_{4})^{2-},$ and $(\bar{\mathrm{VO}_{3}})^{-}.$ , resulting in the formation of various types of inorganic subunits.425,427−429 Here, antiferromagnetic exchange interactions between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers dominate, affording compounds with long-range antiferromagnetic,425 ferrimagnetic,428,429 or canted antiferromagnetic427,429 order below ${\sim}20~\mathrm{K}$ Of these compounds, the first azido−metal−triazolate coordination polymer $\mathsf{C u}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\mathsf{t r z})$ - $\left(\mathbf{N}_{3}\right)$ $\mathbf{\bar{\Psi}}_{\mathbf{{tr}}\mathbf{{z}}}-\mathbf{\Psi}_{\mathbf{{}}}=\mathbf{\Psi}_{1,2,4}$ -triazolate) exhibits the highest magnetic ordering temperature of $\sim20\mathrm{~K},$ based on the bifurcation temperature of zero-field-cooled and field-cooled dc magnetization curves.427 The structure of this framework is comprised of chains of distorted square-pyramidal ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions bridged by $\mu_{1,2}–\mathrm{tr}\mathbf{z}^{-}$ and end-on azido ligands (see Figure 42). The chains are connected in the other two dimensions through the third N atom on the $\mathrm{{trz}^{-}}$ ligands to generate a 3D framework of (10,3)- $d$ topology. This compound exhibits field-dependent magnetic properties and a hysteresis loop at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}\approx200$ Oe and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=0.0028\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ , indicating weak ferromagnetism. This behavior was attributed to the presence of a canted spin structure arising from tilting of the ${\mathrm{{\hat{C}}}}\mathrm{u}^{\mathrm{II}}$ spins in the chains. The main magnetic exchange coupling pathways in this framework are through the end-on azido $\mathrm{\dot{(}C u^{\mathrm{{II}}}-N-C\dot{u}^{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ and $\mu_{1,2}\mathrm{-trz^{-}\left(C u^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{-N\mathrm{-N\mathrm{-}C u^{\mathrm{II}}}}\right)}$ bridges within the chains. Note that magnetic coupling via endon azido bridges is typically ferromagnetic; however, antiferromagnetic interactions can be favored when the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}{\mathrm{N-}}{\mathsf{C u}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ angle is ${>}104^{\circ}$ ,430 as is observed here $\left(117^{\circ}\right)$ .
## 3.7. Compounds Containing Mixed O- and N-Donor Ligands
3.7.1. Introduction. An alternative class of ligands for generating framework magnets are those based on Nheterocycles functionalized with carboxylate groups (see Figure 11). These multifunctional ligands enable rich coordination chemistry and feature a conjugated $\pi$ -system able to mediate strong magnetic exchange coupling via various types of two- to four-atom bridges. Moreover, this class of compounds shows high chemical tunability, as various combinations of the two components can be selected. Specifically, the geometry, denticity, and overall charge of the ligand can be readily modulated by changing the Nheterocycles and carboxylate moieties to construct materials for targeted applications. Accordingly, the employment of ligands featuring both N and O donors may provide advantages in the design of new multifunctional magnetic materials.
3.7.2. Pyridinecarboxylate-Containing Compounds. The use of pyridinecarboxylato ligands in the synthesis of framework magnets has received considerable attention. The first example of such was the 3D framework $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(3-$ $\mathrm{pyc})_{4}(\mathrm{N}_{3})_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ $\scriptstyle3-\mathrm{pyc}^{-}=3$ -pyridinecarboxylate) reported in 2001.431 This compound is comprised of linear trinuclear $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{~3}^{\mathrm{II}}$ subunits bridged by end-on azido and syn−syn carboxylato linkers, which are further connected into a 3D network through the aromatic backbone of 3-pyc− ligands. This compound behaves as a soft ferrimagnet below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=3.7\:\mathrm{K}$ under low applied magnetic fields, attributed to ferromagnetic exchange interactions between antiferromagnetically coupled $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{~3~}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ clusters. Subsequent studies found that incorporation of a second carboxylate group onto the pyridyl backbone enabled the formation of frameworks with magnetic ordering temperatures up to $T_{\mathrm{{c}}}~=~20.0~\mathrm{{K}}^{432,433}$ Specifically, frameworks comprised of hydroxo-bridged ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ chains have provided the highest ordering temperatures. For instance, the compound $\mathrm{Co^{-}{^{I I}_{3}}(O H)_{2}(3,\bar{4+}p y d c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\left(3,4-\mathrm{pydc}^{2-}\right.}&{{}=}\end{array}$ pyridine-3,4- dicarboxylate) features 1D chains with a formula of $\lbrack\mathrm{Co}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\rbrack^{4+},$ , built up from edge-sharing ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{NO}}_{5}$ octahedra that share a corner with a ${\mathrm{Co}}^{{\bar{\mathrm{II}}}}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ octahedron. Antiferromagnetic interactions are favored within the trimeric building units, leading to overall ferrimagnetic chains. The ferrimagnetic chains are coupled antiferromagnetically to give an ordered state below $T_{\mathrm{N}}~=~20.0$ K. Nevertheless, the interchain interactions are relatively weak, and a spin-flop transition to a ferrimagnetic phase was observed under an external magnetic field above 7000 Oe. The compound displays an open hysteresis loop at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ with a coercive field of Hc = 2750 Oe.432
A recent study revealed that a similar enhancement in ordering temperature can be achieved by using a combination of carboxylate-functionalized N-heterocycles and hydroxosubstituted monocarboxylato ligands.434 Namely, the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}(r a c{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{lac})_{2}(\mathrm{pybz})_{2}{\mathrm{:}}3\mathrm{DMF}$ $\left[r a c-\mathrm{O-}\mathrm{lac}^{2-}\right.=$ racemate 2-oxidopropanoate; pybz− = 4-(4-pyridyl)benzoate] represents the first lactato-bridged porous magnet.434 Its structure is comprised of square tubular chains of alternating ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\mathrm{NO}}_{5}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{{\bar{II}}}}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ polyhedra connected by lactate dianions. The chains are then linked by pybz− ligands in the other two directions to generate a 3D framework with square channels $(10.8~\times~10.8~\mathrm{{\AA^{2}})}$ (see Figure 43). Owing to the large separation between chains, the dominant magnetic exchange pathway is via ${\bf C0}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{-O-}{\bf\dot{C}0}^{\mathrm{II}}$ bridges in the lactato-bridged square chains. Notably, the solvent molecules can be removed from the pores of the framework and exchanged with selective guest molecules, such as MeOH, EtOH, $\mathrm{PrOH}_{,}$ , and benzene, without affecting structural integrity. Furthermore, the desolvated framework displays a Langmuir surface area of $1050\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}}$ and adsorbs $\mathrm{N}_{2},\mathrm{~H}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas molecules. Remarkably, the magnetic properties for this framework can be significantly modulated by the nature of the guest molecules in the pores. While the solvated frameworks ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}$ (rac-O$\mathsf{l a c})_{2}\dot{(}\mathsf{p y b z)}_{2}$ ·xsolvent (xsolvent $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 3DMF, 6MeOH, or $4.5\mathrm{EtOH},$ ) exhibit long-range canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{{N}}}=17.5\mathrm{-}18.7\ \mathrm{K},$ the desolvated compound displays single-chain magnet behavior with a blocking temperature of $T_{\mathrm{B}}=3$ K. Upon exposure of the desolvated framework to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ molecules, structural changes and a dramatic loss of crystallinity were observed due to coordination of $_\mathrm{H}\mathrm{_2O}$ to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and partial oxidation of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{III}}.$ , respectively. The hydrated framework behaves as a ferrimagnet below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=32\mathrm{K},$ whereas the oxidized framework exhibits ferromagnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{C}}=8$ K. Note, however, that the latter two frameworks have not been crystallographically characterized. This lactatobridged compound represents the first example of a single material exhibiting four magnetic ground states. Switching between these states can be carried out through postsynthetic modification, which acts to modulate the magnitude and sign of interchain magnetic exchange interactions.
3.7.3. Pyrimidinecarboxylate-Containing Compounds. In contrast to pyridinecarboxylato ligands, pyrimidinecarboxylates can behave as bis(bidentate) ligands and thus adopt a coordination mode similar to that of oxalato and oxamato ligands (see Figure 44). As a result, these ligands can mediate relatively strong magnetic interactions between metal centers and furnish materials with synthetically predictable structures. For instance, the two isostructural compounds $\mathrm{\big[M_{\hbar_{2}}^{I I}\big(p y m c a\big)_{3}\big]\big(O H\big){\cdot}H_{2}O}$ $\mathbf{\widetilde{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathbf{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; $\operatorname{pymca}^{-}=2.$ - pyrimidinecarboxylate) have been shown to exhibit long-range magnetic order.435 These 2D frameworks consist of heterochiral cationic honeycomb layers that stack to generate hexagonal pores in which hydroxide counterions and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solvent molecules reside (see Figure 45, top). Three types of exchange pathways between metal centers are present in the layers: $_{\mathrm{N-C-N,}}$ anti−anti ${\mathrm{O-C-O}}.$ , and trans $_{\mathrm{N-C-C-O}}$ bridges (see Figure 45, bottom). These interactions collectively result in antiferromagnetic nearest-neighbor coupling within the layers. Owing to weak antiferromagnetic interactions between layers, the $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}}$ framework orders as an antiferromagnet with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=21\ \mathrm{K},$ whereas the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework orders as a canted antiferromagnet below $10~\mathrm{K}.$ The different magnetic behavior observed for these two isostructural compounds was proposed to arise from the higher anisotropy of octahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions compared to $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers. Specifically, the local anisotropy, in conjunction with the antisymmetric exchange interaction,225,226 was posited to be the origin of spin canting in the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework.
3.7.4. Azolecarboxylate- and AzolatecarboxylateContaining Compounds. In addition to the pyridine- and pyrimidinecarboxylate-based frameworks discussed above, one $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework and several ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ frameworks featuring linkers consisting of imidazole, triazole, tetrazole, and thiodiazole cores functionalized with one or two carboxylate groups have been shown to exhibit magnetic order at low temperatures.436−440 The homoleptic compound $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(4{\-}\mathrm{imac})_{2}$ · 2MeOH $\left(4{\cdot}{\mathrm{imac}}^{-}\ =\ 4\right.$ -imidazoleacetate) was reported in 1991, and at that time, it displayed the highest critical temperature $(T_{\mathrm{c}}~=~15~\mathrm{K})$ for a molecule-based compound exhibiting canted antiferromagnetic order.436 The structure of this compound is comprised of layers of distorted octahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions linked by carboxylate groups, with MeOH solvent molecules located in the interlayer space that participate in hydrogen bonding interactions with the 2D framework. The presence of a canted spin structure was hypothesized to stem from the presence of two spin sublattices due to local distortion of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers. Accordingly, antiferromagnetic nearest-neighbor interactions via the carboxylate bridges lead to an uncompensated magnetic moment within each layer, and magnetic interactions between layers, through the hydrogenbonded network, were assumed to induce the formation of a long-range ordered state. Notably, this framework displays a hysteresis loop at $4.2\mathrm{K}$ with a large coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=6200$ Oe.
Of the Co-based frameworks within this series, the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}\mathrm{(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{(btca)}_{2}{\cdot}3.7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(\mathtt{b t c a}^{2-}=\right.$ benzotriazolate-5-carboxylate) features hydroxo-bridged ferrimagnetic chains similar to those of the compound $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}^{\smile}(3,4-$ $\mathrm{pydc})_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}$ (see Figure 46, top and center), but it displays more complicated magnetic behavior. Namely, it orders as a ferrimagnet below $8~\mathrm{~K~}$ and exhibits single-chain magnet behavior below the ordering temperature with a relaxation barrier of $\Delta_{\tau}=85(5)\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $\tau_{0}\stackrel{\cdot}{=}2.6\times10^{-11}$ s (see Figure 46, bottom).437 Interestingly, the desolvated framework retains the original structure and exhibits long-range canted antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=4.5\mathrm{~K~}$ and metamagnetic behavior. The change in magnetic properties observed upon desolvation was attributed to stronger antiferromagnetic interactions between the ferrimagnetic chains when the pores of the framework are empty rather than filled with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules.437 All other members within this series of frameworks feature 3D structures without inorganic subnetworks.438−440 The magnetic properties for these compounds are characterized by spin-canted antiferromagnetism due to canting of antiferromagnetically coupled magnetic moments of highly anisotropic octahedral ${\bf C o}^{\mathrm{II}^{\mathbf{\bar{I}}}}$ ions. Accordingly, these frameworks exhibit long-range antiferromagnetic or canted antiferromagnetic order with TN = 2.4−9 K,438−440 with metamagnetic439 and spin glassy behavior438 also having been observed.
## 3.8. Compounds Containing Diamagnetic Benzoquinoid Ligands
3.8.1. Introduction. Encouraged by the success of oxalato linkers in furnishing magnets with predictable structures and relatively high ordering temperatures, researchers have also explored other types of bis(bidentate) bridging ligands. Of particular interest are ligands that allow for greater chemical tunability than the oxalato ligand while retaining strong magnetic exchange coupling between metal centers. Such ligand modifications may be employed to tune the magnetic properties of the frameworks as well as to introduce additional functionalities. Toward that end, $^{2,5}$ -dioxido-1,4-benzoquinone and its derivatives are particularly attractive candidates (see Figure 47).441 Specifically, these ligands can be readily accessed in three redox states $(-2,-3\bullet,$ and $\left.-4\right\rangle$ , all of which allow for strong covalent interactions between the ligands and a wide range of metal centers to afford compounds with welldefined structures.442,443 In addition, despite the long distance between metal centers, the conjugated system can provide an effective pathway for magnetic exchange interactions. Both the substituents (R) on the aromatic ring and donor atoms (E) can be varied to investigate how electronic and steric effects of different atoms or groups affect the magnetic properties of the resulting frameworks. Together, this illustrates the high degree of chemical tunability for this family of ligands and their potential to provide magnetic materials with predictable structures and properties. In this section, we provide an overview of magnets featuring diamagnetic benzoquinoid linkers.
3.8.2. Benzoquinoid-Containing Compounds. Benzoquinoid ligands may furnish homo- and heterometallic 1D, 2D, and 3D coordination polymers of diverse structure types.442−445 The topology of networks comprised of octahedral metal centers that are tris-chelated by bis(bidentate) benzoquinoid ligands is dictated by the distribution of their absolute configurations, similar to what is observed for oxalate frameworks. Specifically, a 2D honeycomb lattice is formed when neighboring metal centers are of opposing chiralities ( $\dot{\Delta}$ or $\Lambda$ ), whereas a 3D network of (10,3)- a topology results when all of the metal centers adopt the same absolute configuration. Because benzoquinoid ligands are larger than oxalate ligands, this type of 3D structure features two interpenetrated (10,3)- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\boldsymbol{a}$ nets of opposing chiralities, resulting in achiral compounds.444,445 Note that the 3D (10,3)- a topology is rare for benzoquinoid-based coordination solids. Accordingly, all current examples of frameworks bridged by diamagnetic benzoquinoid derivatives that exhibit permanent magnetic behavior are 2D networks of honeycomb topology. Specifically, these compounds feature dianionic tetraoxolenebased ligands $\left(\mathrm{E}\ =\ \mathrm{O}\right)$ and are synthesized following the metalloligand approach by reacting a preformed homoleptic tris-chelated octahedral metal complex with a formula of $[\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{R}_{2}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{n})_{3}]^{3-}$ 1 $\mathbf{\chi}^{\mathrm{{TII}}}=\mathbf{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ; $\mathbb{R}=\mathrm{Cl}$ , Br, I, or H) with divalent transition metal ions, such as $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , in the presence of templating cations.446−453
The first structurally characterized layered magnets of this type were three chiral compounds with a formula of $[\mathrm{\dot{(H_{3}O)(p h z)_{3}}][M n^{I I}M^{I I I}(R_{2}A n)\hat{\overline{{{3}}}}]}$ ·solvent $\mathrm{\boldmath~\phz~}=$ phenazine; $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}\ =\ \mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ; ${\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}{\mathrm{An}}^{2-}\ =$ chloranilate; $\begin{array}{r l}{\hat{\mathrm{B}}\mathbf{r}_{2}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{n}^{2-\mathbf{\Phi}}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ bromanilate; solvent $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O})$ and the achiral compound (Bu4N)[MnIICrIII(Cl2An)3] reported in 2013.446 The honeycomb structure is comprised of anionic layers of $[\mathbf{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{{III}}}(\mathbf{R}_{2}\mathbf{An})_{3}]^{-}$ alternating with cationic layers formed by the chiral $\Delta\mathrm{-}[\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\right)\left(\mathrm{phz}\right)_{3}]^{+}$ or achiral $\mathrm{(Bu_{4}N)^{+}}$ ions. The layers are eclipsed for the compounds with $\Delta$ $-[\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\right)\left(\mathrm{phz}\right)_{3}]^{+}$ cations, generating hexagonal channels, but display alternate packing when $(\mathrm{Bu}_{4}\mathrm{N})^{+}$ counterions are employed. Note that the use of a chiral templating cation leads to a chiral structure owing to favorable $\Delta-\Delta$ interactions, in analogy to what had previously been observed for similar metal oxalates.
Magnetic measurements on the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ compounds revealed antiferromagnetic interactions between metal ions through the $\mathrm{R}_{2}\mathrm{An}^{2-}$ $\mathrm{~\bf~R~}=\mathrm{~\bf~Cl~}$ or $\mathrm{Br}\mathbf{\dot{\Sigma}}$ ) linkers to afford ferrimagnetic layers. Moreover, these compounds exhibit ferrimagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{c}}\approx5{-}6\ \mathrm{K},$ as confirmed by ac susceptibility measurements. While the type of templating cation did not affect the ordering temperature of the anionic lattices, the value of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ was found to be significantly affected by the nature of the substituent on the benzoquinoid ligand. Specifically, when the ligand substituent was varied from ${\bf R}={\bf\Psi}$ Cl to Br to I to $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ the ordering temperature increased from $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ $=5.5$ to 6.3 to 8.2 to $11.0~\mathrm{K},$ respectively, for the series with $\mathrm{(Bu_{4}N)^{+}}$ counterions (see Figure 48, top and center). This observed trend in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ ( $\mathrm{'Cl}<\mathrm{Br}<\mathrm{I}<\mathrm{H}\rangle$ ) correlates linearly with the electronegativity of the ligand substituent (see Figure 48, bottom). Here, the higher electronegativity of the ligand substituent results in less electron density on the benzoquinoid ring, and thus weaker coupling and a lower $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ . Indeed, the value of the Weiss temperature $(\theta)$ follows the same trend, with the strongest antiferromagnetic coupling observed for the framework with ${\bf R}={\bf H}$ (see Figure 48, bottom). As such, this study illustrates that the ligand substituent can be employed to tune the magnetic ordering temperature in these materials. Note, however, that only the $\mathrm{~\mathsf~{~R~}~}=\mathrm{~Cl~}$ derivative has been crystallographically characterized, as the poor crystallinity of the other members of this series has prevented full characterization. To compare, the $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{Fe}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}}$ analogue displays similar antiferromagnetic intralayer interactions and a long-range ordered state was observed below ${\sim}3.5$ K owing to spin canting.446
Other strategies for tuning the magnetism in this family of compounds include the employment of coligands and/or solvent guest molecules. For instance, the use of benzaldehyde as a cosolvent in the preparation of these benzoquinoid f r a m e w o r k s a ff o r d e d t h e c o m p o u n d s $\mathrm{(B{\bar{u}}_{4}N)}$ - $\left[\mathrm{Mn^{II}C r^{I I I}(R_{2}A n)}_{3}(\mathrm{phCHO)}\right].\mathrm{phY}$ ( $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{R}=\mathrm{Cl}$ or Br; $\mathrm{\DeltaY=H}$ or CHO), which feature heptacoordinated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions.452 These compounds display an analogous honeycomb structure as described above, with eclipsed layers, and exhibit ferrimagnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=6.7\mathrm{-}7.0\ \mathrm{K}.$ These ordering temperatures are slightly higher than those observed for $\left(\mathrm{Bu}_{4}\mathrm{N}\right)$ - $\left[{\bf M}{\bf\ddot{n}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\bf C}{\bf\dot{r}}^{\mathrm{III}}({\bf R}_{2}{\bf\dot{A}n})_{3}\right]$ $\mathrm{~\bf~\leftmoon~R~}=\mathrm{~\bf~Cl~}$ or Br), indicating that the presence of coligands and lattice solvent molecules can be employed to fine-tune the magnetic ordering temperature, attributed to slight structural changes that alter the strength of magnetic exchange interactions. More significant changes in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ were observed upon incorporating other benzene derivatives (PhBr, PhCl, PhI, PhMe, $\mathrm{PhCN},$ and $\mathrm{PhNO}_{2})$ ) into the hexagonal pores of the $\left(\mathrm{Bu}_{4}\mathrm{N}\right)\left[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{Br}_{2}\mathrm{An})_{3}\right]$ framework.453 Specifically, these compounds afforded ordering temperatures ranging from $T_{\mathrm{c}}=9.5\mathrm{~K~}$ for the PhBr-solvated framework to $T_{\mathrm{c}}=11.4~\mathrm{K}$ for the PhI-solvated analogue, which are much higher than that of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=6.3\mathrm{~K~}$ obtained for the desolvated framework. The observed increase in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ upon inclusion of the benzene derivatives was proposed to arise from host−guest interactions, namely $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions between the benzene rings of the solvates and benzoquinoid rings of the ligands, to give important structural and/or electronic changes. Unfortunately, a full structural characterization of the desolvated framework is not yet available to test these hypotheses.
In line with earlier studies with bimetallic oxalates,277−282 spin-crossover $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ cations have been incorporated into the pores of 2D benzoquinoid frameworks with the goal of achieving multifunctional magnetic materials.447,448 In contrast to the minimal impact of the inserted cations on $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ for the oxalate frameworks, a significant enhancement in the magnetic ordering temperature was observed upon introducing a range of cationic spin-crossover $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complexes into the pores of anionic [MnIICrIII(R2An)3]− (R = Cl or Br) lattices.447,448 These compounds exhibit honeycomb structures and ferrimagnetic behavior, similar to the frameworks with $\mathrm{(Bu_{4}N)^{+}}$ cations, albeit with a nearly 2-fold increase in $T_{c},$ to values of ${\sim}10{-}11$ K. Note, however, that the increase in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ is likely not caused by magnetic interactions, as a near identical ordering temperature was obtained when a paramagnetic $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complex was replaced by a diamagnetic $\mathbf{Ga}^{\mathrm{III}}$ analogue.448 Furthermore, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{\bar{III}}}$ complexes did not display spin-crossover behavior after inclusion in the benzoquinoid lattices; rather, they remained exclusively in their low-spin or high-spin state,447,448 presumably owing to the absence of intermolecular interactions that can facilitate cooperative spin-crossover. As such, these hybrid materials did not show the coexistence of spin-crossover and long-range magnetic order, and the observed change in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ likely stems from structural changes caused by introduction of larger cations into the frameworks.
The effects of the size of the templating cation on the magnetic properties of these materials have been further illustrated through a series of $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}}$ frameworks with inserted alkylammonium cations of various sizes.449 While the compounds $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\left[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{Br}_{2}\mathrm{An})_{3}\right]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{1}$ $\left(\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ - $\mathrm{\big[Mn^{\hat{I}I}C r^{I I I}\big(B r_{2}A n\big)_{3}\big],}$ and $\left(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{NH}\right)\left[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{An})_{3}\right]$ feature the typical honeycomb layered structures with the ammonium cations occupying the cavities in the hexagonal channels (see Figure 49, left), the employment of the slightly larger $(\bar{\mathrm{Et}^{i}\mathrm{Pr}_{2}\mathrm{NH}})^{+}$ cation provided a different bilayer structure for the compound $\left(\mathrm{Et}^{i}\mathrm{Pr}_{2}\mathrm{NH}\right)\left[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}\left(\mathrm{Br}_{2}\mathrm{An}\right)_{3}\right]\cdot0.5\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}.$ · $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Here, half of the cations resides in the pores and the other half is located in the interlayer space (see Figure 49, right). These structural changes have profound effects on the magnetic properties of these compounds. Specifically, the compounds possessing regular honeycomb structures display the typical long-range ferrimagnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=7.9-8.9$ K, whereas the bilayer compound with $\left(\mathrm{Et}^{i}\mathrm{Pr}_{2}\mathrm{NH}\right)^{+}$ cations exhibits weak antiferromagnetic interactions between the ferrimagnetic layers when it is measured in contact with the mother liquor. The solvated bilayer framework exhibits longrange antiferromagnetic order and metamagnetic behavior. Interestingly, the dried sample shows a ferrimagnetic-like order below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=9.0~\mathrm{K},$ which was attributed to a collapse of the structure. Notably, the desolvation/solvation process is reversible, thereby demonstrating the reversible switching of magnetic properties for this compound. The origin of this guest-dependent behavior was postulated to arise from halogen−halogen interlayer interactions that are not present in the compounds with the smaller alkylammonium cations. Furthermore, these compounds show moderate proton conductivities and thus belong to a rare class of multifunctional materials that show a coexistence of long-range magnetic order and proton conduction.
In addition to the heterobimetallic $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ 2D benzoquinoid frameworks, homometallic mixed-valence $\mathrm{Fe^{II}F e^{I I I}}$ frameworks have also been recently reported.450,451 The compounds $[\mathrm{(H_{3}O)(H_{2}O)(p h z)_{3}}][\mathrm{\dot{Fe}^{I I}F\hat{e}^{I I I}(R_{2}A n)_{3}}]$ · $12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{\ph{z}=}$ phenazine; ${\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}{\mathrm{An}}^{2-}=$ chloranilate; $\mathbf{Br}_{2}\mathbf{An}^{2-}=$ bromanilate) show the honeycomb layered structure with eclipsed packing of layers.450 Variable-temperature $\textsc{Q}$ band EPR spectra revealed that both frameworks are Robin−Day class II mixed-valence compounds, as the appearance of a signal at ${\sim}20{\mathrm{-}}40\mathrm{~K~}$ attributed to the $S=\sqrt[5]{_{2}}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers indicated that the delocalized electron becomes trapped at low temperatures. Indeed, magnetic susceptibility measurements are consistent with class II mixed valency, as the presence of distinct $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites at low temperatures gives rise to ferrimagnetic long-range order.
Compared to those of the $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}}$ frameworks with similar cations, the ordering temperatures for the $\mathrm{Fe^{II}F e^{I I I}}$ derivatives of $T_{\mathrm{c}}~=~2.1{-2.4}~\mathrm{\bar{K}}$ are much lower.446 Interestingly, the chloranilate-bridged compound $\mathrm{~\bf~R~}=\mathrm{~\bf~Cl~}_{\prime}$ ) orders at a temperature slightly higher than that of the bromanilatebridged $\langle\mathcal{R}\ =\ \mathrm{\bar{B}r}\rangle$ congener, suggesting that the magnetic coupling strength increases as the electronegativity of R increase s,450 in contrast with the observation for the analogous $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ series with $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{~\mathsf~{~R~}~}=\mathrm{~Cl~}}_{\prime}}\end{array}$ , Br, I, or $\mathrm{H.}^{446}$ This different behavior may be explained by the double-exchange mechanism present in the $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{\DeltaII}}F e^{\mathrm{III}}}$ series. Furthermore, the electron delocalization in these compounds provides high electrical conductivity in the plane parallel to the hexagonal layers, with room-temperature values of $\sigma_{||}=0.03$ and $0.0\dot{0}3\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ for ${\mathrm{R}}=$ Cl and Br, respectively (see Figure 50), confirming their semiconducting behaviors.450 Similar behavior has been observed for the isostructural framework compound (tag)- $\mathrm{\small{~\left[Fe^{II}F e^{I I I}\left(C l C N A n\right)_{3}\right]\cdot29H_{2}O}}$ $\left(\log^{+}{\mathbf{\mu}}=\mathbf{\mu}\right.$ triaminoguanidinium; ${\mathrm{ClCNAn}}^{2-}\ =$ chlorocyanoanilate), which features an asymmetric benzoquinoid ligand.451 This compound has a slightly higher magnetic ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}~=~4~\mathrm{K}$ and a slightly lower in-plane room-temperature conductivity of $\sigma_{||}=$ $0.002~\mathrm{~S~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Notably, the benzoquinoid-based $\mathrm{Fe^{II}F e^{I I I}}$ frameworks exhibit electrical conductivity that is much higher than those of analogous oxalato-bridged compounds,454 owing to their greater effectiveness in promoting electron delocalization through a small-polaron hopping mechanism.451
In summary, the 2D frameworks based on diamagnetic benzoquinoid linkers exhibit modest magnetic ordering temperatures $\left(T_{c}=5.5\mathrm{-}11.4\mathrm{~K}\right)$ that can be tuned by the choice of metal centers, bridging ligand substituents, templating cations, and guest molecules. Furthermore, this family of materials represents rare examples of compounds that show both long-range magnetic order and electrical conductivity, making them attractive candidates for the creation of multifunctional magnetic materials.
## 4. MOF MAGNETS WITH RADICAL BRIDGING LIGANDS
## 4.1. Introduction
## 4.2. Nitroxide Radical-Bridged Compounds
Derivatives of nitronyl nitroxide represent a class of highly stable organic radicals that feature an unpaired electron delocalized over two coordinating O atoms (see Figure 51, top left). Accordingly, these radicals can behave as bis(monodentate) ligands and are attractive spin carriers in magnetic materials. The main limitation of these ligands is the poor coordinating ability of the $_{\mathrm{N-O}}$ group toward metal ions owing to its weak Lewis basicity. However, this problem can be circumvented by employing electron-withdrawing coligands, such as 1,1,1,5,5,5-hexafluoroacetylacetonate $(\mathrm{hfac^{-}})$ , which act to increase the Lewis acidity of the metal centers and thus facilitate the formation of compounds with strong metal− radical bonds. Nitronyl nitroxide radicals are most commonly encountered in 1D chain compounds,97−111 but they have also been successfully incorporated into networks of higher dimensionalities.455−459
The first example of such a framework was the 3D oxamatebased compound $\left(4{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{y}}\mathbf{NO}\right)_{2}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{}_{2}\left(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\left(\mathbf{\opba}\right)}\right)_{3}-$ $\big(\mathrm{DMSO}\big)_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left[4{\cdot}\mathrm{MePyNO^{+}}^{\bullet}=2\right.$ -(4-N-methylpyridinium)-4,4,5,5-tetramethylimidazoline-1-oxyl-3-oxide radical; $\mathrm{\opba^{4-}=1,2}$ -phenylenebis(oxamate)], which features a fully interlocked structure with three distinct spin carriers: 4- MePyNO+• (S = 1/2), MnII (S = 5/2), and CuII (S = 1/2).455,456 The structure consists of two nearly perpendicular hexagonal layers with $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions at the corners and $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions in the middle of the edge of each hexagon. Nitronyl nitroxide radical cations link two-thirds of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers to form linear chains, thereby connecting the two networks (see Figure 52). Variable-temperature dc susceptibility and magnetization data revealed characteristic ferrimagnetic behavior with the occurrence of a long-range order below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=22.5\ \mathrm{K},$ owing to strong antiferromagnetic coupling between MnII and CuII ions through the oxamate bridges. Moreover, the field dependence of the magnetization indicated that the organic radical cation interacts ferromagnetically with the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers in the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ −radical chains and antiferromagnetically with the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions. Furthermore, this compound behaves as a soft magnet with a small coercive field $\left(H_{\mathrm{c}}<10\mathrm{~Oe}\right)$ at $4.2~\mathrm{K}.$
A similar interlocked structure and ferrimagnetic behavior ( ${T_{\mathrm{c}}=22.8\ \mathrm{K}})}$ were observed upon replacement of the methyl groups on the nitroxide radical cations with slightly larger ethyl groups.457 However, when the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions were exchanged for more anisotropic ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers, the magnetic ordering temperature increased to $T_{\mathrm{c}}=37\:\mathrm{K}$ with a drastic enhancement of the coercive field.457,458 Specifically, the coercive field for the compound $\mathrm{\big(4\mathrm{-EtPy}\hat{N O}\big)_{2}C o^{i I}\Sigma_{2}\big(C u^{I I}\big(o p b a\big)\big)_{3}\big(D M S O\big)_{0.5}.}$ DMSO· $0.25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left[4{\cdot}\mathrm{EtPyNO^{+0}}~=~2{\cdot}~\right.$ -(4-N-ethylpyridinium)- $^{4,4,5,5}$ -tetramethylimidazoline-1-oxyl-3-oxide radical], as obtained from magnetic hysteresis measurements at $6\ \mathrm{K},$ was found to be highly dependent on the size of the crystallites. While the largest crystals gave a value of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=8500$ Oe, a remarkable coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}~>~2.4\mathrm{~T~}$ was obtained for crystals that were ${\sim}50$ times smaller.457,458 The analogous (4- $\mathrm{EiPyNO})_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cu}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}$ framework exhibits an ordering temperature ( $T_{\mathrm{c}}=28\ \mathrm{K})$ and a coercive field $(H_{\mathrm{c}}=500$ Oe at $6\mathrm{~K~}$ ) that fall between the values for the $\left(4{-}\mathrm{EtPyNO}\right)_{2}\mathbf{Mn}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Cu}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{(4{-}E t P y N O)_{2}C o^{I I}}_{2}\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}_{3}$ congeners.458
4,4,5,5-tetramethylimidazoline-1-oxyl-3-oxide radical] features ferrimagnetic layers and ferromagnetic chains similar to those of the other members of this series of compounds and orders below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=24.1~\mathrm{K}.$ Interestingly, magnetization data at a very low temperature of $120~\mathrm{\mK}$ revealed a metamagnetic-like transition under a magnetic field of ${\sim}2.1$ T, attributed to flipping of half of the radical spins in the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ −radical chains.459
In addition to the oxamate-based frameworks, where the nitronyl nitroxide radicals act as coligands, two compounds featuring $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{hfac})_{2}$ complexes bridged by trinitroxide radicals have demonstrated permanent magnetism.460,461 The employment of the 3-fold symmetric ligand $1,3,5{\cdot}\mathrm{tris}[p$ -(Ntert-butyl- $.N$ -oxyamino)phenyl]benzene triradical (TOAPB) afforded a 2D layered structure of honeycomb topology. The resulting compound (TOAPB) $\mathsf{\Omega}_{2}(\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\dot{(\mathrm{hfac})}_{2})_{3}{\cdot}\dot{\mathrm{H}}\mathsf{p}$ exhibits long-range magnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{c}}~=~3.4~\mathrm{K},$ which was postulated to arise from ferromagnetic coupling between ferrimagnetic layers.460,461 However, moving to a linear trinitroxide radical ligand that displays much stronger intramolecular nitroxide−nitroxide exchange coupling462 provided a parallel cross-shaped 3D framework, (BAPN) $_{2}(\mathbf{\bar{M}}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{hfac})_{2})_{3}$ $\left\{\mathrm{BAPN}\ =\right.$ bis[3-tert-butyl-5-(N-oxy-tert-butylamino)phenyl] nitroxide triradical}, which orders at $T_{\mathrm{c}}~\stackrel{\cdot}{=}~46~\mathrm{K}.^{\hat{46}1}$ The magnetic data for this compound are consistent with antiferromagnetic interactions between $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions and nitroxide radicals, and ferromagnetic coupling between nitroxide radicals within each ligand, giving an overall $S\ =\ ^{9}/_{2}$ ground state. Finally, note that despite the radical character of this compound, it displays high chemical stability, as no degradation was observed after a year at room temperature in air.
Homoleptic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ compounds featuring bis(chelating) nitronyl nitroxide ligands can also give rise to spontaneous magnetization.463 Here, the metal coordination sphere is free of electron-withdrawing groups. One such compound with a formula of $\left[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}(\mathbf{NITIm})_{3}\right](\mathbf{ClO}_{4})$ $\left[\mathrm{NITIm}^{-\bullet}\ =\ 2.(\$ (2- imidazol-1-ide)-4,4,5,5-tetramethylimidazoline-1-oxyl-3-oxide radical] has been characterized by $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffraction. The structure displays octahedral $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers that are tris-chelated by anionic bis(bidentate) nitroxide ligands to give a mer$\dot{\bf M}{\bf n}^{\mathrm{II}}{\bf N}_{3}{\bf O}_{3}$ coordination sphere. These units are arranged in a 2D framework of honeycomb topology, with perchlorate counterions located in the interlayer space. This compound features ferrimagnetic layers, as confirmed by a saturation magnetization value of $\dot{M_{s}}=6.7(1)$ $\mu_{\mathrm{B}}\ \mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ that is close to the theoretical value of $7~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ expected for antiferromagnetic coupling between two $S=\sqrt[5]{_{2}\mathrm{\mh}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions and three S $={^1}/_{2}$ radicals. Moreover, the framework exhibits magnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=1.4~\mathrm{K}$ and a hysteresis loop at $85~\mathrm{mK}$ with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=270$ Oe and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=0.22~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}~\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ .
## 4.3. Triphenylmethyl Radical-Bridged Compounds
Another stable multitopic organic radical linker suitable for the synthesis of framework materials is the polychlorinated triphenylmethyl tricarboxylate radical $(\mathrm{PTMTC}^{3-{\bf\vec{\Phi}}})$ ) (see Figure 51, top right). This radical ligand features a central carbon atom with high spin density that is sterically shielded by bulky chloro substituents, and the half-life of this species in solution at room temperature under aerobic conditions is estimated to be on the order of 100 years. Furthermore, it is thermally stable at temperatures of $\underline{{\underline{{\:}}}}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .464 The trigonal symmetry of this polycarboxylato ligand facilitates the formation of 2D and 3D networks.
The first example of a framework magnet incorporating this tricarboxylate-functionalized radical bridging ligand was the 2D framework compound $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{PTMTC})_{2}^{\sim}(\mathrm{py})_{6}(\mathrm{EtOH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ · $10\mathrm{EtOH}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (py $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ pyridine) (MOROF-1).465 The structure of this compound features layers of honeycomb topology, with the central carbon on the $\mathrm{PTMTC}^{3-\bullet}$ ligands occupying each vertex of the honeycomb. Each ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ center resides in a squarepyramidal coordination environment consisting of two monodentate carboxylate groups, two pyridine ligands, and one EtOH or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule (see Figure 53). Stacking of the layers generates 1D hexagonal channels with very large pores $(\dot{3}1\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\ \times28\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ .
Desolvation of this compound occurs rapidly at room temperature with dramatic contraction in crystal volume $(25-$ $35\%$ ) and complete loss of crystallinity. However, the original structure is mostly recovered upon soaking the material in EtOH or $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{MeOH}},$ , as confirmed by PXRD analysis. Notably, other organic solvents were unable to induce this quasireversible “shrinking−breathing” process. Variable-temperature dc magnetic susceptibility measurements on a crystalline sample in contact with EtOH revealed antiferromagnetic interactions between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and radical linkers to give an overall ferrimagnetic interaction, with a minimum value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ at 31 K followed by a rapid upturn. The large increase in the value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ at low temperatures suggests the onset of a long-range magnetic order, which was confirmed by ac susceptibility measurements. In contrast, the desolvated compound behaves as a paramagnet.465
Treatment of the $\mathrm{PTMTC}^{3-\bullet}$ radical ligand with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions afforded a framework with a different topology and distinct magnetic behavior.466 Specifically, this compound with a formula of $\mathrm{Co^{II}}_{6}(\mathrm{PTMT\bar{C})_{4}(p y)_{17}(E t O H)(H}_{2}\mathrm{\bar{O})_{4}}$ (MOROF3) exhibits an unusual (6,3)-helical 2D structure, and isothermal magnetization measurements at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ suggested coexisting ferro- and antiferromagnetic interactions between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions and PTMTC3−• radicals. This behavior was attributed to the presence of mixed mono- and bidentate ligand binding modes in the structure and asymmetry of the trigonal nodes. This compound did not exhibit hysteretic behavior at ${1.8~\mathrm{K}};$ however, the onset of a signal in the out-ofphase ac susceptibility $\left(\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{\prime\prime}\right)$ under an external dc field of 500 Oe suggests that long-range magnetic order may occur at a lower temperature.
## 4.4. Organonitrile Radical-Bridged Compounds
4.4.1. TCNE Radical-Bridged Compounds. Building on the remarkable permanent magnetic behavior above room temperature observed for the amorphous compound V$(\mathrm{T}\bar{\mathrm{CNE}})_{x}{\cdot}y\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $(x\approx2;y\approx0.5),^{8\hat{2}-85}$ tremendous effort has been directed toward synthesizing and structurally characterizing related materials featuring $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical ligands. Such in-depth structural analysis is critical to deduce the structure−property relationships for this family of magnets and to enable the rational design of molecule-based magnets with high ordering temperatures for targeted applications.
The first structurally characterized framework magnet featuring the $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical anion was the compound $\big[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(\mathrm{T}\breve{\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{NE}^{-\bullet}\big)\big(\mathrm{Me}{\mathrm{CN}}\big)_{2}\big]\big(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\big)$ reported in 2006.467 The structure, obtained from Rietveld refinement of synchroton PXRD data, is comprised of corrugated layers of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions bridged by planar $\mu_{4}–\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet}$ radical anions. The octahedral coordination sphere of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers is completed by two axial MeCN molecules, and the interlayer space is occupied by $\mathrm{(Fe^{III}C l_{4})^{-}}$ anions. This compound orders as an antiferromagnet below $90\ \mathrm{~K~},$ which was attributed to antiferromagnetic coupling between ferrimagnetic $[\mathrm{Fe^{II}(T C N E^{-\bullet})(M e C N)}_{2}]^{+}$ layers. However, this state is metastable due to a strong structural magnetic anisotropy, and an irreversible transformation to a more stable ferrimagnetic state was observed upon application of a small magnetic field. Accordingly, this compound exhibits magnetic hysteresis at $50\mathrm{K}$ with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=1730$ Oe and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=$ 7500 Oe $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ .
More recently, the minimal impact of the counteranion on the 2D layered structure and magnetic properties for this type of compound was demonstrated.468 In particular, replacing the $\mathrm{(Fe^{III}C\hat{l}_{4})^{-}}$ ions with larger $(\mathrm{{{Sb}^{V}{F_{6}}})^{-}}$ ions afforded the isostructural compounds $\mathsf{\bar{[}M^{I I}(T C N E^{-\bullet})(M e C N)_{2}](S b^{V}F_{6})}$ · $x{\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}{\cdot}y{\mathrm{MeCN}}$ $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\right.$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ ) that display longrange magnetic order below 67 and $96~\mathrm{K}$ for $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\dot{\mathbf{M}}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},$ , respectively. The $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ congener exhibits additional spin glass behavior, speculated to stem from the presence of a significant amount of an amorphous phase originating from partial loss of axially coordinated MeCN molecules.
Another important step toward better understanding the magnetic properties of $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical-based systems was the structural characterization of the compound $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{TCNE})_{2}$ · $0.32\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}.$ 469 Its structure possesses corrugated layers of octahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers each bonded to four $\mu_{4}–\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet}$ radical anions, and these layers are connected by diamagnetic $\mu_{4^{-}}$ $[\mathrm{C}_{4}(\mathrm{CN})_{8}]^{2-}$ ions to generate a 3D framework. Note that the diamagnetic ligands were formed in situ through the dimerization of $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical anions via $C{\mathrm{-}}\bar{\mathrm{C}}$ bond formation. Accordingly, the compound is best formulated as $\mathrm{Fe^{II}(T C N E^{-\bullet})}[\mathrm{C}_{4}(\mathrm{CN})_{8}]_{0.5}{\cdot}0.32\hat{\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ . The dominant spin interaction contributing to the ferrimagnetic behavior for this compound is the antiferromagnetic direct exchange coupling between $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical anions in the layers.469 The realization of a mixture of paramagnetic and diamagnetic TCNE ligands in this compound clarified its high saturation magnetization value and the similar ordering temperature of ${\sim}100$ K as observed for the previously characterized 2D layered frameworks discussed above.86,87,469
The isostructural $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ analogue of the mixed-TCNE ligand framework, $\mathrm{Mn}\mathrm{\stackrel{II}{\_}(T C N E^{-\bullet})[C_{4}(C N)_{8}]_{0.5}.0.74C H_{2}C l_{2}},$ has also been reported.72 Here, significant antiferromagnetic interactions between ferrimagnetic layers via the diamagnetic $[\mathrm{C}_{4}(\mathrm{CN})_{8}]^{2-}$ ligands give rise to dominant antiferromagnetic behavior. This framework exhibits long-range antiferromagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=68\ \mathrm{K},$ as determined by an insignificant $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{\prime\prime}$ signal and the coincidence of zero-field-cooled and fieldcooled magnetization data.
The reaction of TCNE with $\begin{array}{r}{{\mathrm{Mn}}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{I}_{2}\big(\mathrm{THF}\big)_{3}}\end{array}$ in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ afforded two compounds with drastically different structures depending on the reaction time. Interestingly, both compounds exhibit a magnetic ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=171\mathrm{K},$ which represents the current record among structurally characterized MOF magnets.72,73 A reaction time of 2 days gave the 3D framework $\mathrm{\DeltaMn^{II}(T C N E^{-\bullet})_{1.5}(I_{3})_{0.5}{\cdot}0.5T H F.^{72}}$ In contrast to the 3D frameworks discussed above, this compound features exclusively $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical ligands. The structure comprises octahedral $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions bonded to six different $\mu_{4}–\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet}$ ligands; four of them are involved in forming corrugated sheets, and the other two bridge adjacent planes (see Figure 54, top). Notably, the angle between intraand interlayer $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}{-}\mathbf{N}$ bonds is ${\sim}90^{\circ}.$ , and isolated linear chains of $(\mathrm{I}_{3})^{-}$ ions are present for charge compensation. Variable-temperature dc susceptibility and magnetization measurements revealed characteristic ferrimagnetic behavior with a sharp upturn in $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ at ${\sim}185\mathrm{~K~}$ and a bifurcation temperature for the zero-field-cooled (ZFC) and field-cooled (FC) magnetization data of $T_{\mathrm{b}}=171\ \mathrm{K}$ (see Figure 54, center and bottom). The presence of long-range magnetic order was further confirmed by ac susceptibility studies, and magnetic hysteresis was observed at $10\mathrm{~K~}$ with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=$ $600\ \mathrm{Oe}$ and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=8000$ Oe $\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ . Overall, this magnetic behavior is indicative of a soft 3D ferrimagnet, which can be attributed to direct antiferromagnetic coupling between $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{TCNE^{-\bullet}}$ radical ligands within and between layers. Interestingly, preliminary experiments revealed that the ordering temperature increases upon application of an external pressure, reaching a value of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=273$ K under 14.2 kbar of pressure.72
The second compound, a 2D framework with the formula of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet})\mathrm{I}(\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathbf{O}).$ , was obtained after the reaction solution had been allowed to stand for 6 months.73 This species features a corrugated layered structure similar to that of $[\mathrm{\bf\dot{M}^{I I}(T C N E^{-\bullet})(M e C N)_{2}}]^{+}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}^{I I}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}\cdot}$ ), albeit with two distinct environments for the $\mu_{4}–\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet}$ ligands owing to different dihedral angles between the mean $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{{N_{4}}}}$ and ${\mathrm{TCNE}}–C_{6}$ planes (see Figure 55). Furthermore, the interlayer separation of $5.00\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ is significantly shorter than those in other structurally characterized $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ -TCNE frameworks, which are all $>8.0$ Å. The magnetic properties for this compound are very similar to those observed for the 3D framework $\mathrm{Mn^{II}(T C N E^{-\bullet})}_{1.5}(\mathrm{I}_{3})_{0.5}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{THF}$ . Specifically, the zero-fieldcooled (ZFC) and field-cooled (FC) magnetization curves measured under an applied field of 5 Oe revealed a bifurcation temperature of $T_{\mathrm{b}}=172~\mathrm{K}$ and a remanent magnetization that was coincident with the field-cooled data. The latter provided an ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}~=~171~\mathrm{{\calK}},$ which was in agreement with ac susceptibility data. Furthermore, magnetic hysteresis at $10\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=400$ Oe and a remanent magnetization of $M_{\mathrm{r}}=60\ \mathrm{Oe\cm^{3}\ m o l^{-1}}$ .
The long-range order for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet})\mathrm{I}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})$ was attributed to antiferromagnetic interactions between canted ferrimagnetic 2D layers. The lower saturation and remanent magnetization values compared to those obtained for $\mathrm{Mn^{\mathrm{\check{II}}}(T C N E^{-\bullet})}_{1.5}(\mathrm{I}_{3})_{0.5}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{T}\mathrm{\hat{H}F}$ provided support for this interpretation. Furthermore, the short inter- and intralayer distances, along with the high value of $T_{c},$ suggest a 3D magnetic order for this compound, and thus non-negligible interlayer interactions. Finally, note that both $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{TCNE}^{-\bullet})$ - $\mathrm{I}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and $\mathrm{Mn^{II}(T C N E^{-\bullet})}_{1.5}^{\cdot}(\mathrm{I}_{3})_{0.5}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{THF}$ are soft magnets, which is expected given the nearly isotropic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers in these compounds.
4.4.2. TCNQ Radical-Bridged Compounds. In addition to extensive studies of TCNE-based radical systems, compounds featuring the larger derivative 7,7,8,8-tetracyano$p$ -quinodimethane (TCNQ) have also been of immediate interest owing to the higher chemical tunability of this building unit. Specifically, the central aromatic ring can be functionalized with various substituents to tune both electronic and steric properties of the ligand (see Figure 51, bottom left). Linkers based on TCNQ derivatives have been of particular focus in the design of electrically conductive framework magnets. For that purpose, the employment of electron-rich dimeric $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ paddlewheel building units and TCNQ-based linkers has been highly successful, affording a number of frameworks that display permanent magnetic behavior.470−476 Here, an electron transfer from the $\mathtt{R u}_{2}^{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}$ moieties to $\mathrm{TCNQ}.$ based ligands $(\mathrm{TCNQR}_{x};$ R denotes the type of substituent on the aromatic ring, and $x$ denotes the number of substituents) can lead to a mixed-valence state of the form $\left[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\right]^{4.5+}-$ $\mathrm{TCNQR}_{x}^{-\bullet}-\left[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\right]^{4.5+}$ . Such electron transfer results in magnetic double-exchange interactions between $S=1\mathrm{{\Ru}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}_{2}$ and $S={^3}/_{2}\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II,III}}{_2}$ units via the $S=\mathrm{\Omega^{1}/_{2}}\mathrm{TCNQR_{\itx}}^{-\bullet}$ radica2l anion that may lead to long-range magnetic order.
The first example of a successful implementation of this strategy to generate a magnet was the 2D framework compound $(\mathsf{\tilde{R}u}_{2}(\mathrm{TFA})_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{T\tilde{C}N Q F}_{4}){\cdot}3(p{\cdot}\mathrm{xyl}{\cdot}$ ene) $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{TFA}^{-}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ trifluoroacetate; $\mathrm{TCNQF}_{4}~=~2,3,5,6$ -tetrafluoro-7,7,8,8-tetracyano- $p$ -quinodimethane). $^{470,471}$ The structure of this compound displays $\mathrm{Ru}_{2}(\mathrm{TFA})_{4}$ paddlewheel units with two cyano groups from distinct $\mathrm{TCNQF}_{4}$ ligands coordinated to the axial sites of the Ru centers. The overall structure has a fishnet-like hexagonal topology (see Figure 56, top). The $\mathrm{Ru-O_{equatorial}}$ bond distance is strongly influenced by the oxidation state of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ paddlewheel core, indicative here of the partially oxidized state $\left[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\right]^{4.5+}$ . Furthermore, analysis of the degree of charge transfer using the Kistenmacher relationship477 for the $\mathrm{TCNQF_{4}}$ ligand was consistent with one-electron transfer from two $\mathrm{Ru}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{TFA})_{4}$ units to one $\mathrm{TCNQF}_{4}$ ligand, such that this compound is better formulated as $\widetilde{\big(}\big[\mathrm{R}\mathrm{u}_{2}\big]^{4.5+}\big(\mathrm{TFA}\big)_{4}\big)$ - $(\mathrm{TCNQF_{4}^{-\bullet}})([\mathrm{Ru}_{2}]^{4.5+}(\mathrm{TFA})_{4})\cdot3($ $p$ -xylene). This compound exhibits long-range antiferromagnetic order below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=95\ \mathrm{K},$ as evidenced by a maximum value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ at $96~\mathrm{K}$ (see Figure 56, bottom) and no $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{\prime\prime}$ signal down to $1.8~\mathrm{K}$ at zero dc field. Additionally, a spin-flop transition to a paramagnetic phase was observed with a critical field of $4.74\mathrm{~T~}$ at $1.82~\mathrm{K},$ and a large open butterfly-type hysteresis loop indicated an intermediate canted spin phase.
This metamagnetic-like behavior was posited to stem from antiferromagnetic interactions between strongly coupled magnetic layers and the strong magnetic anisotropy from the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ units. Notably, the room-temperature electrical conductivity for this compound is $\sigma=4.{\overset{\frown}{6}}\times10^{-4}\operatorname{S}\operatorname{cm}^{-1};$ , which is 100 times greater than that observed for the analogue bearing $\mathrm{Ru}_{2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and a diamagnetic TCNQ bridging ligand. Along these lines, the electron affinity of the $\mathrm{TCNQR}_{x}$ acceptor plays a critical role in determining the efficiency of electron transfer from the $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}_{~2}^{\mathrm{II}}$ core.478,479 Indeed, the mixed-valence state was not accessed when the $\mathrm{TCNQF}_{4}$ ligand was replaced by less electronegative TCNQ derivatives, including $\mathrm{TCNQH}_{4},$ $\mathrm{TCNQBr}_{2},$ $\mathrm{TCNQCl}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{TCNQF}_{2}$ .471 Accordingly, those compounds showed only paramagnetic behavior.
Interestingly, the mixed-valence state could also be accessed by employing $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ paddlewheel units with 1,2-chlorobenzoate $\left(1,2\mathrm{-Clbz^{-}}\right)$ ligands in conjunction with $\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ (2,5- dimethoxy-7,7,8,8-tetracyano- $\cdot p$ -quinodimethane) bridging ligands.472 The resulting compound $\left(\left[\mathrm{R}\mathbf{u}_{2}\right]^{4.5+}\left(1,2\mathrm{-}\check{\mathrm{Clb}}\mathbf{\check{z}}\right)_{4}\right)$ - $\bar{\big(}\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}^{-\bullet})(\bar{\big[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\big]}^{4.5+}(\bar{1,}2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Clbz})_{4})\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is isostructural to the 2D network described above and shows similar long-range antiferromagnetic order under low external magnetic fields $\mathit{'}H<30\bar{0}\mathrm{~Oe})$ with $T_{\mathrm{N}}=75\ \mathrm{K},$ along with metamagnetic behavior. However, this compound undergoes a nearly reversible desolvation without losing crystallinity, with associated changes in magnetic properties. Specifically, the desolvated framework behaves as a ferromagnet below $T_{\mathrm{C}}\approx56$ $\begin{array}{r}{\left\vert\mathrm{K},\right\vert}\end{array}$ as evidenced by the growth of a $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{\prime\prime}$ signal during the desolvation process. The difference in magnetic behavior for the two compounds was attributed to slight structural changes caused by the order/disorder of $1,\bar{2}\substack{-\mathrm{Clbz}^{-}}$ ligands. In particular, the disorder of the phenyl groups in the desolvated framework was posited to disturb the antiferromagnetic interlayer interactions that are responsible for the antiferromagnetic behavior of the solvated framework. Note that the magnetic properties of TCNQ-based frameworks had previously been shown to be altered by a reversible desolvation/solvation process.480 However, in that case, only the poorly crystalline desolvated compound displayed magnetlike behavior.
Another fascinating example of how drastic changes in magnetic behavior can be induced by desolvation/solvation treatments is the isostructural compound ( $\mathrm{Ru}_{2}(2,3,5-$ $\mathrm{Clbz})_{4})_{2}\big(\mathrm{TCNQMe}_{2}\big){\cdot}4\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $(2,3,5\mathrm{-Clbz^{-}}=2,3,5$ -trichlorobenzoate; $\mathrm{TCNQMe}_{2}~=~2,5$ -dimethyl-7,7,8,8-tetracyano- $p$ - quinodimethane).473 The solvated framework displays a charge-localized state that is formulated as $\mathrm{([Ru_{2}]^{4+}(}2,3,5\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{Clbz}\tilde{\mathrm{)_{4})(T C N Q M e_{2}\tilde{\Psi})([\mathrm{Ru_{2}}]^{5+}(2,3,5-C l b z)_{4}){\cdot}4C H_{2}C l_{2}}}$ and orders below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=101\ \mathrm{K}$ owing to ferromagnetic interactions between ferrimagnetic layers. In contrast, the desolvated framework displays a charge-disproportionate disordered state at ${\sim}100\mathrm{~K~}$ with a formula of $([\mathrm{{R},\mathrm{{u}}_{2}]^{4/5+}}(2,3,5\mathrm{{-Clbz})_{4})}$ - $\mathrm{(TCNQMe_{2}^{-\bullet}/T C N Q M e_{2}^{\quad2-\bullet}}$ ) $\left[\mathrm{R}\mathrm{u}_{2}\right]^{5+}\left(2,3,5{\cdot}\mathrm{Clbz}\right)_{4})$ . This electronic state transfers to the nearly fully charge-transferred state with a formula of $\left([\mathrm{R}\mathbf{u}_{2}]^{5+}(2,3,\dot{5}\mathrm{-Clb}\dot{\mathbf{z}})_{4}\right)$ $\left(\mathrm{TCNQMe}_{2}^{2-}\right)$ - $\left(\left[\mathrm{R}\mathbf{u}_{2}\right]^{5+}(2,3,5{\cdot}\mathrm{Clb}\mathbf{z})_{4}\right)$ at high temperatures. The desolvated framework also orders as a ferrimagnet, but with a drastically lower ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=34\:\mathrm{K}$ that is in accord with the presence of both the $\mathrm{TCNQMe}_{2}^{-\bullet}$ radical and diamagnetic dianionic bridging ligands. Notably, the desolvation/solvation process is mostly reversible, with respect to both structural and magnetic properties, demonstrating a switchable change in the magnetic ordering temperature of $\mathrm{\sim}70~\mathrm{K},$ achieved by guestinduced electronic state and slight structural modulations. Although the electrical conductivity for these compounds has not been reported, this study highlights that a guest-induced modulation of electronic states may be an attractive route toward multifunctional magnetic MOFs and other porous materials.
A similar modification of the electronic states for these compounds was achieved by tuning the electron-donating ability of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ paddlewheel units.474 Specifically, changing the substitution position of fluorine atoms on the aromatic ring of benzoate coligands from ortho to meta to para afforded a series of three compounds with a general formula of $\mathrm{(Ru}_{2}\mathrm{(1,}x\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{Fbz})_{4})_{2}\big(\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{O\hat{M}e})_{2}\big)$ ·solvent $\bar{(1,x-F b z^{-}=1,x}$ -difluorobenzoate; $x=2{\-}-4,$ ) that exhibit different charge-ordered states. While the framework bearing $1,2\mathrm{-Fbz}^{-}$ ligands displays the mixed-valence state $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}[\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}_{2}]^{4+}(1,2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{z})_{4})$ 0 $\mathrm{\bar{T}C N Q(O M e)}_{2}^{-\bullet})$ - $\left(\left[\mathrm{R}\mathbf{u}_{2}\right]^{5+}\left(1,2\mathrm{-Fbz}\right)_{4}\right)$ , the analogue with $1,3\mathrm{-Fbz}^{-}$ ligands features exclusively $\left[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\right]^{5+}$ paddlewheel units and diamagnetic $\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}{}^{2-}$ linkers. On the other hand, the derivative bdiesaprrinogp $1,4\mathrm{-Fbz}^{-}$ loirgdaenrdesd estparte nwtisth ae imntiexrtumre oaft $\left[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\right]^{4+},$ $\big[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\big]^{5+}$ , $\mathrm{TCNQ(OMe)}_{2}\mathrm{^{-\bullet}};$ , and $\mathrm{TCNQ(OMe)}_{2}\mathrm{}^{2-}$ species in a 1:3:1:1 ratio, similar to the desolvated $\bar{\langle\mathrm{Ru}_{2}(2,3,5\mathrm{-}}$ $\mathrm{Clbz})_{4}\rangle_{2}\big(\mathrm{TCNQMe}_{2}\big)$ framework discussed above. The mixed-valence framework exhibits long-range ferrimagnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=88\ \mathrm{K},$ followed by antiferromagnetic order at $T_{\mathrm{N}}=83\ \mathrm{K},$ ascribed to antiferromagnetic coupling between ferrimagnetic layers. In contrast, the charge-disproportionate framework orders as a ferrimagnet at a much lower temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=27\mathrm{K},$ owing to the alternating arrangement of strongly coupled ferrimagnetic and weakly coupled paramagnetic domains via $\mathrm{TCNQ(OMe)}_{2}^{-\bullet}$ and $\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{\dot{OMe}})_{2}{}^{2-}$ ligands, respectively. This change in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ with charge state is in accord with the study discussed above. Note that this electronic-state modulation can also be tuned by applied pressure.
Additional functional groups, such as 1,2,5-thiadiazole moieties, can be incorporated onto the TCNQ backbone to afford suitable bridging ligands for the synthesis of framework magnets. Two such 2D frameworks of fishnet-like topology feature localized mixed-valence $[\mathrm{Ru_{2}}]^{4+}{-}(\mathrm{BTDA{-}T C N\hat{Q})^{-}\Sigma}$ $\left[\mathrm{Ru}_{2}\right]^{5+}$ [BTDA-TCNQ $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ bis(1,2,5-thiadiazolo)tetracyano- $\cdot p$ - quinodimethane] states and exhibit ordering temperatures of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=83\mathrm{-}93\ \mathrm{K},$ in accord with other members of this class of frameworks discussed above.475 In addition, one 3D framework magnet featuring this ligand has been synthesized. Specifically, the compound $(\mathrm{R}\mathbf{u}_{2}\overline{{(1,3\mathrm{-Fbz})_{4}}})_{2}$ (BTDA-TCNQ)·1.6(4- ClPhMe)· $3.4\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ features similar $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ paddlewheel units as the 2D frameworks; however, twisting of the $\mu_{4}$ -(BTDATCNQ) bridging ligands leads to the formation of helical chains that connect into a 3D network (see Figure 57).476 Bond length analysis implies a charge-localized state with noni n t e g e r v a l u e s , $[\bf R_{4}]^{\delta(4+\delta)+}(1,3-F b z)_{4})$ ((BTDA$\mathrm{TCNQ)^{(1+\delta)-})\big(\big[R u_{2}\big]^{5+}\big(1,3\mathrm{-Fbz}\big)_{4}\big)}$ $(\delta~\approx~0.1-0.4)$ . This compound exhibits long-range magnetic order with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=107$ K, and coercive fields were detected up to $100~\mathrm{K},$ providing a value of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=7250$ Oe at $1.8~\mathrm{K}^{476}$ Indeed, this is the highest magnetic ordering temperature reported for a TCNQ-based magnet. Note, however, that the value of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ for this sole example of a 3D TCNQ-based magnet is only $6\mathrm{K}$ higher than that for the best 2D analogue.473 These compounds feature similar charge-localized electronic states, as such a doubleexchange mechanism is likely needed to provide much higher ordering temperatures for this family of magnets.
Finally, a homologous series of compounds with a formula of $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{TCNQ})_{2}$ $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}^{\mathbf{V}}}=\mathbf{\Lambda}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\right.$ , FeII, $\dot{\mathbf{C}}\mathbf{o}^{\mathrm{II}};$ , or $\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}^{\cdot}}$ ) has been reported to show spontaneous magnetization.89 These compounds exhibit ordering temperatures of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=7–44\mathrm{~K~}$ and behave as glassy magnets. However, owing to their poor structural characterization, these compounds will not be discussed further in this review.
## 4.5. Semiquinoid Radical-Bridged Compounds
As illustrated in the previous section, 2D framework magnets featuring diamagnetic tetraoxolene linkers exhibit high chemical tunability but modest magnetic ordering temperatures owing to the relatively weak superexchange coupling between metal centers through long diamagnetic linkers. As such, taking advantage of the facile redox chemistry of benzoquinoid ligands and building on the promise of the radical-bridged systems discussed above, recent efforts have focused on incorporating semiquinoid radical linkers into frameworks.
The compound $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)_{2}\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\left(\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{An}\right)_{3}\right]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot6\mathrm{DMF}$ $\left(\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{An}^{2-}\right.=$ chloranilate) represents the first example of a structurally characterized extended solid with tetraoxolene radical linkers.481 The structure of this compound features 2D honeycomb layers, with each Fe center ligated by three bis(bidentate) chloranilate ligands. The layers are eclipsed, leading to the formation of 1D hexagonal channels, and the charge of the dianionic network is compensated by $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})^{+}}\end{array}$ ions situated in the pores within the layers. Bond distance analysis, in conjunction with Raman and Mössbauer spectroscopy, revealed delocalized ligand mixed valency and the presence of exclusively high-spin $\breve{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions. As such, spontaneous electron transfer from the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ starting material to $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{An}^{2-}$ occurred during synthesis to provide a framework best formulated as $\mathrm{\big[Fe^{HI}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{Cl}_{2}^{'}\mathrm{An}^{8/3-}\big)_{3}\big]^{2-}$ . Variabletemperature magnetization studies showed a spontaneous magnetization to occur below $100\mathrm{~K~}$ (see Figure 58). The long-range order, which was posited to be of a 2D ferrimagnetic nature, was confirmed by ac susceptibility measurements to provide an ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}= $ $80\ \mathrm{~K~}\$ This compound behaves as a glassy magnet with a coercive field of $H_{\mathrm{c}}=2630$ Oe at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ Furthermore, the framework remains intact upon desolvation and exhibits a very large Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $88\breve{5}\left(105\right)\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . The desolvated compound also displays ferrimagnetic behavior, albeit with a lower ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=26\ \mathrm{K},$ attributed to structural distortions of the framework. Accordingly, this framework magnet adds to the handful of metal−organic compounds that display reversible solvent-induced switching of magnetic ordering temperature.
Furthermore, this compound exhibits high room-temperature electrical conductivity values of $\sigma=1.4\AA^{-7})\times10^{-2}$ and 1.0(3) $\times~10^{-3}~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{~cm^{-1}}$ for the solvated and desolvated forms, respectively.482 The coexistence of high-temperature magnetic order and high conductivity, as observed for these frameworks, is remarkable and nearly unprecedented in a metal−organic material (see Figure 59).
This compound can be reduced by one electron per formula unit with $\dot{\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{p}_{2}\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ $\left(\mathrm{Cp}^{-}\ =\ \right.$ cyclopentadienyl anion) via a postsynthetic single-crystal-to-single-crystal process to afford the compound $\mathrm{[\check{C}p_{2}C\dot{o}^{I I I}]_{1.43}(M e_{2}N H_{2})_{1.57}[\check{F}e^{I I I}{}_{2}(C l_{2}A n^{3-\bullet})_{3}]}$ · 4.9DMF, which features exclusively $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{An}^{3-\bullet}$ radical bridging ligands.482 The 2D honeycomb structure remains intact, and despite a net decrease in overall spin magnitude, the reduced compound exhibits long-range order with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=105\ \mathrm{K},$ which represents an increase of $25\mathrm{~K~}$ over that for the parent framework. This behavior was attributed to stronger intralayer coupling due to increased radical character and anionic charge of the chloranilate ligands. This framework was also tentatively described as a 2D ferrimagnet. Notably, this compound exhibits an open magnetic hysteresis loop up to $100~\mathrm{K}$ with coercive fields of $\bar{H_{\mathrm{c}}}~=~4520$ and 9 Oe at 1.8 and $100\ \mathrm{K},$ respectively. However, the room-temperature electrical conductivy for the reduced compound is $\sigma=5.1(3)\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is significantly less than that for the parent framework. The decrease in conductivity was hypothesized to arise from loss of ligand mixed valency, as facile charge hopping is prevented when the hole is filled by an electron. Nevertheless, these combined values of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ and $\sigma$ are exceptionally high for metal−organic materials (see Figure 59), highlighting the tremendous potential for metal−semiquinoid frameworks as conductive magnets.
There is also a great interest in developing materials with simultaneously switchable magnetic and electrical properties, as they may enable dramatic improvements in spintronic technologies, including data storage and processing.483 Toward this end, the paramagnetic 2D $\mathsf{\bar{M}n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ benzoquinoid framework compound $(\mathrm{\dot{M}e_{4}N)_{2}\mathrm{\overline{{{[}M n^{I I}_{2}(C l_{2}A n^{2-})_{3}]\cdot3.2\mathrm{\dot{E}t_{2}O}}}}}$ was reduced by three electrons via a postsynthetic single-crystal-to-singlecrystal chemical reduction process, in analogy to the $\breve{\mathrm{Fe}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ framework discussed above, to afford the compound $\mathrm{Na}_{3}\big(\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{N}\big)_{2}\big[\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}\big(\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{An}^{3-\bullet}\big)_{3}\big]$ ·3.9THF.484 This reduced compound is a permanent magnet below $T_{\mathrm{c}}=41\mathrm{~K~}$ $\left(H_{\mathrm{c}}\right.=$ 300 Oe at $1.8~\mathrm{~K~}$ and exhibits a room-temperature conductivity value of $\sigma=2.27(1)\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}.$ , which is 200000-fold higher than that for the parent compound (see Figure 60). Importantly, this redox chemistry is reversible (see
Figure 60). While both the magnetic ordering temperature and electrical conductivity are modest, this framework provides an important proof-of-principle example that highlights the ability of MOFs to serve as a facile platform to achieve simultaneous redox switching of permanent magnetic behavior and electrical conductivity.
In addition to these 2D metal−semiquinoid magnets, a structurally characterized 3D framework with a similar composition has demonstrated long-range magnetic order.485 The compound $\mathrm{(Bu_{4}N)_{2}[F e^{H I}\Sigma_{2}(d h\breve{b}q^{3-\bullet})_{2}(d h\breve{b}q^{2-})]}$ $\left({\mathrm{dhbq}}^{2-}\right.$ $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2{,}5$ -dioxido-1,4-benzoquinone) displays ligand mixed valency similar to that of the 2D $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ analogue discussed above, with formally two $\mathrm{dhbq}^{3-\bullet}$ radical ligands and one diamagnetic $\mathrm{dhbq}^{2-}$ ligand per formula unit. The type of mixed valency in this compound was established as Robin−Day class II/III mixed valency from the sharp absorption edge of the intervalence charge-transfer band in the diffuse reflectance ultraviolet−visible−near-infrared spectrum, representing the first observation of a class II/III mixed valency in a MOF. The structure of this framework consists of two interpenetrated (10,3)- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\boldsymbol{a}$ nets of opposing chiralities, where the $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{III}}}$ ions are in an analogous octahedral coordination environment as in the 2D honeycomb structures (see Figure 61, top).444,445 Variabletemperature dc magnetic susceptibility measurements, in conjunction with low-temperature magnetic hysteresis data, suggested competing ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic interactions that prevented the occurrence of 3D order until antiferromagnetic metal−radical coupling prevailed at low temperatures, giving rise to long-range ferrimagnetic order below 8 K. In contrast to the low magnetic ordering temperature, this compound exhibits the highest roomtemperature electrical conductivity value of $\sigma~=~1.6(1)~\times $ $10^{-1}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ reported for a 3D MOF (see Figure 61, bottom). The high conductivity was attributed to strong electronic correlation within this material owing to the mixed-valence state of the quinoid linkers.
Postsynthetic chemical reduction of this compound by 0.7 electron per formula unit afforded the compound $\mathrm{Na}_{0.9}\big(\mathrm{Bu}_{4}\mathrm{N}\big)_{1.8}\big[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}{}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dhbq}^{3-\bullet}\big)_{2.7}\big(\mathrm{dhbq}^{2-}\big)_{0.3}\big],$ which exhibits a 3D structure nearly identical to that of the parent material based on PXRD analysis. This reduced compound exhibits a slightly higher magnetic ordering temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}=12\mathrm{K}$ and a significantly lower room-temperature conductivity of $\sigma=$ $6.2(\mathrm{\breve{1}})~\times~10^{-3}~\mathrm{\S~\cm^{-1}}$ compared to those of the parent framework (see Figure 61, bottom). These correlated changes in magnetic ordering temperature and electrical conductivity are in accord with those observed for the 2D analogue, consistent with the increased number of strong metal−radical interactions and fewer holes after reduction.
## 4.6. A Pyrazine Radical-Bridged Compound
Recently, the redox activity of the pyrazine ligand was exploited to generate an air-stable layered solid that exhibits both long-range magnetic order and high electronic conductivity.486 Specifically, the charge-neutral compound $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\big(\mathrm{pyz}^{0.5-\bullet}\big)_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{{pyz}=}\right.$ pyrazine) was accessed through a reaction between $\mathrm{Cr^{II}C l}_{2}$ and pyrazine at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Its 2D structure is comprised of nearly square-grid layers of octahedral $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions bridged by pyrazine linkers, with the layers stacking in a staggered fashion. Crystallographic and spectroscopic analyses were consistent with a spontaneous one-electron reduction of the pyrazine moieties per formula unit occurring during synthesis, where the extra electron is delocalized over two ligands. Accordingly, this compound shows strong $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugation and antiferromagnetic exchange coupling between $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ and the pyrazine radical ligands, giving rise to ferrimagnetic order below ${\sim}55\ \mathrm{~K~}$ Furthermore, hysteretic behavior with a remanent magnetization was observed below the ordering temperature. The interlayer magnetic interactions are very weak, indicating a primarily 2D magnetic character for this compound. Moreover, $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Ci}_{2}(\mathrm{pyz}^{0.5-\ \circ})_{2}$ exhibits a high room-temperature electrical conductivity of $\sigma=3.2\times10^{-2}\mathrm{{S}}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , which operates via a 2D hopping-based transport mechanism. Notably, this conductivity value is on the same order of magnitude as that for the best 2D semiquinoidbridged framework magnet.
## 5. CONCLUSIONS AND OUTLOOK
The combination of the coordination chemistry toolbox and crystal engineering techniques has enabled the rational design of molecule-based framework magnets with exceptional properties. The foregoing discussion surveys the development of the field of metal−organic framework magnets, focusing on structurally characterized compounds featuring a wide range of diamagnetic and radical linkers. The work highlighted above illustrates that the chemical versatility of organic ligands and their ability to mediate magnetic exchange between paramagnetic metal centers have furnished frameworks of numerous structure types exhibiting permanent magnetism and other novel magnetic and functional behavior.
Structural characterization of these compounds has been critical to elucidate the mechanism of long-range magnetic behavior, in particular the interplay between intra- and intersubunit exchange interactions. Such studies have provided important design principles for generating new framework magnets with high ordering temperatures. Specifically, the employment of $\pi$ -conjugated organic linkers facilitates electronic and magnetic communication over distances much longer than those of their nonconjugated counterparts and thus enables the formation of open framework structures with permanent magnetic behavior. Furthermore, compounds featuring one primary pathway of magnetic exchange typically exhibit higher values of $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ than those showing more complicated structures and multiple exchange pathways, as different interactions commonly act in opposing directions and thus overall decrease the coupling strength between metal centers. Along these lines, the incorporation of organic radical linkers has been particularly successful, affording the MOF magnets that display the highest ordering temperatures ( $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ up to 171 K).72,73 Despite the success and rapid advancement of these materials, their performance is still modest compared to those of inorganic solid-state magnets, which show permanent magnetic behavior above room temperature.
A few examples of amorphous or polycrystalline roomtemperature metal−organic magnets have already been realized,13,82−85,88 suggesting that structural characterization of these types of compounds is within reach. Indeed, a layered mixed-valence phthalocyaninato $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}F e^{\mathrm{III}}}$ framework was recently reported to possess ferromagnetism and magnetic hysteresis up to $350~\dot{\mathrm{K}}^{487}$ This compound is polycrystalline, leading to superparamagnetic behavior with a large distribution of blocking temperatures. Accordingly, several strategies can be foreseen to design structurally well-defined MOF magnets that operate at high temperatures. One route that holds tremendous promise is the introduction of high-anisotropy metal ions, such as $4\mathrm{d}/5\mathrm{d}$ transition metal and lanthanide ions, into framework materials. Here, large spin−orbit coupling manifests in high magnetic anisotropy, which is an essential element in achieving hard permanent magnets. Because heavy transition metal centers typically adopt low-spin states, the combination of 3d and 4d/5d transition metal centers with high electronic spin and magnetic anisotropy, respectively, might be an attractive way to improve the performance of such magnets. Along these lines, combining first-row transition metals with $\overline{{{\bf M}{\bf o}}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ has enabled exceptionally strong magnetic coupling through cyanide in molecular complexes.488,489 Furthermore, multimetallic transition metal−lanthanide frameworks are also of interest, as most high-performing inorganic solid-state magnets, such as $\mathrm{Nd}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{14}\mathrm{B}$ and $s_{\mathrm{{mCo}_{5},\ell}}$ feature both types of metal centers.
Another attractive approach to increasing the ordering temperatures of framework magnets is to move from O- and N-based donors to S- and P-based ligands. The more radially diffuse orbitals of sulfur and phosphorus relative to oxygen and nitrogen should provide a route to stronger magnetic coupling between metal centers and organic linkers, as has been demonstrated in dinuclear semiquinoid-bridged $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complexes.490 Moreover, the use of sulfur-based ligands is particularly interesting owing to the high electrical conductivity of organosulfur compounds, such as tetrathiafulvalene and its derivatives,271 rendering such frameworks promising candidates as conductive magnets. Notably, postsynthetic metal and linker exchange reactions, which have been the subject of substantial recent efforts,38−45 may afford frameworks with irreversible metal−ligand coordination, as such materials often cannot be accessed through direct synthesis.
Finally, as illustrated with the examples in this review, emerging research has demonstrated that the magnetic and electronic properties of framework magnets can be modulated in a reversible manner through both chemical and physical processes, such as ligand functionalization, inclusion or removal of guest molecules, and redox reactions. This multifunctional behavior highlights the prospect for these compounds in sensing applications and magnetic switching devices. Indeed, the high electrical conductivity of several radical-bridged framework magnets underscores the potential for these materials for spintronics applications. Furthermore, MOF-based layered van der Waals magnets could make an enormous impact in this area. Such materials may retain longrange structural and magnetic order down to the monolayer, 404 and experimental and theoretical studies suggest that using anionic aromatic linkers such as perthiolated coronene,491 phthalocyaninato,487,492,493 or hexaiminotriphenylene494 ligands facilitates efficient $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugation that allows for the creation of new room-temperature semiconducting ferromagnets.
